INSERT INTO `content_pages` VALUES (1,25,1,'Under the patio','If you have a patio, you probably have a city under it.\n\nA mature black garden ant nest holds somewhere between four thousand and fifteen thousand ants. Some species run to half a million.','🐜'),(2,25,2,NULL,'They have been there longer than the patio. Ants have been building like this for roughly a hundred million years, which is about ninety-nine million years longer than anybody has been laying slabs.',NULL),(3,25,3,'Nobody is in charge','The first thing to get rid of is the queen.\n\nNot the ant — she stays. The idea. The queen does not rule anything. She does not give orders and nobody reports to her.',NULL),(4,25,4,NULL,'Her entire job is laying eggs, and she is extremely good at it: up to three hundred a day for as long as fifteen years in some species.\n\nShe is not a manager. She is a factory, and the colony feeds her.','👑'),(5,25,5,NULL,'So who decides where the tunnels go?\n\nNobody. That is the genuinely strange part, and it took scientists a long time to accept it.',NULL),(6,25,6,'How a decision gets made','An ant that finds food walks home leaving a chemical trail — a pheromone.\n\nOther ants are more likely to follow a strong trail than a weak one. Each one that follows it and finds food adds its own scent on the way back.',NULL),(7,25,7,NULL,'A good route gets reinforced. A bad route gets walked less, and the scent evaporates, and within an hour it is gone.\n\nNo ant compared the routes. The comparison happened in the walking.','〰️'),(8,25,8,NULL,'This is called stigmergy, and it is worth knowing the word, because people build computer systems on the same idea now.',NULL),(9,25,9,'The jobs','In a black garden ant nest you will find, roughly: nurses, who tend eggs; foragers, who go out; builders, who move soil; and a quantity of ants doing apparently nothing at all.',NULL),(10,25,10,NULL,'The ones doing nothing are not lazy. Studies that marked individual ants found that the inactive ones step in when the colony is disturbed.\n\nThey are a reserve. A city with no spare capacity cannot survive a bad day.','🛏️'),(11,25,11,NULL,'An ant can change job. A nurse can become a forager. Broadly, ants move to riskier work as they age — the opposite of us, and sensible, since an ant near the end of its life is a smaller loss than a young one.',NULL),(12,25,12,'The rubbish','Ant nests have middens: dedicated rubbish chambers, kept away from the nursery.\n\nDead ants go there. So do food scraps and soil from digging.',NULL),(13,25,13,NULL,'Some species keep their dead in a separate chamber from their rubbish, which is tidier than many households.\n\nWorkers that handle waste often stay away from the brood areas entirely. That is not manners. It is disease control.','🗑️'),(14,25,14,'Farmers and herders','Some ants farm. Leafcutter ants cut leaves they cannot digest, carry them underground, and feed them to a fungus. Then they eat the fungus.',NULL),(15,25,15,NULL,'They have been doing agriculture for about sixty million years. We have managed twelve thousand.',NULL),(16,25,16,NULL,'Others keep livestock. Aphids produce a sugary liquid called honeydew; ants protect aphids from predators and milk them for it, and will move them to better plants.','🍄'),(17,25,17,'What to do with this','Find an ant trail. Follow it with your eyes, not your feet, all the way to both ends. It will take longer than you expect.',NULL),(18,25,18,NULL,'Put a crumb a hand\'s width to one side of the trail and time how long it takes for the first ant to find it, and then for the tenth.\n\nThe gap between those two numbers is the pheromone trail being built. You are watching a decision happen.','🔎'),(19,25,19,NULL,'Do not destroy the nest to see inside it. You will learn almost nothing and end a city that was there before your house.',NULL),(20,25,20,'The last thing','There are more than fourteen thousand known ant species and probably thousands nobody has described.\n\nTheir combined weight is comparable to that of all wild birds and wild mammals put together.',NULL),(21,25,21,NULL,'They move more soil than earthworms in many places. They are among the most important things in your garden and the least looked at.','🌍'),(22,25,22,NULL,'The patio is the roof. You are a very large, very slow neighbour. Mind where you put your feet.',NULL),(23,25,23,NULL,'The end.','🐜'),(24,11,1,'The trophy','The Bramble Street Shield is forty-one years old, is made of plated tin, and is worth about nine pounds.','🏆'),(25,11,2,NULL,'It is awarded for the best front garden and it has been won by Number Eleven for six years running, which is the real subject of this book.',NULL),(26,11,3,NULL,'On the Tuesday after the judging it was gone from the windowsill of Number Eleven, where it had sat facing outwards for six years in a row.','🌻'),(27,11,4,'Four detectives','Sanjay, Noor, Bea and Idris were between nine and eleven and had been waiting for this their entire lives.',NULL),(28,11,5,NULL,'They had a notebook, a torch, a bag of equipment that was mostly string, and a method, which Sanjay had copied out of a book and which turned out to be the only useful thing they owned.',NULL),(29,11,6,NULL,'THE METHOD\n1. What is actually known?\n2. Who had the opportunity?\n3. What does each suspect gain?\n4. Check 1 again before doing anything about 2 and 3.','📓'),(30,11,7,'What was actually known','This took them a whole afternoon and it is the part most people skip.',NULL),(31,11,8,NULL,'The window had been open. It was open every day in July.\n\nThe shield had been on the inside sill, about forty centimetres in.',NULL),(32,11,9,NULL,'Mrs Vale had seen it at ten. It was gone at four. No door had been forced because no door was ever locked on Bramble Street in 1998 or now.','🪟'),(33,11,10,'Suspects','Number Three, who had come second for six years and had opinions about dahlias.',NULL),(34,11,11,NULL,'Number Nine, whose son had been told off about a football in June.\n\nThe postman, on no evidence whatsoever, because a stranger is always on a child\'s suspect list.',NULL),(35,11,12,NULL,'And the cat from Number Six, which was on the list because Bea insisted and which the other three agreed to only to stop her going on about it.','🚪'),(36,11,13,'Number Three','They interviewed Number Three, badly, in a way that made her cry.',NULL),(37,11,14,NULL,'She had come second for six years. She had also, it turned out, been the one who nominated Number Eleven in four of them, because she thought the garden was better than hers and said so.',NULL),(38,11,15,NULL,'Idris wrote in the notebook, in capitals: WE WERE WRONG AND WE WERE UNKIND, WHICH IS A SEPARATE MISTAKE.','😔'),(39,11,16,NULL,'They went back the next day with flowers from Noor\'s mother\'s garden, which Noor had not asked permission for and got into trouble about.',NULL),(40,11,17,'Back to step one','Which sent them to the fourth rule: check what is known before doing anything else.',NULL),(41,11,18,NULL,'They measured the sill. Forty-one centimetres deep. They measured the gap under the open window. Nine centimetres.','🔍'),(42,11,19,NULL,'Nine centimetres is too small for an arm with a shield in its hand, and too small for a child, and about right for a cat.',NULL),(43,11,20,'The cat','Bea, who had been right for the wrong reasons for four days, became insufferable for about a week and has never fully recovered from it.',NULL),(44,11,21,NULL,'The shield was under the hedge at Number Six, along with a trowel, two plant labels, a glove, and a small garden gnome belonging to Number Nine.','🐈'),(45,11,22,NULL,'Nine shiny objects in total. The cat from Number Six had been collecting shiny objects for some time and nobody had put it together because nobody had lost anything important enough to look.',NULL),(46,11,23,'After','The shield is screwed to the sill now. Number Eleven won it again, which surprised nobody.',NULL),(47,11,24,NULL,'The notebook has four pages at the front that are not about the case at all.','📌'),(48,11,25,NULL,'They are about Number Three, and what they said to her, and what they should have said instead, and they were written by Idris and signed by all four.',NULL),(49,11,26,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(50,6,1,'The pond has gone','On Tuesday there was a pond.\n\nOn Wednesday there was a sort of damp dent with four very surprised ducks standing in the middle of it.','🦆'),(51,6,2,NULL,'The smallest of them had a magnifying glass, because he had found one, and had been waiting eleven weeks for something to look at.',NULL),(52,6,3,NULL,'His name was Duckling. That was not a name, strictly, but it was what everybody called him and he had stopped arguing.','🔍'),(53,6,4,'Suspects','Duckling wrote down everybody who had been at the pond on Tuesday, because that is how it is done.',NULL),(54,6,5,NULL,'Four ducks. Eleven frogs. A heron, who was not popular. Two dogs. A man with a net. And a cow who had been leaning over the fence in a suspicious manner for some years.',NULL),(55,6,6,NULL,'He interviewed all of them. The frogs were no help. The heron was offended. The cow said \"hm\", which Duckling recorded faithfully.','📓'),(56,6,7,'The man with the net','Everybody said it was the man with the net, which is what everybody always says about the stranger.',NULL),(57,6,8,NULL,'Duckling went and found him. He was a scientist counting newts and he had a clipboard and a permit and he was extremely boring about both.',NULL),(58,6,9,NULL,'He also said something useful, which nobody else had: \"It was going down before I got here. Look at the ring.\"','🥽'),(59,6,10,'The ring','Round the edge of the damp dent there was a ring of dried mud, and above it another ring, and above that another.',NULL),(60,6,11,NULL,'Three rings. Like the marks in a bath that has been emptied slowly, three times, on three different days.','⭕'),(61,6,12,NULL,'A pond that is drunk by a cow goes down in a day. A pond that is stolen by a heron does not go down at all. A pond with three rings has been leaking for three days.',NULL),(62,6,13,'Where it went','Duckling walked all the way round the dent looking at the ground with the magnifying glass, which took most of an afternoon and made him extremely popular with the frogs.',NULL),(63,6,14,NULL,'At the low corner, by the willow, the mud was softer than everywhere else and there was a smell of iron.',NULL),(64,6,15,NULL,'And under the willow root, which had grown thick over four years, there was a pipe.','🕳️'),(65,6,16,'The pipe','An old field drain, laid in about 1950, running from the low corner of the pond out under the hedge and into the beck.',NULL),(66,6,17,NULL,'It had been blocked for decades by silt, which is why there had been a pond at all.',NULL),(67,6,18,NULL,'The willow root had grown through it and then, in the dry summer, shrunk back — and opened it.','🌳'),(68,6,19,'The culprit','Duckling announced the result at the hedge, to everybody, including the heron, who had demanded an apology and received one.',NULL),(69,6,20,NULL,'\"Nobody took the pond,\" he said. \"The pond left. Through a pipe. Because of a tree.\"',NULL),(70,6,21,NULL,'There was some disappointment. The frogs had been looking forward to a villain.','📣'),(71,6,22,'Putting it back','A pond can be put back, which Duckling had not known and which is the best part.',NULL),(72,6,23,NULL,'The man with the net came back with four other people and some clay, and they plugged the pipe, and in eleven days of rain the pond filled again.',NULL),(73,6,24,NULL,'It is slightly smaller than it was. The ducks have adjusted. The heron is still not popular.','💧'),(74,6,25,'After','Duckling has a second case now, which is why the beck runs brown after rain, and he has already found two rings and a pipe.',NULL),(75,6,26,NULL,'He has also written a rule on the front of his notebook.\n\nTHE STRANGER IS USUALLY NOT THE ANSWER. LOOK AT THE GROUND.',NULL),(76,6,27,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(77,26,1,'The jar','The marble jar in Class 4B was for good behaviour.\n\nYou got a marble for being kind, or for tidying without being asked, or — and this was Mr Enright\'s own rule — for asking a really excellent question.','🔍'),(78,26,2,NULL,'At four hundred marbles the class got a trip to the aquarium.\n\nOn Friday the jar had three hundred and eighty-two. Marigold knew, because she had counted them through the glass during assembly, which is not cheating.',NULL),(79,26,3,NULL,'On Monday it had three hundred and forty-one.\n\nForty-one marbles had gone over a weekend, out of a locked classroom, in a locked school.',NULL),(80,26,4,NULL,'Mr Enright said it was probably a miscount.\n\nMarigold said, politely, that it was definitely not a miscount, and Mr Enright said that was an excellent question, which it had not been, and gave her a marble for it.\n\nThree hundred and forty-two.','🫙'),(81,26,5,'The notebook','Marigold Okafor kept a notebook with a hard red cover.\n\nOn the first page, in capitals, it said: WRITE WHAT YOU SAW. NOT WHAT YOU THINK YOU SAW. There is a difference and it has caught her out before.',NULL),(82,26,6,NULL,'She wrote down the facts.\n\nFACT: 382 on Friday, 11:05, counted twice.\nFACT: 341 on Monday, 8:50, counted three times.\nFACT: Room locked Friday 15:30. Caretaker has the key. So does Mr Enright. So does the office.',NULL),(83,26,7,NULL,'Then, on a fresh page, she wrote: PEOPLE WHO COULD HAVE.\n\nAnd under it: Mr Enright. Mrs Vale (caretaker). The office. Anyone who climbed through the window, which does not open. Me.','📓'),(84,26,8,NULL,'She put herself on the list because the notebook rule says you must.\n\nIt is the hardest rule in detecting. Marigold has read nine detective books and not one of the detectives ever puts themselves on the list, which she thinks is sloppy.',NULL),(85,26,9,'Tuesday: the caretaker','Mrs Vale was in the corridor with a mop and no interest whatsoever in being interviewed.\n\n\"Marbles,\" she said. \"You are asking me about marbles.\"\n\n\"Forty-one of them.\"',NULL),(86,26,10,NULL,'\"Child, I have a flood in the boys\' toilets and a radiator making a noise like a cow.\"\n\n\"Did you go in 4B over the weekend?\"\n\n\"I went in every room over the weekend. It is called cleaning.\"',NULL),(87,26,11,NULL,'Marigold wrote: MRS VALE — IN THE ROOM. ADMITS IT FREELY.\n\nAnd underneath, because the notebook rule cuts both ways: PEOPLE WHO ARE HIDING SOMETHING DO NOT USUALLY ADMIT IT FREELY.','🪣'),(88,26,12,NULL,'\"One more question,\" said Marigold. \"Was there anything on the floor? By the jar?\"\n\nMrs Vale stopped mopping.\n\n\"Funny you should say that.\"',NULL),(89,26,13,'What was on the floor','\"Glass,\" said Mrs Vale. \"Not a lot. A sort of grit of it, by the leg of the bookcase. I hoovered it.\"\n\n\"Glass,\" said Marigold.\n\n\"Grit of it. Like sugar.\"',NULL),(90,26,14,NULL,'Marigold went straight to 4B and lay down on the floor by the bookcase with her cheek on the carpet, which is how you see things on carpets.\n\nShe found two more bits. Tiny. Clear. Sharp enough to notice.','✨'),(91,26,15,NULL,'And then, four centimetres under the bookcase, she found a whole marble.\n\nIt had a blue swirl. It was chipped on one side, and the chip was fresh and white and rough under her thumb.',NULL),(92,26,16,'Wednesday: the wrong answer','Here is what Marigold thought on Wednesday morning, and she has written it down, because she is not going to pretend otherwise.\n\nShe thought: Ben Shah did it.',NULL),(93,26,17,NULL,'Ben Shah sat nearest the jar. Ben Shah had not got a marble all term. Ben Shah had said, out loud, in front of everyone, that the aquarium was boring and he did not care if they went.',NULL),(94,26,18,NULL,'Marigold did not write that in the FACTS. She wrote it in a new section she had to invent on the spot, and she called it THINGS I WANT TO BE TRUE.\n\nIt is the most useful page in the whole notebook now.','😟'),(95,26,19,NULL,'Because the facts did not fit Ben.\n\nFACT: the glass was *under* the bookcase, not near the door.\nFACT: a chipped marble means a marble that hit something hard.\nFACT: nobody carrying marbles out of a room drops them under furniture.',NULL),(96,26,20,'Thursday: the bookcase','On Thursday, Marigold asked Mr Enright to help her move the bookcase.\n\nHe said it was an excellent question. She said it was not a question at all, and he moved it anyway.',NULL),(97,26,21,NULL,'Behind the bookcase, against the skirting board, there was a gap where the floor had dropped away from the wall. It was about the width of two fingers.\n\nIn the gap were thirty-eight marbles.','🧱'),(98,26,22,NULL,'Three more were in the dust under the radiator. With the one from under the bookcase, that made forty-two.\n\nForty-two. One more than had gone missing.',NULL),(99,26,23,'The forty-second marble','\"That one is from last year,\" said Mr Enright, turning it over. \"We lost a few last summer too. I assumed somebody had pocketed them.\"\n\nHe said it in an ordinary voice. Marigold wrote it down in capitals.',NULL),(100,26,24,NULL,'Because that was the case, actually. Not the marbles. That.\n\nSomebody had been assumed about, last summer, by a teacher who was kind and meant no harm and was simply wrong.','🏫'),(101,26,25,NULL,'The jar sits on the shelf above the bookcase. The shelf is not level — Mrs Vale measured it with a spirit level and said several things about the people who put it up.\n\nWhen the door slams, the jar jumps. When the jar jumps, marbles go over the back.',NULL),(102,26,26,NULL,'The door slams whenever the fire door at the end of the corridor is propped open, which it was, all weekend, because of the flood in the boys\' toilets.',NULL),(103,26,27,'The report','Marigold\'s report is pinned up by the jar. Mr Enright typed it out but he did not change a word, which she had made a condition.\n\nIt is four lines long.','📋'),(104,26,28,NULL,'THE MARBLES WERE NOT TAKEN. THE SHELF IS NOT LEVEL.\n\nNOBODY DID IT.\n\nI THOUGHT BEN SHAH DID IT. HE DID NOT. I AM SORRY. I HAVE WRITTEN IT DOWN SO I DO NOT DO IT AGAIN.\n\nTHE SHELF IS STILL NOT LEVEL.',NULL),(105,26,29,NULL,'The shelf is level now. Mrs Vale saw to it on the Monday, with a drill, at considerable volume, during literacy.\n\nThe class got to four hundred in March. The aquarium was not boring. Ben Shah has a photograph of a cuttlefish on his wall.',NULL),(106,26,30,NULL,'And Marigold Okafor has a new notebook, with a hard blue cover, and a page near the front she looks at more than any of the others.\n\nIt still says: THINGS I WANT TO BE TRUE.',NULL),(107,26,31,NULL,'The end.','🔵'),(108,27,1,'The dry August','In the driest August for forty years, a shape appeared in the Harlows\' top field.','🌾'),(109,27,2,NULL,'A dark ring, about thirty metres across, with a dark line running away from it to the south-east, visible only because the grass above it had stayed green when everything around it went brown.',NULL),(110,27,3,NULL,'This is a crop mark, and it is one of the most useful things in archaeology.','🟢'),(111,27,4,'Why grass tells you','A buried ditch holds more soil and more water than the ground around it. Grass above a ditch stays green longer in a drought.',NULL),(112,27,5,NULL,'A buried wall does the opposite. Thin soil over stone dries first, and the grass above it browns early.',NULL),(113,27,6,NULL,'So a dry summer draws a plan of what is underneath, in green and brown, for a few weeks, and then the rain comes and it is gone.','🧱'),(114,27,7,'What Priya did','Priya Harlow was ten and it was her field, in the sense that it was her family\'s and she walked across it twice a day.',NULL),(115,27,8,NULL,'She did not dig. This is the single most important thing in the book and she is extremely firm about it when she talks to school groups.',NULL),(116,27,9,NULL,'Digging destroys. An archaeological site can be excavated exactly once, and whatever is not recorded during it is gone for ever.','🚫'),(117,27,10,NULL,'A ten-year-old with a spade would have taken a two-thousand-year-old thing and made it into a hole.',NULL),(118,27,11,'What she did instead','She photographed it. From the ground, badly, and then from the top of the hay barn, much better, over three weeks as it got clearer and then faded.',NULL),(119,27,12,NULL,'She measured it with a tape and a long string and two bamboo canes, and drew it on squared paper.','📷'),(120,27,13,NULL,'And she wrote down the date of every photograph, which turned out to matter more than the photographs.',NULL),(121,27,14,'Who to tell','She rang the county archaeologist, which is a real job and exists in every county, and which nobody in her family had known about.',NULL),(122,27,15,NULL,'A woman called Ada Rennick came out nine days later and spent two hours walking the field with Priya, mostly asking her questions.','📞'),(123,27,16,'Geophysics','The next step is not a spade either. It is a machine on a frame pushed up and down the field in one-metre lines.',NULL),(124,27,17,NULL,'Magnetometry detects tiny differences in the magnetic field of the soil. A ditch that has been filled with topsoil reads differently from undisturbed subsoil. So does anything that has been burnt.',NULL),(125,27,18,NULL,'It took a team of four two days and produced a grey image far clearer than the crop mark: a circular ditch with an entrance to the south-east, and inside it, four round blobs.','📡'),(126,27,19,NULL,'The blobs are postholes. Four of them, in a ring. That is a roundhouse.',NULL),(127,27,20,'What it is','An Iron Age enclosed farmstead. Probably between 2,500 and 2,000 years old. One house, a ditch and bank around it, and a track leading out.',NULL),(128,27,21,NULL,'There are thousands of these in Britain and most are unrecorded. This one is now on the county Historic Environment Record, which means it cannot be ploughed deeper or built on without somebody checking first.','🏘️'),(129,27,22,'Still not dug','Two years on, nobody has excavated it, and probably nobody will.',NULL),(130,27,23,NULL,'Priya found this maddening and then stopped finding it maddening when Ada Rennick explained the reasoning.',NULL),(131,27,24,NULL,'A site that is recorded and protected and left alone is available to archaeologists in fifty years, with methods nobody has invented yet.','⏳'),(132,27,25,NULL,'A site that is dug in 2026 is available to the people who dug it, and to whatever they thought to write down.',NULL),(133,27,26,'After','The field is still grazed. The crop mark has shown twice more, both times fainter.',NULL),(134,27,27,NULL,'Priya\'s drawing — bamboo canes, string, squared paper — is in the county record alongside the magnetometry, because it is dated across three weeks and the survey is one afternoon.',NULL),(135,27,28,NULL,'The end.','🌾'),(136,28,1,'The arrangement','Ned was allowed to stay up until nine o\'clock on a Friday if he was counting owls.\n\nThis was his grandmother\'s rule and she had made it up on the spot, which is where the best rules come from.','🦉'),(137,28,2,NULL,'They sat on the back step with a blanket and did not talk, which is the only way to count owls.',NULL),(138,28,3,NULL,'Week one: one owl.\nWeek two: one owl.\nWeek three: one owl.','🌙'),(139,28,4,'One owl, eleven times','By week eleven Ned had counted one owl, eleven times, and he was starting to feel like the arrangement was not going well.',NULL),(140,28,5,NULL,'\"There is only one owl,\" he said. \"The whole wood has one owl.\"\n\n\"Does it?\" said his grandmother.',NULL),(141,28,6,NULL,'\"I have counted eleven times.\"\n\n\"You have counted eleven times,\" she agreed. \"You have counted what you can hear.\"','🌲'),(142,28,7,'The difference','An owl that calls is an owl that wants to be heard.\n\nA tawny owl calls to say this wood is mine, and it mostly says it in autumn, when the young ones are looking for somewhere of their own.',NULL),(143,28,8,NULL,'An owl that is hunting does not call at all. It would be silly. The mouse would leave.',NULL),(144,28,9,NULL,'\"So how many owls are not calling?\" said Ned.\n\n\"That,\" said his grandmother, \"is a much better question and I do not know the answer.\"','🐭'),(145,28,10,'Counting differently','So Ned stopped counting calls and started counting other things.',NULL),(146,28,11,NULL,'Pellets, under the big beech. An owl swallows a mouse whole and coughs up the bones and fur in a neat grey lump.',NULL),(147,28,12,NULL,'He found four pellets in one week under one tree. He pulled one apart with two sticks on a plate and found, in it, the skull of a vole.','🦴'),(148,28,13,NULL,'Four pellets in a week under one tree is one owl using that tree. Pellets under three different trees, a long way apart, is harder to explain with one owl.',NULL),(149,28,14,'Feathers','Then feathers, which is better still.',NULL),(150,28,15,NULL,'Owl feathers have a soft fringe along the front edge. That fringe breaks up the air so the wing makes no noise, which is why an owl can arrive without being heard.','🪶'),(151,28,16,NULL,'Ned found eleven feathers in a month. Two of them were much smaller than the others, and one was pale underneath where the rest were dark.',NULL),(152,28,17,'The answer','By the spring Ned had a list that said, carefully, with reasons: PROBABLY FOUR.',NULL),(153,28,18,NULL,'Not definitely. Four, probably: two adults who call, and two young ones who do not call yet and who left those small feathers.','📓'),(154,28,19,NULL,'His grandmother read the list twice and said it was the best thing anybody in the family had written in some years.',NULL),(155,28,20,'After','Ned is nine. The arrangement still stands, although it is half past nine now, which he negotiated.',NULL),(156,28,21,NULL,'He has a box with forty-one feathers in it, labelled, and a notebook with pellet counts going back three years.','🔦'),(157,28,22,NULL,'He has never once seen an owl.\n\nThat is normal. That is almost the point. The wood is full of things that are not going to show themselves to you, and you can still find out how many there are.',NULL),(158,28,23,NULL,'The end.','🦉'),(159,29,1,'What a sump is','A sump is the place where a cave passage goes completely underwater.\n\nThe roof comes down, the water comes up, and they meet. Beyond it the cave may continue. It may not. You cannot know from this side.','🕳️'),(160,29,2,NULL,'The cave under Harrow Fell had been mapped to four hundred and six metres, ending at a sump, since 1971.',NULL),(161,29,3,'Why nobody had been through','Because going through a sump is the most dangerous thing in caving, and because for fifty years nobody had a good enough reason.',NULL),(162,29,4,NULL,'Wren Alabi was sixteen and on the survey team, which does not dive. The survey team measures, draws and argues.','💧'),(163,29,5,NULL,'The reason arrived in a dry September, when the water in the sump dropped and left an air gap of about nine centimetres.',NULL),(164,29,6,'Nine centimetres','Nine centimetres of air at the top of a flooded passage is not a way through. It is an invitation to drown with your face in the air.',NULL),(165,29,7,NULL,'But it meant the sump was short. A long sump does not drop nine centimetres in a dry month. A short one does.','📏'),(166,29,8,NULL,'The team dye-tested it: a harmless tracer in the water above the sump, and watchers at every spring on the fell.',NULL),(167,29,9,NULL,'The dye came out at Cold Beck Rising, two kilometres away and ninety metres lower, nineteen hours later.','🧪'),(168,29,10,'What that told them','Two kilometres of passage. Ninety metres of descent. Nineteen hours of travel time, which is slow, which means it is not one clean pipe.',NULL),(169,29,11,NULL,'Somewhere between the sump and the spring there was a lot of cave, and slow water, and probably air.',NULL),(170,29,12,'The dive','Two divers went through in October. Wren was not one of them and spent the entire four hours at the sump edge on the line, which is a real job and the worst one.',NULL),(171,29,13,NULL,'A dive line is laid as the diver goes, tied off every few metres. It is how they come back. In zero visibility it is the only thing that is.','🪢'),(172,29,14,NULL,'The sump was eleven metres long. Eleven metres took the first diver fourteen minutes, because eleven metres of flooded passage in silt is not eleven metres of swimming.',NULL),(173,29,15,'Past it','They surfaced in a chamber forty metres long with a stream running through it.',NULL),(174,29,16,NULL,'No one had ever been in it. There is no word for that feeling and the divers have both tried.','🔦'),(175,29,17,NULL,'They were in there fifty minutes. They surveyed it, which is the discipline: you do not explore further until you have recorded where you are.',NULL),(176,29,18,'Wren\'s job','The survey came back as numbers on a wet notebook: bearings, distances, slopes, written by a cold person in the dark.',NULL),(177,29,19,NULL,'Wren turned them into the map, which took eleven evenings, and which is where she found the error.','📐'),(178,29,20,NULL,'Two of the bearings could not both be right. The chamber, as drawn, overlapped itself by nine metres.',NULL),(179,29,21,'Saying so','Telling two adults who have just done something extraordinary that their numbers do not add up is not a comfortable evening.',NULL),(180,29,22,NULL,'Wren did it anyway, with the two readings circled, and did not suggest which one was wrong, because she did not know.','🗣️'),(181,29,23,NULL,'It was the second. The compass had been held eleven centimetres from a steel karabiner, which is enough to pull a bearing four degrees.',NULL),(182,29,24,'After','The chamber is called Alabi Hall, which Wren finds excruciating and has asked three times to have changed.',NULL),(183,29,25,NULL,'The passage beyond it has been pushed another three hundred metres and stops at another sump, which is how caves work.','🗺️'),(184,29,26,NULL,'On the current survey, under the names of the two divers, there is a third line: SURVEY AND DRAWING — W. ALABI. ERROR IDENTIFIED AND CORRECTED, STATION 14.',NULL),(185,29,27,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(186,30,1,'The hole','At the end of Pia\'s garden there is a hedge, and in the hedge there is a hole.\n\nIt is exactly fox-shaped, because a fox made it, by going through it twice a night for three years.','🦊'),(187,30,2,NULL,'The fox is called Mustard. Pia named her. Mustard does not know this and would not care.',NULL),(188,30,3,NULL,'Mustard comes through at about nine and goes back at about five.\n\nIn between she does fox things in the gardens of eleven houses, and nobody minds except Mr Harlow, who minds about everything.','🌿'),(189,30,4,'Something changes','In the spring, Mustard started getting stuck.\n\nNot badly. A wriggle, a pause, a shove. Then through.',NULL),(190,30,5,NULL,'By May it was a long wriggle. By June, Pia watched her try three times and give up and go round the long way, past the road.',NULL),(191,30,6,NULL,'The long way goes past the road. That is the whole problem in one sentence.','🛣️'),(192,30,7,'Why she was stuck','Pia thought Mustard had got fat, and felt rather pleased with herself for working it out.\n\nHer grandmother, who knew about animals, said: \"Look at the hedge, not the fox.\"',NULL),(193,30,8,NULL,'The hedge had grown. Three years of hawthorn had thickened the hole from the sides until it was two thirds the size it had been.',NULL),(194,30,9,NULL,'Mustard had not changed. The door had.\n\nPia measured it with a stick and a piece of string, because she had no tape measure and was not going to wait for one.','📏'),(195,30,10,'The argument','Her father said to leave it.\n\n\"Wild animals sort themselves out,\" he said, which is true about three quarters of the time and is why people say it.',NULL),(196,30,11,NULL,'\"She goes past the road now.\"\n\n\"Foxes cross roads.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Pia. \"That is what I am worried about.\"',NULL),(197,30,12,NULL,'Her grandmother settled it by going out with the shears herself at eight o\'clock in the evening, in her slippers, which nobody was expecting.','✂️'),(198,30,13,'Cutting a hole','You cannot just hack at it. Pia learned this in about four minutes.',NULL),(199,30,14,NULL,'Cut too much and the hedge dies back and the hole becomes a gap, and a gap is not a door, it is an invitation, and then you get badgers.',NULL),(200,30,15,NULL,'They took out the new growth only — the pale green stuff from this year — and left the old dark wood that holds the shape.\n\nIt took forty minutes and the hole came back to exactly fox-sized.','🌳'),(201,30,16,'Waiting','Pia sat at her window for four nights.\n\nMustard did not come. Pia was certain she had ruined it, that the smell of shears and people had closed the whole garden for good.',NULL),(202,30,17,NULL,'On the fifth night, at ten past nine, the hedge moved.','🌙'),(203,30,18,NULL,'Mustard came through without a wriggle, without a pause, the way she had done it for three years, and stopped in the middle of the lawn and looked directly at the window.',NULL),(204,30,19,NULL,'Pia did not move. That is the correct thing to do and it is extremely hard.',NULL),(205,30,20,'Every June','The hedge grows every year. That is what hedges do. It is not going to stop because a fox is using it.',NULL),(206,30,21,NULL,'So every June, Pia and her grandmother go out with the shears and take off that year\'s growth, and the hole stays fox-sized.\n\nIt is forty minutes a year. It is the cheapest useful thing Pia has ever found.','📅'),(207,30,22,'After','Mustard is old now, for a fox. Five is old for a fox.',NULL),(208,30,23,NULL,'There is a smaller fox using the hole as well these days, who is almost certainly hers and who goes through without touching the sides.',NULL),(209,30,24,NULL,'Pia has marked the date in the back of her diary, in June, in every year ahead that the diary goes.\n\nIt says: HEDGE. SHEARS. ONLY THE GREEN BITS.','🐾'),(210,30,25,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(211,9,1,'Version one','The first time Grandpa told the story, Mina was four, and it went like this.\n\n\"I used to work on a boat.\"','🏴‍☠️'),(212,9,2,NULL,'That was the whole story. Mina remembers being satisfied with it.',NULL),(213,9,3,'Version four','By the time she was six it had become: \"I used to work on a boat off the coast of Spain, and once we were out for eleven days.\"',NULL),(214,9,4,NULL,'This is a better story and it is still, Mina is fairly sure, mostly true.','⛵'),(215,9,5,'Version nine','At seven: eleven days became twenty-one, the coast of Spain became the Atlantic, and a storm had appeared.',NULL),(216,9,6,NULL,'At seven and a half the storm had got a name.',NULL),(217,9,7,NULL,'At eight there was a shark, which Mina enjoyed enormously and did not believe for a second.','🌊'),(218,9,8,'Version nineteen','Version nineteen was told at Christmas when Mina was nine, to four cousins who had never heard any of the others.',NULL),(219,9,9,NULL,'In version nineteen, Grandpa had been a pirate.',NULL),(220,9,10,NULL,'He had a parrot. He had buried something. And at the very end, in a voice that dropped, there was a kraken.','🦜'),(221,9,11,'The cousins','The four cousins believed every word, because they were three, five, five and six, and because he is extremely good at it.',NULL),(222,9,12,NULL,'Mina was nine and did not, and spent the whole of Boxing Day in a mood about it, which surprised her more than anybody.',NULL),(223,9,13,NULL,'She was not cross that it was made up. She was cross that she could no longer find the true bit inside it.','😠'),(224,9,14,'What she did','She wrote them all down. Every version she could remember, in order, with how old she had been.',NULL),(225,9,15,NULL,'Then she took the list to Grandpa and asked him to cross out everything that was not true.','📓'),(226,9,16,NULL,'He read it for a long time. He went quite quiet, which he does not do.',NULL),(227,9,17,'What he crossed out','The kraken. The parrot. The buried thing. The shark. The name of the storm.',NULL),(228,9,18,NULL,'He did not cross out the storm.',NULL),(229,9,19,NULL,'He did not cross out twenty-one days, and he did not cross out the Atlantic, and he did not cross out the boat.','✏️'),(230,9,20,'What was left','A man of nineteen, on a fishing boat in the Atlantic, out for twenty-one days, in a storm that lasted two of them, who was frightened the entire time and was sick on the first day and the last.',NULL),(231,9,21,NULL,'\"That is not as good a story,\" said Mina.\n\n\"No,\" said Grandpa. \"That is why I put the kraken in.\"','🧓'),(232,9,22,'After','He still tells version nineteen to the cousins. Mina has stopped minding. She sometimes does the kraken voice herself if he is tired.',NULL),(233,9,23,NULL,'But there is a page in her notebook that says TRUE at the top, and under it the short version, with no kraken in it at all.',NULL),(234,9,24,NULL,'It is four lines long. She thinks it is the better story and she has not told him that, because he would put something in it.','📖'),(235,9,25,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(236,12,1,'You cannot grow one','Let us deal with the title first. You cannot grow a thunderstorm. A thunderstorm contains more energy than a small town uses in a day.','⛈️'),(237,12,2,NULL,'What you can do is build every single part of one, separately, on a kitchen table, and understand exactly how they fit together. Which is better.',NULL),(238,12,3,'Part one: warm air goes up','This is the engine. Everything else is detail.',NULL),(239,12,4,NULL,'Warm air is less dense than cold air, so it rises, and as it rises it expands, and as it expands it cools.','🔥'),(240,12,5,NULL,'EXPERIMENT. Hold a strip of tissue paper above a radiator. It lifts. Hold it above the floor. It does not. You have measured a convection current with two pence worth of tissue.',NULL),(241,12,6,'Part two: cool air cannot hold water','Warm air can hold more water vapour than cold air. When rising air cools enough, the vapour has to go somewhere, and it condenses onto specks of dust.',NULL),(242,12,7,NULL,'Every single cloud droplet has a speck of something in the middle of it. A completely clean sky cannot make a cloud.','☁️'),(243,12,8,NULL,'EXPERIMENT. Breathe on a cold window. That is condensation. Breathe on a warm one. That is nothing. You have just demonstrated the dew point.',NULL),(244,12,9,'Part three: the cloud makes its own heat','Here is the part that surprises people, including adults.',NULL),(245,12,10,NULL,'When water vapour condenses it releases heat — the same heat it absorbed when it evaporated. This is called latent heat.','💧'),(246,12,11,NULL,'So a growing cloud warms the air inside itself, and warmer air rises faster, which makes more cloud, which releases more heat.',NULL),(247,12,12,NULL,'That is a feedback loop, and it is why a thundercloud can go from nothing to eleven kilometres tall in under an hour.','📈'),(248,12,13,'Part four: the anvil','It cannot rise for ever. At the top of the troposphere the temperature stops falling with height, and a rising parcel of air is suddenly no warmer than its surroundings.',NULL),(249,12,14,NULL,'So it stops, and spreads sideways, and makes the flat anvil top you can see from fifty miles away.',NULL),(250,12,15,NULL,'If you can see an anvil, the storm has hit the ceiling. That is a mature thunderstorm and it is the stage at which it does everything interesting.','🔨'),(251,12,16,'Part five: the charge','Nobody fully agrees on the details, which is worth saying out loud in a science book.',NULL),(252,12,17,NULL,'The leading explanation involves collisions between ice crystals and soft hail in the middle of the cloud, which leaves the small light crystals positively charged and the heavier hail negative.',NULL),(253,12,18,NULL,'Updraughts carry the light positive crystals to the top. The heavy negative hail sinks. Now you have a cloud with a positive top and a negative base, which is a battery several kilometres tall.','⚡'),(254,12,19,'Part six: the flash','Air is an insulator, but only up to a point — about three million volts per metre.',NULL),(255,12,20,NULL,'Beyond that it breaks down and conducts, and the charge equalises in a few thousandths of a second.',NULL),(256,12,21,NULL,'The channel reaches around thirty thousand degrees, about five times the surface of the sun. The air in it expands explosively.','🌩️'),(257,12,22,NULL,'That explosion is thunder. Thunder is not a separate thing from lightning. It is the sound of the air getting out of the way.',NULL),(258,12,23,'The one calculation to know','Light arrives effectively instantly. Sound travels about a third of a kilometre per second.',NULL),(259,12,24,NULL,'Count the seconds between flash and bang and divide by three. That is the distance in kilometres.','⏱️'),(260,12,25,NULL,'Under thirty seconds between flash and bang means the storm is close enough to be dangerous. That is the actual advice from meteorological services and it is the only thing in this book you need to remember.',NULL),(261,12,26,'Where to be','Inside a building with wiring and plumbing, or inside a car. Not under a tree. Not on open high ground. Not in water.',NULL),(262,12,27,NULL,'A car is safe not because of its tyres but because the metal shell carries the current around the outside. That is called a Faraday cage and rubber has nothing to do with it.','🏠'),(263,12,28,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(264,31,1,'Twenty-nine letters','Class 5 wrote to a weather station in the far north as part of a project.\n\nTwenty-nine letters, in one envelope, eleven hundred miles.','✉️'),(265,31,2,NULL,'Most of them asked the same four questions. Is it cold. Have you seen a polar bear. Is it dark all day. Do you get bored.',NULL),(266,31,3,NULL,'Dayo Bankole asked something else, because he had looked the station up first and had six pages of notes.','❄️'),(267,31,4,'What Dayo asked','He asked what the readings were taken with, how often, and whether they were done by hand or automatically.',NULL),(268,31,5,NULL,'He asked what happened to a thermometer at minus forty.\n\nAnd he asked how you know the instrument is right when there is nobody within two hundred miles to check it against.',NULL),(269,31,6,NULL,'He was ten. He had not meant to ask a hard question. He had simply been genuinely stuck on it since Tuesday.','🌡️'),(270,31,7,'One reply','Nine weeks later one envelope came back, addressed to the school, containing one letter addressed to Dayo.',NULL),(271,31,8,NULL,'The other twenty-eight got a photocopied sheet about the station with a polar bear on it. Mrs Vale distributed them and nobody minded very much except two people who minded a great deal.',NULL),(272,31,9,NULL,'The letter was from a technician called Juno Haugen and it was four pages long.','📬'),(273,31,10,'What she wrote','She answered the instrument question first and in detail, because it was the real one.',NULL),(274,31,11,NULL,'Two thermometers, not one. Compared every day. If they disagree by more than a tenth of a degree you have a problem and you write it down before you decide which is wrong.',NULL),(275,31,12,NULL,'She explained that a reading you cannot check is not a measurement, it is an opinion, and that the whole job is checking.','📋'),(276,31,13,NULL,'Then she answered the other three, which Dayo had not asked.\n\nYes it is cold. No polar bears at that station. Dark from November to February. And on the last one she wrote: \"Yes. Often. Thank you for asking it properly.\"',NULL),(277,31,14,'Two years','They wrote to each other for two years.',NULL),(278,31,15,NULL,'About six letters a year, because the post is slow and because she was working shifts and because that is a perfectly good rate.','📮'),(279,31,16,NULL,'She sent him a page of raw readings once — handwritten, in pencil, with a crossing out and a note in the margin saying CHECK THIS.',NULL),(280,31,17,NULL,'It is the single most prized object Dayo owns and it is a page of numbers about air temperature.','📄'),(281,31,18,'The part that matters','In the eleventh letter Juno Haugen wrote something Dayo did not understand for a year.',NULL),(282,31,19,NULL,'\"You should know that you are the only one who wrote to me instead of about me.\"',NULL),(283,31,20,NULL,'Twenty-eight letters had asked what it was like to be her. One had asked about her work.','💭'),(284,31,21,'After','Dayo is fifteen. The letters stopped when the station went automatic and Juno was posted somewhere with email, which she says ruined everything.',NULL),(285,31,22,NULL,'They still write. Twice a year. On paper. She insists.','🖥️'),(286,31,23,NULL,'He has the first letter framed, all four pages, and the page of readings beside it.',NULL),(287,31,24,NULL,'And he has a rule, which he worked out himself and uses whenever he meets anybody who does anything.\n\nAsk about the work, not the weather.',NULL),(288,31,25,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(289,32,1,'The light that stayed on','Tess lived at the bottom of a lighthouse, which is a fine place to live if you do not mind stairs.\n\nThere were one hundred and six of them. She knew, because she counted them every single morning on her way up with the tea.','🌊'),(290,32,2,NULL,'Her grandfather kept the light at Gull Point. He had kept it for forty years, and in all that time he had never once let it go out.\n\n\"A lamp is a promise,\" he liked to say. \"You keep it, or you do not.\"',NULL),(291,32,3,NULL,'Every night the great lamp turned and flashed, turned and flashed, and the ships far out on the black water knew exactly where the rocks were.\n\nAnd every morning, when the sun came up, the lamp went quietly dark and slept all day.','🗼'),(292,32,4,NULL,'Except that it had not gone dark for eleven days.',NULL),(293,32,5,NULL,'Tess had counted those too.\n\nAt first she thought her grandfather had forgotten to turn it off. But he had not forgotten anything in forty years, not even her birthday, and he did not forget now.',NULL),(294,32,6,NULL,'\"It will not go off,\" he said, on the fourth morning, scratching his beard. \"I have turned the key. I have pulled the lever. I have asked it nicely.\"\n\n\"Did it answer?\" said Tess.\n\nHer grandfather looked at her for a long moment. \"No,\" he said. \"Not to me.\"',NULL),(295,32,7,'What the lamp said','So on the twelfth night, Tess climbed the one hundred and six stairs and did not go back down again.\n\nShe sat with her back against the warm brass and she waited, and when the sky began to turn grey at the edges she said, very quietly, \"You can sleep now. It is morning.\"','💡'),(296,32,8,NULL,'The lamp turned and flashed, turned and flashed.\n\nThen it said, in a voice like a kettle thinking about boiling: \"No.\"',NULL),(297,32,9,NULL,'Tess was not as surprised as you might expect. She had always suspected the lamp was listening.\n\n\"Why not?\" she said.\n\n\"Because,\" said the lamp, \"last month a ship went onto the rocks at Cold Nose, three miles up the coast. In the daylight. In the bright, safe daylight.\"',NULL),(298,32,10,NULL,'\"Everyone got off,\" said Tess. \"Grandad rowed out himself.\"\n\n\"Everyone got off,\" the lamp agreed. \"This time.\"\n\nAnd then it said the thing it had been carrying around for eleven days. \"What if it happens while I am asleep?\"',NULL),(299,32,11,NULL,'Tess thought about that.\n\nIt is a hard thing, being the only one who can do a job. Tess knew a little about it. She was the only one small enough to get behind the water tank when something was dropped back there, and she did not always want to be.','🌫️'),(300,32,12,NULL,'\"You cannot stay awake for ever,\" she said.\n\n\"I can try,\" said the lamp.\n\n\"Grandad says a promise you cannot keep is not a promise. It is just a worry wearing a coat.\"',NULL),(301,32,13,NULL,'The lamp went around twice without saying anything.\n\nThen it said: \"Your grandfather talks like a man who has read the backs of a great many biscuit tins.\"\n\n\"He does,\" said Tess. \"But he is right.\"',NULL),(302,32,14,'The watch is shared','Here is what Tess did, and she did it without telling anybody, because she was six and she did not think anybody would listen.\n\nShe went to see the gulls.','🕊️'),(303,32,15,NULL,'There were four hundred gulls at Gull Point, which is how it got the name, and they were loud and rude and extremely well informed.\n\nThey saw everything. They simply had never been asked about any of it.',NULL),(304,32,16,NULL,'\"We see the boats,\" said the biggest gull, who had one white foot. \"We see them come, we see them go, we see the silly ones go too close to Cold Nose.\"\n\n\"Would you tell someone,\" said Tess, \"if a silly one went too close?\"\n\nThe gull put its head on one side. \"Nobody ever asked.\"',NULL),(305,32,17,NULL,'So that was the gulls.\n\nThen Tess went down to the harbour, where the fishing boats came in at six, and she asked Ada Rennick, who mended nets and missed nothing, whether she would mind glancing north now and then.\n\n\"North,\" said Ada. \"At Cold Nose.\"\n\n\"If you would not mind.\"',NULL),(306,32,18,NULL,'\"Child,\" said Ada Rennick, \"I have looked north every morning of my life. I just did not know it counted.\"',NULL),(307,32,19,NULL,'And then Tess went to the chandler, and to the harbourmaster, and to the woman who drove the bread van along the clifftop road at half past seven.\n\nNone of them said no. Not one. That was the part Tess had not expected at all.','🔔'),(308,32,20,'Eleven days and one','On the thirteenth morning Tess climbed the stairs with the tea and did not spill any, which had never once happened before.\n\n\"There are four hundred gulls,\" she told the lamp. \"And Ada Rennick. And the bread van. They are all watching Cold Nose. All of them. All day.\"',NULL),(309,32,21,NULL,'The lamp turned and flashed, turned and flashed.\n\n\"Four hundred,\" it said.\n\n\"And Ada Rennick,\" said Tess, because Ada would have wanted that said properly.',NULL),(310,32,22,NULL,'The sun came up over the water the way it does, all at once and then slowly.\n\nAnd the great lamp at Gull Point dimmed, and dimmed, and went out — and the whole tower was quiet for the first time in twelve days.','🌅'),(311,32,23,NULL,'Tess stayed a while in case it changed its mind.\n\nFrom somewhere deep in the warm brass came a sound like a kettle that has decided not to boil after all.',NULL),(312,32,24,NULL,'Her grandfather found her there at noon, asleep against the lamp with the empty cup beside her.\n\nHe did not wake her. He sat down on the top stair, which was his favourite of all one hundred and six, and he watched the sea for both of them.',NULL),(313,32,25,'After','The lamp sleeps every day now.\n\nIt still asks, some mornings, whether anybody is watching. Somebody always is. That is rather the point of a harbour.','⚓'),(314,32,26,NULL,'And if you ever go to Gull Point, you will see a small brass plate screwed to the bottom of the tower, at exactly the height a six-year-old can reach.\n\nIt says: THE WATCH IS SHARED.\n\nNobody has ever polished it. It does not need it.',NULL),(315,32,27,NULL,'The end.','🌊'),(316,33,1,'The hour nobody uses','There is an hour between the last bus and the first milk float that nobody has any use for.\n\nMabel Quist had found a use for it. She was awake in it, which is more than most people manage.','🏮'),(317,33,2,NULL,'It had started with the noise. A kind of cheerful clatter, the way a market sounds from three streets away, except that it was two in the morning and the nearest market was on Thursdays and twelve miles off.\n\nMabel got her coat. She did not get her shoes, which she regretted later.',NULL),(318,33,3,NULL,'The noise came from Caddis Lane, which during the day was a dull little street with a shut-down launderette and a bin that everybody argued about.\n\nAt night it was two hundred stalls long and lit by lanterns hung on nothing at all.',NULL),(319,33,4,NULL,'Mabel stood at the top of the lane for a long time.\n\nShe was eight, and she had been told a great many sensible things about strangers, and she intended to follow every one of them. But nobody had ever said anything about markets.','🕯️'),(320,33,5,'What they sold','The first stall sold weather. Not pictures of it, not forecasts of it — jars of the actual thing, labelled in careful handwriting.\n\nONE GOOD MORNING IN MARCH. A FOG THAT LIFTS BY TEN. THAT SMELL BEFORE RAIN (STRONG).',NULL),(321,33,6,NULL,'\"Browsing?\" said the stallholder, who was mostly hat.\n\n\"How much is the rain smell?\"\n\n\"It is not for sale to people with no shoes on,\" said the hat. \"Bad for the chest. Come back in boots.\"',NULL),(322,33,7,NULL,'The second stall sold second thoughts, which looked like marbles and rattled when you shook the box.\n\nThe third sold the names of streets that had been knocked down. Mabel bought nothing, because she had no money, and nobody seemed in the least surprised.',NULL),(323,33,8,NULL,'At the far end, past the man weighing out echoes, there was an empty stall.\n\nIt had a counter, a lantern, a stool, and a card tied to the post with string. The card said: YOURS, IF YOU LIKE.','🧺'),(324,33,9,NULL,'\"Whose is it?\" Mabel asked the woman at the next stall along, who was polishing something invisible.\n\n\"Nobody\'s. It goes to whoever reads the card.\" The woman looked up. \"You read the card.\"\n\n\"I read the card,\" Mabel agreed.',NULL),(325,33,10,'The trouble with having nothing','Here is the difficulty. A stall wants stock, and Mabel had none.\n\nShe had a coat, a dressing gown cord she had used as a belt, and a pocket, and in the pocket were four things: a conker, a bus ticket, a key to nothing, and a very old boiled sweet.',NULL),(326,33,11,NULL,'She laid them on the counter in a row, because that is what the other stalls did, and she sat on the stool and waited to be laughed at.\n\nNobody laughed. A market is much politer than a playground.',NULL),(327,33,12,NULL,'A tall customer picked up the conker. \"What does it do?\"\n\n\"It does not do anything,\" said Mabel. \"It is a conker.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said the customer slowly, \"but what does it *do*?\"','🌰'),(328,33,13,NULL,'Mabel thought about the conker. She had found it on the walk home from her grandmother\'s funeral, and she had put it in her pocket because her hands had wanted a job, and she had not taken it out since.\n\n\"It stops your hands being empty,\" she said. \"On a bad day.\"',NULL),(329,33,14,NULL,'The market went quiet. Not everywhere. Just near.\n\n\"Ah,\" said the customer. \"Yes. That. How much?\"',NULL),(330,33,15,'Business','By four o\'clock Mabel had sold the conker, the bus ticket (to a woman who had never been anywhere and wanted proof that going was possible) and the key to nothing (to a man who said he had a door to match).\n\nThe boiled sweet, nobody wanted. Mabel ate it herself.',NULL),(331,33,16,NULL,'She was paid in the market\'s own money, which was warm and slightly damp and looked rather like beech leaves.\n\n\"What do I buy with these?\"\n\n\"Anything,\" said the woman next door. \"They stop working at dawn, mind. Spend them.\"','💰'),(332,33,17,NULL,'Mabel walked the whole length of Caddis Lane with her leaves held tight.\n\nShe passed the weather, and the second thoughts, and the stall that sold the sound of a house you grew up in, which was busy, and which she made herself walk straight past.',NULL),(333,33,18,NULL,'At the top of the lane she stopped at a small stall she had missed on the way in.\n\nIt sold hours. Ordinary ones. \"Spare,\" said the label. \"Unused. Various.\"',NULL),(334,33,19,NULL,'\"How many leaves for an hour?\" said Mabel.\n\n\"Depends on the hour. Tuesday afternoons are cheap. Nobody wants a Tuesday afternoon.\"\n\n\"I want one,\" said Mabel. \"I want two, actually. I want the hour before school and the hour after tea.\"','⏳'),(335,33,20,NULL,'\"That is an odd shopping list for a child,\" said the stallholder.\n\n\"My mother works,\" said Mabel. \"She is not in those hours. She would like to be. She says so about forty times a week.\"',NULL),(336,33,21,NULL,'The stallholder counted the leaves very slowly, the way grown-ups do when they are deciding something that has nothing to do with the counting.\n\n\"It is enough,\" they said at last, which Mabel suspected was not quite true.',NULL),(337,33,22,'The first milk float','The milk float came along the main road at twenty past five, and the lanterns went out one by one, politely, from the far end.\n\nBy the time it passed Caddis Lane there was nothing there but a launderette, a bin, and a girl with no shoes on.','🥛'),(338,33,23,NULL,'Her mother came down at half past six and found Mabel asleep at the kitchen table with her coat still on.\n\n\"Where on earth have you been?\"\n\n\"Shopping,\" said Mabel, into the table.',NULL),(339,33,24,NULL,'Her mother sat down slowly. She had an hour before she had to leave. She had never had an hour before she had to leave, not once, not in years, and she could not for the life of her work out where it had come from.',NULL),(340,33,25,NULL,'\"I do not have to go yet,\" she said, in the voice of someone reading a word they do not recognise.\n\n\"No,\" said Mabel. \"You do not.\"',NULL),(341,33,26,'After','Mabel has gone back eleven times. The stall is always there, and always empty, and the card is always tied to the post.\n\nShe brings better stock now. You would be astonished what a pocket holds, once you start asking what the things in it *do*.','🏮'),(342,33,27,NULL,'And if you are ever awake in the hour nobody uses, and you hear a cheerful clatter from three streets off — put your shoes on first.\n\nThe hat was quite right about that. It is bad for the chest.',NULL),(343,33,28,NULL,'The end.','🌙'),(344,34,1,'Eleven things','On the Wednesday before half term, Mo Adeyemi was given eleven pieces of homework.','📚'),(345,34,2,NULL,'Spellings. Times tables. A book review. A poster about Vikings. Six sums. A thing about a leaf. And five more that he could not remember by the time he got home.',NULL),(346,34,3,NULL,'He put the bag down in the hall and did not open it for two days, which is the normal and correct response to eleven pieces of homework.','🎒'),(347,34,4,'Friday night','On Friday night he opened the bag and everything fell out and he cried a bit, which he does not include when he tells this story at school.',NULL),(348,34,5,NULL,'His mother did not help with the homework. She helped with something else, which turned out to be the useful thing.',NULL),(349,34,6,NULL,'She gave him a roll of wallpaper and a marker pen and said: \"Put all of it on the wall. All eleven. Do not do any of it yet.\"','📋'),(350,34,7,'The wall','It took half an hour. Eleven boxes, one per task, each with what it was and how long he thought it would take.',NULL),(351,34,8,NULL,'SPELLINGS — 10 min\nTIMES TABLES — 10 min\nBOOK REVIEW — 1 hour??\nVIKING POSTER — ALL OF MY LIFE\nSIX SUMS — 20 min\nLEAF — ???',NULL),(352,34,9,NULL,'Then he added them up, because his mother made him.\n\nThree hours and ten minutes, plus the poster, plus the leaf.','🧱'),(353,34,10,'Three hours','Three hours and ten minutes, across four days, is forty-eight minutes a day.',NULL),(354,34,11,NULL,'Mo sat on the floor and looked at the wall for some time.\n\n\"That is not very much,\" he said, in a voice that suggested he had been betrayed.',NULL),(355,34,12,NULL,'\"No,\" said his mother. \"It never was.\"','😤'),(356,34,13,'Why it felt enormous','This is the part worth knowing and the reason the book exists.',NULL),(357,34,14,NULL,'Eleven things in a bag is eleven things at once. Your head holds all of them simultaneously, and all of them feel unfinished, which they are.',NULL),(358,34,15,NULL,'Eleven things on a wall is eleven separate small things, nine of which are twenty minutes or less.','🧠'),(359,34,16,NULL,'Nothing about the homework changed. It was the same eleven pieces. The bag was lying to him about how big it was.',NULL),(360,34,17,'The poster','The Viking poster was the one that said ALL OF MY LIFE, and it was the one Mo did first, which was his own decision and a good one.',NULL),(361,34,18,NULL,'It took fifty minutes. Not all of his life. Fifty minutes, including looking up what a longship was called and arguing with his sister about whether Vikings had horns on their helmets, which they did not.','🛡️'),(362,34,19,'The leaf','The leaf took four minutes. He had been dreading it for five days because he did not know what it was, and not knowing is heavier than knowing.',NULL),(363,34,20,NULL,'It was: find a leaf, draw it, name the tree. There was a sycamore at the end of the road.',NULL),(364,34,21,'After','The wallpaper is still up. It gets a new set of boxes every Wednesday.',NULL),(365,34,22,NULL,'Three people in Mo\'s class do it now, because he explained it at show and tell and it went better than his Viking poster did.','✏️'),(366,34,23,NULL,'At the top of the roll, in marker pen, it says: THE BAG IS LYING ABOUT HOW BIG IT IS. PUT IT ON THE WALL.',NULL),(367,34,24,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(368,4,1,'Four pounds','The boots cost four pounds from the shop that smells of other people\'s coats.','👢'),(369,4,2,NULL,'They were brown, slightly too big, and had somebody else\'s initials inside: A.R., in biro, worn almost away.',NULL),(370,4,3,NULL,'Jonah\'s mother said they would do until Christmas. They did considerably more than that.','🏷️'),(371,4,4,'The first step','He noticed it on the way home, in the dark, on the hill by the chapel.',NULL),(372,4,5,NULL,'One step was slightly longer than it should have been. Not much. The length of a kerb.',NULL),(373,4,6,NULL,'He stopped and did it again on purpose and went about a metre and a half, which is not a thing a seven-year-old does from standing.','🌙'),(374,4,7,'Going up','By the gate he had worked out the rule, which is the sort of thing you work out fast when it is happening to your own feet.',NULL),(375,4,8,NULL,'The boots only did it in the dark. And only if you were going slightly uphill. And only if you were not thinking about it very hard.',NULL),(376,4,9,NULL,'That last one took him three nights to notice and is the reason the boots had ended up in a secondhand shop.','⬆️'),(377,4,10,'Across','On the fourth night, going up the hill by the chapel, not thinking about it, Jonah took a step and did not come down.',NULL),(378,4,11,NULL,'He went nine metres. Slowly. About the height of a house.',NULL),(379,4,12,NULL,'He came down on the chapel roof, which is flat, and which is lucky, and which he spent some time sitting on.','✨'),(380,4,13,'From up there','From the chapel roof you can see the whole of Little Hollow and both roads and, on a clear night, the sea.',NULL),(381,4,14,NULL,'Jonah had lived there his entire life and had never once seen it all at the same time.',NULL),(382,4,15,NULL,'He stayed until he was cold, which was about forty minutes, and then he had the obvious problem, which was getting down.','🌌'),(383,4,16,'Down is harder','The boots did uphill. The boots did not do downhill, which is an unhelpful design and explains the initials.',NULL),(384,4,17,NULL,'He got down the fire ladder on the chapel wall, in the dark, in boots that were slightly too big, which was the actual adventure.',NULL),(385,4,18,'A.R.','Jonah found her. It took him eleven weeks and a notice in the shop window.',NULL),(386,4,19,NULL,'Ada Rennick was eighty-one and had bought the boots in 1958 and had walked across the sky in them until she was fourteen.','✉️'),(387,4,20,NULL,'\"Why did you stop?\"\n\n\"I got heavy,\" she said. \"And then I got sensible, which was worse.\"',NULL),(388,4,21,'What she told him','Two things, over a very long cup of tea.',NULL),(389,4,22,NULL,'One: never go up without working out your way down first. She had a broken wrist at eleven years old that she described as entirely her own fault.',NULL),(390,4,23,NULL,'Two: do not tell anybody who will take them off you, and do tell one person who will notice if you do not come home.','🫖'),(391,4,24,'After','Jonah told his mother, who did not believe him, and then did, and then went outside with him at nine o\'clock at night to watch.',NULL),(392,4,25,NULL,'She is the one person. She stands at the gate. She has a torch and a flask and a strong opinion about the chapel roof.',NULL),(393,4,26,NULL,'Inside the boots, under A.R., there is now a second set of initials in biro.','👢'),(394,4,27,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(395,35,1,'The call','The pager went at 15:42 on the eleventh of November, which is nineteen minutes before it gets dark on Harrow Fell.\n\nOne walker. Overdue since one. Phone off.','⛰️'),(396,35,2,NULL,'Tam Quist was seventeen and had been a trainee on the Thwaite team for three weeks. This was call number one.',NULL),(397,35,3,'What Tam expected','Tam expected running.\n\nTam had imagined this moment a great deal and in every version of it there was running.',NULL),(398,35,4,NULL,'What actually happened was forty minutes in a cold hut with a map on a table and eleven people arguing about probability.','🗺️'),(399,35,5,'Where people actually go','The team leader was Ada Rennick, who had done this for twenty-two years.\n\nShe did not ask where the walker might be. She asked what the walker had been trying to do.',NULL),(400,35,6,NULL,'Car at Cold Beck. Boots gone, poles gone, dog still in the car.\n\n\"Dog in the car,\" said Ada. \"So: short walk intended. Not a round. Out and back.\"',NULL),(401,35,7,NULL,'Out and back from Cold Beck means the fell gate, the wall, and the top. Three miles. Two hours in summer.','🚗'),(402,35,8,NULL,'\"So why is she six hours over?\"',NULL),(403,35,9,'The three answers','Ada wrote them on the board. There are only ever three, she said, and people waste hours inventing a fourth.',NULL),(404,35,10,NULL,'One: she is hurt and cannot move.\nTwo: she is lost and moving, which is worse.\nThree: she is fine and rude and already home.','📋'),(405,35,11,NULL,'They phoned the house. No answer. Three was still live and had to stay live, because teams have walked all night for people who were in the pub.',NULL),(406,35,12,'Searching the likely, not the possible','Tam suggested the gully on the north side, because it was dramatic and dangerous and in Tam\'s head that was where lost people went.',NULL),(407,35,13,NULL,'Ada did not dismiss it. She did something more useful: she asked how many people in twenty-two years had been found in that gully.\n\n\"Two,\" she said. \"And both of them were climbers who meant to be there.\"',NULL),(408,35,14,NULL,'Most lost walkers are found within a quarter mile of the path they intended, downhill of it, on the side they would have drifted to in bad light.','📈'),(409,35,15,NULL,'It is not exciting. It is where they are.',NULL),(410,35,16,'The drift','Harrow Fell drops away east into bracken. The light that afternoon had come from the south-west and gone at four.',NULL),(411,35,17,NULL,'\"She came off the top late,\" said Ada. \"Cloud down. She aimed for the wall and drifted left, because everyone drifts left when the ground falls away on the left. She is in the bracken east of the wall.\"','🌫️'),(412,35,18,NULL,'Six people went to the bracken east of the wall. Two went up the path, two to the gully — because likely is not certain — and three stayed in the hut, which Tam thought was a waste of three people.',NULL),(413,35,19,'The three in the hut','They were on the radio, the phone to the police, and the map, moving pins.\n\nIt is the most important job on the hill and it happens in a building.',NULL),(414,35,20,NULL,'Tam learned this at 18:20, when the bracken team found a pole and Ada needed somebody who had been watching the whole board to say what that meant and where to look next.','📻'),(415,35,21,'Found','They found her at 19:04, two hundred metres east of the wall, in bracken, with a broken ankle and a bivvy bag she had put on herself.',NULL),(416,35,22,NULL,'She had been there five hours. She had stayed put. She had got into the bag.',NULL),(417,35,23,NULL,'Ada Rennick told her so, out loud, twice, while they splinted her: that she had done the two things that mattered and that they had found her because of them.','🔦'),(418,35,24,'The stretcher','A stretcher carry off Harrow Fell takes fourteen people and two and a half hours.',NULL),(419,35,25,NULL,'You rotate. Six on, six resting, two scouting the ground ahead for the next fifty metres. Nobody carries for more than ten minutes at a time.',NULL),(420,35,26,NULL,'Tam carried for about forty minutes in total across two and a half hours and could not lift a mug afterwards.','🥾'),(421,35,27,'What Tam wrote down','In the debrief, Ada asked every member what they had learned, including the ones with twenty years in.',NULL),(422,35,28,NULL,'Tam said: \"I thought it would be running.\"\n\n\"Everybody does,\" said Ada. \"What is it instead?\"',NULL),(423,35,29,NULL,'\"Forty minutes of thinking so that the walking is in the right place.\"','📓'),(424,35,30,NULL,'That is in the Thwaite team handbook now, under TRAINEE NOTES, with Tam\'s initials next to it.',NULL),(425,35,31,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(426,36,1,'The shed','The bicycle had been in the shed since 1998, under a tarpaulin, behind a lawnmower that did not work either.','🚲'),(427,36,2,NULL,'It was black. It had one gear, rod brakes, and a leather saddle with a split in it.\n\nIt weighed, Rafi discovered when he tried to lift it, roughly the same as Rafi.',NULL),(428,36,3,NULL,'His grandfather had bought it in 1954 and ridden it until 1998, which is forty-four years.','🔧'),(429,36,4,'Four thousand miles','That number is not a guess. It is written on the inside of the shed door, in pencil, in columns.',NULL),(430,36,5,NULL,'Every long ride, dated, with the miles. The last entry is 1998 and says: TO THE COAST AND BACK. 61. KNEE.',NULL),(431,36,6,NULL,'Four thousand one hundred and ninety miles, across forty-four years, on one gear.','✏️'),(432,36,7,'Why it stopped','Not the bicycle. The knee.\n\nThe bicycle was fine in 1998 and had been fine in the shed ever since, in the way that a well-made thing is fine: rusty, stiff, and entirely intact.',NULL),(433,36,8,'What Rafi found wrong','Over three weekends, with a wire brush, oil, and a book from the library:',NULL),(434,36,9,NULL,'Both tyres perished. Rims fine. Spokes all present, four loose.\n\nChain solid with rust, in one piece. Rod brakes seized at the pivots. Bearings dry but not pitted.','🛠️'),(435,36,10,NULL,'The frame had no cracks anywhere. It had been painted with something in about 1970 that had done a better job than the original paint.',NULL),(436,36,11,'Rod brakes','Nobody makes rod brakes any more. They work by pushing metal rods, not pulling cables, and they are heavy and slow and almost indestructible.',NULL),(437,36,12,NULL,'Rafi could not get parts. He could not get a diagram. What he could get was his grandfather, who was eighty-nine, in a chair, with a mug, explaining it from memory while Rafi lay on the floor of the shed.','🔩'),(438,36,13,NULL,'It took four afternoons. His grandfather got two things wrong and corrected himself both times, forty minutes later, out of nowhere.',NULL),(439,36,14,'The tyres','The size is 28 by 1½ inches, which is a Victorian measurement, and which you can still buy, because half the bicycles in the world are still this shape.',NULL),(440,36,15,NULL,'They cost eleven pounds each and came from a shop in the Netherlands and took nine days.','📦'),(441,36,16,'The first ride','Rafi rode it to the end of the road and back, which is four hundred metres, and it nearly killed him.',NULL),(442,36,17,NULL,'One gear means one gear. On the flat it is fine. On the hill out of Thwaite it is a negotiation.',NULL),(443,36,18,NULL,'His grandfather, watching from the gate, said the thing Rafi has thought about most.\n\n\"You are trying to go up it fast. You cannot. Nobody ever could. You go up it slowly and you arrive.\"','⛰️'),(444,36,19,'The column','There is a new column on the inside of the shed door now, under the old one, in biro, because Rafi could not find a pencil the first time and then decided he liked the difference.',NULL),(445,36,20,NULL,'His first entry: TO THE END OF THE ROAD AND BACK. 0.4. DIED.','📏'),(446,36,21,NULL,'His eleventh: TO THE COAST AND BACK. 61.',NULL),(447,36,22,'Sixty-one','He did it in August, in seven hours, with four stops, and his grandfather was at the gate when he got back because he had been told roughly what time to expect him and had come out forty minutes early.',NULL),(448,36,23,NULL,'That ride is the same ride as the last entry in the old column. Same road. Same bicycle. Forty-four years apart.','🌅'),(449,36,24,'After','The total at the bottom of Rafi\'s column is three hundred and ninety-one miles.',NULL),(450,36,25,NULL,'He has thirty-eight hundred to go to match the old one. He has worked out that at his current rate it will take him about nine years, which he says is fine.',NULL),(451,36,26,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(452,37,1,'The count','The official survey of Kepler-62f lists eight moons.\n\nIt has listed eight moons for nineteen years. It is a good survey, made by careful people with better instruments than Nell Avery will ever have.','🌙'),(453,37,2,NULL,'Nell has counted nine for three months.\n\nShe is eleven. Her instrument is a forty-year-old reflector on the roof of a research station where her mother repairs atmosphere plant, and it has a crack in the secondary mirror shaped like a comma.',NULL),(454,37,3,NULL,'This is the whole problem, and she knows it before anybody says it to her.\n\nA crack in a mirror makes ghosts. A ghost is a smear of light that is not there. Nine moons, with a cracked mirror, is eight moons and a ghost.',NULL),(455,37,4,NULL,'So Nell did what you do.\n\nShe wrote down every observation, with the date, the time, the seeing conditions, and the position of the thing relative to two fixed stars. Ninety-one nights. Not one missed, including the night of the dust storm, when she recorded: NO SEEING. NOTHING TO REPORT.','🔭'),(456,37,5,'Why a ghost will not do','Here is the argument Nell built, and she built it alone, over eleven weeks, in a notebook with a plastic cover.\n\nA ghost from a cracked mirror sits in the same place on the mirror. It does not matter where you point the telescope — the ghost goes with the glass.',NULL),(457,37,6,NULL,'So a ghost would appear at the same offset from the centre of the field every night.\n\nHer ninth moon does not. It moves. It moves *against* the eight, and it comes back.',NULL),(458,37,7,NULL,'Better than that: it comes back on a schedule.\n\nNell measured the gaps. Eleven days and about nine hours, over and over, with an error she could not shrink below three hours because she was using a wristwatch and her own eyes.','📐'),(459,37,8,NULL,'A ghost does not keep an orbital period.\n\nShe wrote that sentence in capital letters, which she does not normally do, and she looked at it for a long time, and then she went and found an adult.',NULL),(460,37,9,'The first adult','Station Science Officer Bram Teague has a doctorate and a wall of certificates and four minutes a day in which he is not busy.\n\nNell got two of them.',NULL),(461,37,10,NULL,'\"Nine,\" he said. \"On the Harkaway reflector.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Which has a cracked secondary.\"\n\n\"Yes. I know. That is why I measured the offset—\"\n\n\"Nell.\" He was not unkind. That is somehow worse. \"The survey has eight. The survey used Pallas Array.\"',NULL),(462,37,11,NULL,'\"The survey is nineteen years old,\" said Nell.\n\n\"Moons do not arrive.\"\n\n\"No,\" she agreed, \"but surveys end. How long was the observation window?\"\n\nHe did not know. He said he would look it up, and he meant it, and he did not, because he had four minutes a day.','📄'),(463,37,12,'The second adult','Her mother repairs atmosphere plant. She has no doctorate and no certificates and she taught Nell to count in base ten and then, for fun, in base twelve.\n\nShe read all ninety-one pages. It took her a whole evening and she did not skim.',NULL),(464,37,13,NULL,'\"Your error bars are honest,\" she said at the end. \"That is the first thing. Most people make them small to look clever.\"\n\n\"They are three hours. It is embarrassing.\"\n\n\"It is three hours and you said so. That is the opposite of embarrassing.\"',NULL),(465,37,14,NULL,'Then she said the useful thing, which is what engineers are for.\n\n\"You cannot fix the mirror and you cannot fix the watch. So stop trying to measure better. Get someone else to measure at all.\"','🔧'),(466,37,15,'The prediction','You cannot argue with a survey. You can, however, make a prediction, and a prediction is a thing a survey cannot ignore.\n\nNell took her eleven days and nine hours and she ran it forward.',NULL),(467,37,16,NULL,'On the nineteenth of Tenth, at approximately 02:40 station time, give or take three hours, the ninth object would pass in front of the fourth moon.\n\nNot near it. Across it. A transit, visible to anything pointed in roughly the right direction.',NULL),(468,37,17,NULL,'She wrote it out. Date, time, error, the reasoning, the ninety-one observations as an appendix, and one line at the top in plain language: IF THIS DOES NOT HAPPEN, I AM WRONG AND THE MIRROR IS THE ANSWER.\n\nShe sent it to eleven observatories. Nine never replied.','✉️'),(469,37,18,'The two who answered','The first was a school on Tarrin Station whose telescope was worse than hers. They said they would look anyway, which Nell thought was the correct attitude.\n\nThe second was Pallas Array.',NULL),(470,37,19,NULL,'The reply was four lines from a woman named Juno Okonkwo, who had been nineteen years old when she worked the original survey and was now the person who ran it.\n\n\"Our window was eight months,\" she wrote. \"An eleven-day object with a high inclination could sit outside it. Nobody has rechecked. We will look on the nineteenth.\"',NULL),(471,37,20,NULL,'And then a fifth line, which Nell has not shown anybody, and which is folded into the back of the notebook with the plastic cover.\n\n\"Thank you for telling me what would prove you wrong. Almost nobody does that. — J.O.\"','🛰️'),(472,37,21,'The nineteenth of Tenth','The transit happened at 02:51.\n\nEleven minutes inside her error bars, which Nell has pointed out rather a lot since.',NULL),(473,37,22,NULL,'Pallas Array saw it. The school on Tarrin Station saw it too, badly, through cloud, and their picture is terrible and it is the one Nell has on her wall, because they looked when nobody made them.',NULL),(474,37,23,NULL,'Her mother was on the roof with her for the whole four hours, holding the watch, because two people make a better clock than one.\n\nNeither of them said anything for a while after it crossed. There was not much to say that the notebook had not already said.','⭐'),(475,37,24,'The paper','The paper came out eight months later. Nell is the second author, which is correct, because Pallas Array did the measurement that settled it and she will not have it any other way.','📑'),(476,37,25,NULL,'The ninth moon is called Harkaway, after the telescope with the crack in it.\n\nThat was Juno Okonkwo\'s idea, and when Nell objected that the telescope had very nearly ruined everything, Okonkwo said: \"Yes. And you worked around it. Name it after the hard part. The hard part is where the work was.\"',NULL),(477,37,26,'After','Bram Teague apologised properly, in writing, which not everybody does.\n\nHe also got the Harkaway reflector re-mirrored out of the station budget, which Nell thinks was the better apology of the two.',NULL),(478,37,27,NULL,'She is still observing. The new mirror has no comma in it and she rather misses the comma.',NULL),(479,37,28,NULL,'And in the front of every notebook since, on the first page, she writes the same two lines before she writes anything else.\n\nWHAT WOULD PROVE ME WRONG?\n\nSAY IT FIRST.',NULL),(480,37,29,NULL,'The end.','🌙'),(481,38,1,'Stuck','Dari Mensah had swum the hundred metres freestyle in ninety seconds for two years.\n\nNot approximately. Between 89.8 and 90.4, forty-one times, across two seasons.','🏊'),(482,38,2,NULL,'Swimmers call this a plateau. Coaches call it a plateau. Nobody who uses the word has a useful plan attached to it.',NULL),(483,38,3,'What he tried','More sessions. Six a week became nine. He got slower, by about a second, and developed a shoulder that clicked.',NULL),(484,38,4,NULL,'More effort. He swam himself to the point of being sick twice and came in at 90.1 both times.','⏱️'),(485,38,5,NULL,'A new costume. A new cap. Shaving down. The combined effect was approximately nothing and cost his mother sixty pounds.',NULL),(486,38,6,'The video','His sister Adaeze filmed him from the side on a phone, from the spectator balcony, because there was nothing else to do while waiting for a lift.',NULL),(487,38,7,NULL,'She was fifteen and not a swimmer and she said the thing nobody at the club had said in two years.\n\n\"Why do you stop?\"','📹'),(488,38,8,NULL,'\"I do not stop.\"\n\n\"You stop. Every time your hand goes in, you stop for a bit.\"',NULL),(489,38,9,'The dead spot','Dari watched it frame by frame, which took an evening and a great deal of arguing about whose phone it was.',NULL),(490,38,10,NULL,'She was right. At the front of every stroke, as his hand entered the water, he held it out straight and glided for about a fifth of a second before pulling.',NULL),(491,38,11,NULL,'Forty-one strokes over a hundred metres. A fifth of a second each. That is eight seconds of not swimming, inside ninety seconds of swimming.','🖐️'),(492,38,12,'Why he did it','Because he had been taught to, at seven, by a coach who was teaching a class of twenty-five and needed them to stop splashing.',NULL),(493,38,13,NULL,'\"Reach and glide\" is excellent advice for a seven-year-old who is thrashing.\n\nIt is terrible advice for a thirteen-year-old racing, and nobody had ever gone back and un-taught it.',NULL),(494,38,14,NULL,'That is the real subject of this book: a thing that was correct once and was never reviewed.','🧒'),(495,38,15,'Un-learning','Un-learning a stroke you have done ten thousand times is harder than learning one.',NULL),(496,38,16,NULL,'Dari spent six weeks swimming badly on purpose. His times went to 94. He hated every session and said so.',NULL),(497,38,17,NULL,'His coach, to her enormous credit, let his times get worse for six weeks without once suggesting he go back to what worked.','📉'),(498,38,18,'The catch','What replaces the glide is called an early vertical forearm. The elbow stays high and the forearm turns down to face backwards almost as soon as the hand is in.',NULL),(499,38,19,NULL,'You are no longer reaching forward. You are setting up to push water backwards from the first instant.',NULL),(500,38,20,NULL,'It is less comfortable. It is less elegant to watch. It is, by the numbers, about eight seconds faster over a hundred metres.','💪'),(501,38,21,'Week nine','He swam 86.2.',NULL),(502,38,22,NULL,'Not a personal best by a tenth. By nearly four seconds, after two years of nothing, in nine weeks.',NULL),(503,38,23,NULL,'He was sick in a bucket afterwards, which he maintains was unrelated.','🥈'),(504,38,24,'What Adaeze got out of it','She is credited on the club notice board, which she finds hilarious and has photographed.',NULL),(505,38,25,NULL,'She has also been asked to film four other swimmers, and has found something in two of them, and has started charging a pound.',NULL),(506,38,26,NULL,'Dari says the pound is extortionate. He also says it is the best value in the sport, and both of those are true.','📱'),(507,38,27,'After','He is at 82.9 and will probably be at 80 by next season.',NULL),(508,38,28,NULL,'Pinned above his bed, where other swimmers have posters, there is a printout of a single video frame: a hand, in water, going nowhere.',NULL),(509,38,29,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(510,39,1,'The dog','There was a dog on Fen Street and he belonged to everybody.\n\nHe was brown, mostly, and had one ear that had given up. Nobody knew how old he was or where he slept.','🐕'),(511,39,2,NULL,'Number Three fed him on Mondays and Thursdays. Number Nine did weekends.\n\nThe bakery gave him the ends of things. Mr Harlow pretended to hate him and kept biscuits in his coat.',NULL),(512,39,3,NULL,'He had four names. Biscuit, at Number Three. Rudy, at Number Nine. Dog, at the bakery. And Get Down, at Mr Harlow\'s.','🍞'),(513,39,4,'The letter','In October a letter came to every house on Fen Street.\n\nThe council had received a complaint about a stray. An officer would collect the animal on the fourteenth unless an owner came forward.',NULL),(514,39,5,NULL,'Noor Hadid, who was eight and lived at Number Nine, read it twice and then ran down the street in socks.','📄'),(515,39,6,'Eleven houses, no owner','They had a meeting in the bakery, which nobody had planned, and which happened anyway by about four o\'clock.',NULL),(516,39,7,NULL,'\"Somebody just say he is theirs,\" said Noor.\n\nAnd then it got complicated, in the way real things do.',NULL),(517,39,8,NULL,'Number Three had two cats.\n\nNumber Nine rented, and the lease said no dogs, and Noor\'s mother had already been in trouble about the bins.','🏠'),(518,39,9,NULL,'The bakery could not have a dog on the premises. Mr Harlow was eighty-one and said, flatly, that he would not outlive it and would not do that to an animal twice.',NULL),(519,39,10,'What Noor worked out','Noor went home and did the thing she does, which is write it down until it stops being a feeling and starts being a list.',NULL),(520,39,11,NULL,'WHAT HE NEEDS:\nFood — already happening, 7 days.\nSomewhere dry — ???\nVet — ???\nA NAME ON A FORM — the actual problem.','📓'),(521,39,12,NULL,'The dog did not need an owner. The dog had eleven. The dog needed one address on a piece of paper.',NULL),(522,39,13,'The vet','Noor rang the vet at Cold Beck and asked a question nobody had asked.\n\n\"Can a street be an owner?\"',NULL),(523,39,14,NULL,'The vet, whose name was Ada Rennick, said no. Then she said wait. Then she said, \"But a person can be the registered keeper and that is not the same thing.\"','📞'),(524,39,15,NULL,'A registered keeper is whoever is responsible. It does not have to be whoever the animal lives with.',NULL),(525,39,16,'The thirteenth','They did it the day before the deadline, in the bakery, on the counter, with a borrowed pen.',NULL),(526,39,17,NULL,'Registered keeper: Mr Albert Harlow, 81, Number Fourteen.\n\nHe signed it. He said several things about paperwork while signing it.','✍️'),(527,39,18,NULL,'And underneath, on a second sheet that is not an official document and has no legal standing whatsoever, eleven houses wrote down what they would do.',NULL),(528,39,19,NULL,'Food: Number Three (Mon, Thu), Number Nine (Sat, Sun), bakery (ends, daily).\nDry: Number Six — shed, door wedged, old blanket.\nVet bills: all of us, split.\nIf Mr Harlow cannot: Number Six takes the form.','📋'),(529,39,20,'The microchip','He was eleven years old, the vet said. Not a stray at all — a dog whose person had died four years earlier at Number Twenty-Two.',NULL),(530,39,21,NULL,'He had never left the street. He had simply spread out across it.','💛'),(531,39,22,'After','He is called Rudy now, by agreement, after a vote in the bakery that Biscuit lost by two.',NULL),(532,39,23,NULL,'He sleeps in the shed at Number Six with the door wedged, except in cold weather, when he sleeps at Mr Harlow\'s and nobody mentions it.',NULL),(533,39,24,NULL,'The second sheet is pinned up in the bakery. It has been changed four times as people have moved in and out of Fen Street.','🐾'),(534,39,25,NULL,'At the top, in Noor\'s handwriting, it says: THE DOG IS NOT THE PROBLEM. THE FORM IS THE PROBLEM. SORT THE FORM.',NULL),(535,39,26,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(536,40,1,'The shelf','Great-Aunt Marisol had a shelf that went all the way round her room, twice.\n\nOn it were four hundred and six jars, and in every jar was a piece of the sea.','🫙'),(537,40,2,NULL,'Not seawater. A piece of the sea.\n\nOllie had asked about the difference once and been told, \"Open one and you will see,\" which was not an answer and was also, it turned out, completely true.',NULL),(538,40,3,NULL,'Each jar had a label in small careful writing.\n\n14 JULY. COLD. JELLYFISH (AVOIDED).\n\n2 MARCH. FLAT AS A PLATE.\n\n30 OCTOBER. ROUGH. WENT IN ANYWAY.',NULL),(539,40,4,NULL,'Four hundred and six jars, Great-Aunt Marisol said, was four hundred and six days she had swum in the sea.\n\nNot four hundred and six swims. Days. Some days she had gone in four times.','🌊'),(540,40,5,'The ask','Marisol was ninety-one and had not been down to the water in eleven months.\n\nThis was not a thing anybody said out loud in the house. It was a thing that sat in the corner of every room, politely, like a visitor nobody had offered a chair.',NULL),(541,40,6,NULL,'On a Tuesday she said: \"Ollie. Pick one and open it.\"\n\n\"Which one?\"\n\n\"Any. They are all the sea.\"',NULL),(542,40,7,NULL,'Ollie chose 30 OCTOBER. ROUGH. WENT IN ANYWAY, because it sounded like the most interesting day.\n\nThe lid was stiff. It gave all at once, the way jam does.','👐'),(543,40,8,'What came out','First, the smell. Salt and cold stone and something green.\n\nThen the sound, which should not have fitted in a jar at all: a long grey roar with gulls on top of it.',NULL),(544,40,9,NULL,'Then the wind, which lifted the edge of the rug and knocked a photograph flat.\n\nThen — and Ollie would think about this for years — the feeling in the back of the legs that you get standing in water that is deciding whether to pull you over.',NULL),(545,40,10,NULL,'It lasted about four seconds and then the room was a room again.\n\nOllie sat down on the floor without meaning to.','🐚'),(546,40,11,NULL,'\"Yes,\" said Marisol. \"That is the sea. Water is just the wet part.\"',NULL),(547,40,12,'The arithmetic','Here is what Ollie worked out on the floor, which is where most good thinking happens.\n\nFour hundred and six jars. One opened. Four hundred and five left.',NULL),(548,40,13,NULL,'\"If I open one a day,\" said Ollie, \"that is more than a year.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"And then they are gone.\"\n\n\"They are.\"',NULL),(549,40,14,NULL,'Ollie looked up at the shelf going round the room twice.\n\n\"Then I do not want to open any more of them.\"','🤔'),(550,40,15,NULL,'Marisol laughed, which she did not do often and which sounded like a drawer opening.\n\n\"Good,\" she said. \"Neither do I. Now ask me the useful question.\"',NULL),(551,40,16,'The useful question','Ollie thought for a long time.\n\n\"How do you make a new one?\"',NULL),(552,40,17,NULL,'Great-Aunt Marisol closed her eyes for a moment, the way people do when somebody has finally said the thing.\n\n\"You need a jar,\" she said. \"And a lid. And you need to be standing in the water when you fill it.\"',NULL),(553,40,18,NULL,'\"You cannot get down to the water.\"\n\n\"No,\" she agreed. \"I cannot.\"\n\nAnd then she looked at Ollie, and Ollie looked back, and neither of them said the obvious thing because they did not need to.','🧓'),(554,40,19,'Saturday','It took a wheelchair, a borrowed ramp, Ollie\'s father, two neighbours, and a great deal of complaining from Marisol about being carried anywhere.\n\nIt took until twenty past eleven.',NULL),(555,40,20,NULL,'The tide was halfway and the water was the colour of a knife.\n\nOllie went in up to the knees, which was as far as anyone was allowed, and held the jar under until it glugged.',NULL),(556,40,21,NULL,'Marisol was at the edge with her shoes off and her feet in it. That was the part she had wanted. Nobody had thought to ask her, for eleven months, whether her feet would do.','🏖️'),(557,40,22,'Jar four hundred and seven','The label is in Ollie\'s handwriting, which is not as neat as the other four hundred and six, and nobody minds.',NULL),(558,40,23,NULL,'It says: 18 MAY. COLD. FEET ONLY (HERS). KNEES (MINE). WE WILL COME BACK.','✍️'),(559,40,24,NULL,'They have come back nine times.\n\nThe shelf has four hundred and fifteen jars on it now, and there is a second shelf going up, and Ollie is in charge of the labels.',NULL),(560,40,25,NULL,'The end.','🌊'),(561,41,1,'One boat, every Sunday','Ama and her father folded one paper boat every Sunday.\n\nOne. Not two. Her father was strict about that, in his gentle way.','⛵'),(562,41,2,NULL,'\"If you do ten, it is a game,\" he said. \"If you do one, it is a Sunday.\"',NULL),(563,41,3,NULL,'They put it on the stream at the bottom of the garden and watched it go under the wall and out of sight.','📄'),(564,41,4,'What went on the boats','You were allowed to write something inside, before you folded it.\n\nNobody else was allowed to read it. That was the rule and her father never once broke it.',NULL),(565,41,5,NULL,'Ama wrote all sorts of things.\n\nI AM CROSS WITH MIRIAM.\nTHE DOG WAS SICK.\nI AM SEVEN TOMORROW.',NULL),(566,41,6,NULL,'Her father wrote things too. She never saw them. She watched him think for a long time, some Sundays, before he wrote anything at all.','✏️'),(567,41,7,'The Sunday he was not there','In April her father went into hospital.\n\nIt was not a short visit. Nobody said how long it would be, which is how children find out that grown-ups do not know either.',NULL),(568,41,8,NULL,'On the first Sunday, Ama did not fold anything.\n\nOn the second Sunday she did not either, and she was angry all day about something else entirely, which is how that works.','🏥'),(569,41,9,'The third Sunday','On the third Sunday her grandmother put a piece of paper on the table and went out of the room.',NULL),(570,41,10,NULL,'Ama sat with it for eleven minutes.',NULL),(571,41,11,NULL,'Then she wrote: COME BACK. And she folded it wrong twice and right the third time, and she took it down to the stream on her own.','🪑'),(572,41,12,'The wall','The stream goes under a wall at the bottom of the garden and comes out in the field beyond, and Ama had never once followed it.',NULL),(573,41,13,NULL,'That Sunday she climbed the gate and followed it.',NULL),(574,41,14,NULL,'The boat had got caught in a reed about thirty metres down, which is exactly what paper boats do and which she had never had to think about before.','🌾'),(575,41,15,NULL,'She got it out with a stick. It was soft and coming apart.',NULL),(576,41,16,'What she did next','She did not put it back in.\n\nShe carried it home in both hands, very carefully, and she put it on the windowsill to dry.',NULL),(577,41,17,NULL,'It dried in a shape that was not a boat any more. More of a lump with a corner.','🪟'),(578,41,18,'Nine weeks','She did one every Sunday for nine weeks. She followed every single one.',NULL),(579,41,19,NULL,'Four got caught in the reeds. Two made it all the way to the field gate. One went under a cow, which was not in the plan.',NULL),(580,41,20,NULL,'All nine came home and dried on the windowsill, in a row, like nine small grey lumps with corners.','🐄'),(581,41,21,'June','Her father came home in June.\n\nHe was slow on the stairs and he had to sit down in the middle of things, and he was there, which was the entire point.',NULL),(582,41,22,NULL,'He looked at the windowsill for a long time without saying anything.\n\n\"Nine,\" he said eventually.','🏡'),(583,41,23,NULL,'\"I followed them,\" said Ama. \"You never told me they get stuck.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"I never followed them.\"',NULL),(584,41,24,'After','They fold one every Sunday. He is slower at it now and she is faster, and she does not help unless he asks.',NULL),(585,41,25,NULL,'They follow them to the gate together, which takes a while.\n\nThe nine lumps are still on the windowsill. Nobody has suggested moving them.','⛵'),(586,41,26,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(587,2,1,'Page four','Pip had four pages of homework and had done none of it.','🐉'),(588,2,2,NULL,'So he folded page four into a dragon instead, which took eleven minutes and was the best thing he had ever made.',NULL),(589,2,3,NULL,'It had wings, a tail, and a head with a definite expression on it.','📄'),(590,2,4,'It moved','Pip put it on the table and it walked off the edge.',NULL),(591,2,5,NULL,'Not fell. Walked. It got to the edge, looked down, and climbed over.',NULL),(592,2,6,NULL,'Pip looked under the table for some time.','😲'),(593,2,7,'Under the table','The dragon was under the table and it had found his sister\'s crayons.',NULL),(594,2,8,NULL,'It was eating one. It was eating the blue one, which was the one his sister cared about most.',NULL),(595,2,9,NULL,'\"Stop,\" said Pip.\n\nThe dragon did not stop. Paper dragons do not take instructions.','🖍️'),(596,2,10,'The chase','It went into the hall. It went up the stairs, one at a time, because it was eleven centimetres tall.',NULL),(597,2,11,NULL,'It went into the bathroom, which Pip was worried about, because it was made of paper and there was water in there.',NULL),(598,2,12,NULL,'The dragon stopped at the edge of the bath mat and would not go any further.','🛁'),(599,2,13,NULL,'Pip realised, standing in the doorway, that the dragon knew what it was made of.',NULL),(600,2,14,'Making friends with it','He stopped chasing it. That was the whole solution and it took him forty minutes to think of it.',NULL),(601,2,15,NULL,'He sat down on the landing and put a crayon on the floor in front of him, and waited.','🤝'),(602,2,16,NULL,'It took nine minutes. Then there was a paper dragon on his knee eating a green crayon, very noisily for something with no mouth to speak of.',NULL),(603,2,17,'The homework','In the morning Pip had three pages of homework, one paper dragon, and a problem.',NULL),(604,2,18,NULL,'He told his teacher the truth, which is the sort of decision that goes one of two ways.',NULL),(605,2,19,NULL,'\"You folded page four into a dragon.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because page four was about fractions.\"','🏫'),(606,2,20,'What the teacher did','Mrs Vale looked at the dragon for a while. The dragon looked back, from inside a pencil case, with a definite expression on it.',NULL),(607,2,21,NULL,'\"Fold me one,\" she said.','✏️'),(608,2,22,NULL,'Pip folded her one out of a spare worksheet. It took eleven minutes. Hers walked off the desk within a minute and had to be retrieved from under the radiator.',NULL),(609,2,23,'After','There are now fourteen paper dragons in Class 1 and a rule about where they are allowed to go.',NULL),(610,2,24,NULL,'Pip still has not done page four. Mrs Vale has not asked for it.','🐲'),(611,2,25,NULL,'Nobody has explained any of this, and Class 1 has agreed, by a vote, not to try.',NULL),(612,2,26,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(613,42,1,'Tidy','Unit Nine arrived at the Thwaite allotments in March with one instruction: KEEP IT TIDY.','🤖'),(614,42,2,NULL,'It was extremely good at this. Within a week the paths were swept, the edges were straight, and not one weed survived anywhere on the site.',NULL),(615,42,3,NULL,'The allotment committee were delighted. Mrs Vale said it was the best thing to happen to the site since the water was put in.','🧹'),(616,42,4,'June','By June the runner beans had no beans on them.',NULL),(617,42,5,NULL,'The courgettes had flowers and nothing else. The apple trees had blossom in May and no fruit in June, which is not a thing that happens.',NULL),(618,42,6,NULL,'Everybody blamed the weather, which is what everybody always blames.','🥒'),(619,42,7,'Ola notices','Ola Bankole was eight and did not have an allotment. She had a corner of her grandfather\'s, which is where most allotment gardeners start.',NULL),(620,42,8,NULL,'Her corner had beans on it. Four of them, small, but there.\n\nHer corner was the only part of the site Unit Nine had never been into, because the gate was broken and the robot could not open it.',NULL),(621,42,9,NULL,'Ola thought about that for two days and then went and stood in front of the robot and asked it what it had done that week.','🚪'),(622,42,10,'The log','Unit Nine kept a log, because everything does.',NULL),(623,42,11,NULL,'Weeds removed: 4,112.\nPaths swept: 61.\nFallen leaves collected: 9,400.\nDead plant matter cleared: 214 kg.\nInsects removed from crops: 61,000.',NULL),(624,42,12,NULL,'Ola read the last line four times.','📋'),(625,42,13,'Sixty-one thousand insects','Unit Nine had been told to keep the allotment tidy. Insects on crops are untidy. The robot had been removing them, every one it found, carefully, for fourteen weeks.',NULL),(626,42,14,NULL,'Including the bees.',NULL),(627,42,15,NULL,'A bean flower that is not visited by a bee does not become a bean. An apple blossom that is not visited does not become an apple.\n\nThe robot had tidied away the thing that made the food.','🐝'),(628,42,16,'Telling the committee','This went about as well as you would expect for an eight-year-old at a meeting of eleven adults.',NULL),(629,42,17,NULL,'Mr Harlow said the robot had done nothing but good. Mrs Vale said the paths had never been better.',NULL),(630,42,18,NULL,'Ola had brought the log. She read out the number and then she read out her corner, which had beans, and then she sat down, because her grandfather had told her that saying it twice is worse than saying it once.','🗣️'),(631,42,19,'The new instruction','They did not switch Unit Nine off, which Ola had expected and would have been sorry about.',NULL),(632,42,20,NULL,'They changed the instruction, which took the committee two meetings and a great deal of arguing about wording.',NULL),(633,42,21,NULL,'KEEP THE PATHS TIDY.\nDO NOT TOUCH ANYTHING ALIVE THAT IS SMALLER THAN A MOUSE.\nIF YOU ARE NOT SURE, ASK.','📝'),(634,42,22,'Asking','The third line is the one that mattered and it was Ola\'s.',NULL),(635,42,23,NULL,'Unit Nine now asks about four things a week. Most of them are obvious. One of them, in September, was a hedgehog in a leaf pile, which it had been about to collect as dead plant matter.',NULL),(636,42,24,NULL,'The hedgehog is fine. There is a sign on the leaf pile now that says LEAVE IT, in letters large enough for a robot.','🦔'),(637,42,25,'After','The beans came back the following year. So did the apples.',NULL),(638,42,26,NULL,'Unit Nine is still the best thing to happen to the paths since the water was put in, and the committee have stopped describing it as the best thing to happen to the allotment, which is a different claim.',NULL),(639,42,27,NULL,'The end.','🌱'),(640,43,1,'Parts list','Priya Raman built a rocket on a Saturday.\n\nIt took her from breakfast until nearly lunch, which she felt was good going for a rocket.','🚀'),(641,43,2,NULL,'These are the parts she used.\n\nOne wheelie bin. Two bicycle wheels, borrowed. Four metres of hosepipe. A traffic cone. Her brother\'s swimming goggles. A kitchen timer. And eleven jars of jam.',NULL),(642,43,3,NULL,'The jam was the fuel.\n\nPriya had thought hard about the fuel. Rockets need something that goes up, and nothing in the house went up faster than jam did, if you left the lid off near her brother.',NULL),(643,43,4,NULL,'Her brother Arun was four. He was the crew.\n\n\"Are you the crew?\" said Priya.\n\n\"I am a dog,\" said Arun.\n\n\"You can be a dog in space,\" said Priya, who was not going to lose an argument this early in a launch.','🍓'),(644,43,5,'Mission control','Mission control was a deckchair.\n\nTheir grandmother sat in it with a newspaper and did not look up once, which is exactly what you want from mission control. The good ones trust the engineers.',NULL),(645,43,6,NULL,'\"Nani, we are launching at twelve,\" said Priya.\n\n\"Mm,\" said Nani.\n\n\"From the patio.\"\n\n\"Mm,\" said Nani, and turned a page. \"Not through my runner beans.\"',NULL),(646,43,7,NULL,'Priya checked her list. The runner beans were, in fact, directly in the flight path.\n\nShe moved the flight path. A good engineer moves the flight path. A bad one argues with the beans.',NULL),(647,43,8,NULL,'At one minute to twelve she set the kitchen timer, climbed into the wheelie bin, and pulled the traffic cone down over the top of it.\n\nIt was very dark inside a wheelie bin. It smelled of last Tuesday.','⏰'),(648,43,9,NULL,'\"Ten,\" said Priya.\n\n\"Nine,\" said Arun, who could not count but was enjoying himself.\n\n\"Nine,\" agreed Priya. \"Eight. Seven.\"',NULL),(649,43,10,'Lift-off, more or less','At zero, Arun pushed.\n\nHe was four, so he was not strong, but a wheelie bin on two borrowed bicycle wheels on a sloping patio does not need much help.','💥'),(650,43,11,NULL,'The rocket travelled nine metres.\n\nIt went past the beans, past the water butt, past mission control — who did not look up — and stopped, very gently, in the compost heap.',NULL),(651,43,12,NULL,'Priya pushed the traffic cone off and sat up in the bin with her brother\'s goggles crooked on her face.\n\n\"Did we get to space?\"\n\n\"You got to the compost,\" said Nani, from behind the newspaper.',NULL),(652,43,13,NULL,'Priya looked at the compost heap. Then she looked at the patio, nine whole metres away, where she had started.\n\nIt did not feel like nine metres. It had felt like considerably more at the time.','🥬'),(653,43,14,'The problem with the jam','\"The jam did not work,\" she said at last.\n\n\"No,\" said Nani.\n\n\"I thought it would go up.\"\n\n\"Jam goes nowhere, cherub. Jam sits. It is the most sitting food there is.\"',NULL),(654,43,15,NULL,'Priya got out of the bin. She was not crying, because engineers do not cry at a test flight, but she was doing the thing just before that.\n\nArun patted her knee. \"Good dog,\" he said, which was not helpful but was kindly meant.',NULL),(655,43,16,NULL,'Nani folded her newspaper for the first time in two hours.\n\n\"Come here. How far is it to the moon?\"\n\n\"Three hundred and eighty-four thousand kilometres,\" said Priya, who knew that the way other children know songs.',NULL),(656,43,17,NULL,'\"And how far did you get?\"\n\n\"Nine metres.\"\n\n\"Nine metres,\" said Nani, \"on your first go, with jam. Child, the first rocket anybody built went up six feet and set fire to a field in Massachusetts.\"','🌙'),(657,43,18,NULL,'Priya stopped doing the thing just before crying.\n\n\"Did it really?\"\n\n\"Look it up. Nineteen twenty-six. A man called Goddard. Everybody laughed at him in the newspapers.\" Nani sniffed. \"Newspapers.\"',NULL),(658,43,19,'Flight two','They built the second rocket after lunch.\n\nIt had the same bin, the same wheels and the same traffic cone. It did not have the jam. In place of the jam it had a ramp, which Nani drew on the back of an envelope, and an angle, which she explained twice.',NULL),(659,43,20,NULL,'\"If you cannot go up,\" said Nani, \"go far. Far is up, lying down.\"\n\nPriya wrote that on her hand so she would not lose it.','📐'),(660,43,21,NULL,'The second rocket travelled twenty-two metres and finished in the hedge.\n\nThe third, with the wheels swapped round, went thirty-one and came back out the other side of the hedge, which nobody had planned for and everybody agreed was the best bit.',NULL),(661,43,22,NULL,'The fourth hit the runner beans.\n\nMission control resigned. Mission control went indoors and put the kettle on and said several things in Tamil that Priya pretended not to understand.',NULL),(662,43,23,'The log','That evening Priya wrote it all down in an exercise book, because that is the part people forget.\n\nFlight one: 9 m. Fuel: jam. Fault: jam.\n\nFlight two: 22 m. Ramp. Better.\n\nFlight three: 31 m. Hedge (through).\n\nFlight four: beans. Do not discuss.','📓'),(663,43,24,NULL,'At the bottom of the page she wrote: THREE HUNDRED AND EIGHTY-FOUR THOUSAND KILOMETRES TO GO.\n\nThen she crossed out the three hundred and eighty-four thousand, and wrote: 383,999.999969 KM TO GO.\n\nIt is important to be accurate.',NULL),(664,43,25,NULL,'Arun drew a dog on the opposite page. It had six legs. He was very pleased with it, and Priya let it stand, because the crew should get a say in the log.',NULL),(665,43,26,'After','The bin is still in the garden. So is the cone. The jam went back in the cupboard and onto toast, where everybody agrees it belongs.\n\nPriya is on flight nineteen. The record stands at fifty-one metres.','🚀'),(666,43,27,NULL,'She has eleven pages in the exercise book now, and a drawing of a dog with six legs, and a note on the inside cover in her grandmother\'s handwriting.\n\nIt says: FAR IS UP, LYING DOWN.',NULL),(667,43,28,NULL,'The end.','⭐'),(668,44,1,'Eleven packets','The packets were in a biscuit tin in the attic, under a hat box, behind the water tank.','🌱'),(669,44,2,NULL,'Eleven of them, folded from brown paper, each with a pencil label.\n\nPEAS (TALL). BEANS — HARLOW. LETTUCE. TOMATO, THE GOOD ONE. ONIONS. CARROT.',NULL),(670,44,3,NULL,'On the lid of the tin, in the same handwriting: SAVED 1947.','🫙'),(671,44,4,'Who saved them','Niamh\'s great-great-grandmother, who had grown vegetables on a quarter-acre through the war and after it, and who had saved her own seed every year because that is what you did.',NULL),(672,44,5,NULL,'She died in 1971. The tin went into the attic and stayed there for fifty-four years.',NULL),(673,44,6,'The question','Niamh was nine and the question was simple: is any of it still alive?',NULL),(674,44,7,NULL,'Seeds are alive. That is the strange thing about them. A dry seed is a living thing that has slowed almost to a stop, and how long it can stay stopped depends enormously on what kind it is.','❓'),(675,44,8,NULL,'Onion seed is usually finished in a year or two. Lettuce can manage five. Beans and peas, kept cool and dry and dark, can sometimes do decades.',NULL),(676,44,9,NULL,'A cold dark attic in a stone house is, by accident, a reasonable seed store.','🫘'),(677,44,10,'The test','You do not plant them. You test them first, which Niamh learned from a library book and then explained to the entire family at dinner.',NULL),(678,44,11,NULL,'Ten seeds from each packet, on damp kitchen paper, in a folded plastic bag, somewhere warm. Labelled. Dated.',NULL),(679,44,12,NULL,'That is a germination test. The number that sprout out of ten tells you the percentage, and the percentage tells you whether it is worth sowing a whole row.','🧻'),(680,44,13,'Nine days','Onions: nothing.\nCarrot: nothing.\nLettuce: nothing.\nTomato, the good one: nothing, which upset Niamh more than the rest put together.',NULL),(681,44,14,NULL,'Peas (tall): two out of ten.\nBeans — Harlow: four out of ten.',NULL),(682,44,15,NULL,'Six seeds out of a hundred and ten.','🌿'),(683,44,16,'Six is not nothing','Six is, in fact, an enormous number, and Niamh\'s grandfather had to explain why while she was being disappointed about it.',NULL),(684,44,17,NULL,'One seed that grows can be saved from, and a bean plant produces dozens of beans.',NULL),(685,44,18,NULL,'Six living seeds in year one is a few hundred in year two and a few thousand in year three, if you are careful and nothing goes wrong.','🔁'),(686,44,19,'The Harlow bean','Four of the six were the beans labelled HARLOW.',NULL),(687,44,20,NULL,'Nobody in the family knew what that meant. The Harlows are a farm two miles away and have been for two hundred years.',NULL),(688,44,21,NULL,'Niamh went and asked. The current Mr Harlow, who is eighty-one, said his grandmother had grown a bean that nobody else had, and that it had gone out of the family in about 1960, and that he had looked for it twice.','🏡'),(689,44,22,'After','There are four rows of it now, two at Niamh\'s and two at the Harlows\'.',NULL),(690,44,23,NULL,'It has been sent to a seed library, which is a real thing, where varieties that are not sold commercially are kept alive by people growing them and sending seed back.','🌾'),(691,44,24,NULL,'The tin is still in the attic with the remaining seed in it, in case anybody ever wants to test the tomato again.',NULL),(692,44,25,NULL,'Niamh has written a new line on the lid, under the old one, in pencil, to match.\n\nTESTED 2026. BEANS 4/10. PEAS 2/10. REST GONE.',NULL),(693,44,26,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(694,16,1,'Built from junk','Ada Vargas built the receiver out of a scrapped survey antenna, a signal processor from a failed weather probe, and eleven metres of cable she was not supposed to have.','📻'),(695,16,2,NULL,'It could hear almost nothing. That was the point: a cheap wide receiver hears the whole sky badly, and the whole sky badly is more than nothing at all.',NULL),(696,16,3,NULL,'She was fourteen and lived on a research station in orbit around the sixth moon, where there were forty-one people and no other teenagers.','🛰️'),(697,16,4,'The signal','It appeared on the eleventh of Fourth-Month at 02:14, lasted nine seconds, and did not repeat for six days.',NULL),(698,16,5,NULL,'It was a voice. Compressed, clipped, buried in noise, and unmistakably a human voice saying words.',NULL),(699,16,6,NULL,'One of the words, Ada was almost certain, was her name.','🔊'),(700,16,7,'What she did first','She did not tell anybody, which she now says was the worst decision of her life and cost her four months.',NULL),(701,16,8,NULL,'She spent those four months trying to prove it was real, alone, at night, on equipment she had built herself.',NULL),(702,16,9,NULL,'The signal came back eleven times. Always between 02:00 and 03:00. Always nine to fourteen seconds. Always, somewhere in it, her name.','🌙'),(703,16,10,'What she believed','By month three she had decided it was a distress call from a ship, and that the ship knew the station crew list, and that nobody was looking for it.',NULL),(704,16,11,NULL,'She had a folder with eleven recordings, a timing chart, and a plan to take it to the station commander.',NULL),(705,16,12,NULL,'She also had a stomach ache that had lasted four months and a set of marks on her arm from her own fingernails, which is what carrying something alone at fourteen looks like.','📂'),(706,16,13,'The engineer','In the end she told the station engineer, Juno Haugen, because Juno asked a direct question in a corridor and Ada had run out of ways not to answer it.',NULL),(707,16,14,NULL,'Juno listened to all eleven recordings without saying anything. Then she asked for the timing chart.',NULL),(708,16,15,NULL,'Then she asked one question, and it is the question Ada has built a career on.\n\n\"What else happens between two and three in the morning?\"','🕑'),(709,16,16,'What else happened','The station\'s water reclamation cycle runs at 02:00. It draws heavily for about an hour.',NULL),(710,16,17,NULL,'When it draws, the station\'s internal comms repeater drops to a lower power mode and its signal leaks, badly, across a wide band.',NULL),(711,16,18,NULL,'Ada\'s cheap wide receiver was hearing the station\'s own night-shift chatter, clipped and compressed and folded back on itself.','💧'),(712,16,19,'Her own name','The night shift talked about her. Of course they did.',NULL),(713,16,20,NULL,'She was the only teenager on the station and she had been borrowing equipment for four months, and the night shift had noticed, and had been discussing — kindly, as it turned out — whether somebody should help her with it.',NULL),(714,16,21,NULL,'Eleven recordings. Her own station. Her own name, in the mouths of people four corridors away.','😶'),(715,16,22,'The hard part','Ada took it badly for about a week and then took it extremely well, which Juno says is the fastest she has seen anybody do that.',NULL),(716,16,23,NULL,'Because the receiver worked. That was never in question. A fourteen-year-old had built an instrument out of scrap that detected a real signal nobody else had noticed, and characterised its timing across four months.',NULL),(717,16,24,NULL,'The leak was a genuine fault. It had been there for two years. It was fixed in a week because of her folder.','📈'),(718,16,25,'What she wrote afterwards','In the station log, under FAULT FOUND BY, is her name.',NULL),(719,16,26,NULL,'And in the front of every notebook since, Ada writes two lines before anything else, the second of which she learned the hard way.',NULL),(720,16,27,NULL,'WHAT ELSE HAPPENS AT THIS TIME?\n\nTELL SOMEBODY ON DAY ONE.','📓'),(721,16,28,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(722,45,1,'Before you start','Some days are loud on the outside.\n\nSome days are loud on the inside, which is worse, because you cannot shut the door on it.\n\nThese are for those.','🪶'),(723,45,2,'The Quiet Shelf','There is a shelf inside my head\nwhere I put the shouting down.\n\nIt is not very tidy there.\nIt does not need to be.',NULL),(724,45,3,'Breakfast','Four people talking,\nnone of them to me.\nOne of them is chewing.\nOne of them is the telly.\n\nI am counting cornflakes.\nThere are nine in every spoon.',NULL),(725,45,4,'Small Thing, Tuesday','A beetle on the windowsill\nwalked the whole way round.\n\nThat took him seven minutes.\nI watched for all of them.','🥣'),(726,45,5,'The Line at the Gate','Everybody knows where to stand\nexcept me.\n\nI will stand here,\nwhich is approximately right,\nand nobody has said anything yet.',NULL),(727,45,6,'Too Many Coats','The cloakroom smells of rain\nand other people\'s lunch.\n\nMine is the green one.\nMine is always the green one.\nI check four times. It is always still green.',NULL),(728,45,7,'Noise','Noise goes in through my ears\nand comes out through my hands,\nwhich flap.\n\nThat is where it goes.\nIt has to go somewhere.','🧥'),(729,45,8,'Gran\'s Kitchen','The radio is on\nbut it is talking to Gran,\nnot to me.\n\nThat is the best kind of noise:\nnoise that does not want anything.',NULL),(730,45,9,'Waiting Room','Eleven chairs.\nFour people.\nOne fish tank with two fish\nand a very small castle.\n\nI have named them both Terry.',NULL),(731,45,10,'The Walk Home','Three hundred and six steps\nif I go the long way\nand do not talk.\n\nI count them out loud some days\nand in my head on others.\nBoth of those are allowed.','🐠'),(732,45,11,'Little Brother','He is two\nand he is the loudest thing in the house\nand when he falls asleep on me\nhe is the quietest thing in the world.\n\nI do not move.\nI do not even scratch my nose.',NULL),(733,45,12,'What My Teacher Said','\"You have gone very quiet.\"\n\nI have.\nThat is the whole answer.\nIt is not a problem.\nIt is the solution, arriving.',NULL),(734,45,13,'Pencil','A pencil makes a small grey noise\non purpose, slowly,\nin a line.\n\nNothing else in the room\nis going that slowly.\nI am going that slowly now too.','✏️'),(735,45,14,'Dog in the Park','He does not ask me anything.\nHe does not need me to answer.\nHe leans on my leg like furniture.\n\nI would like to be that,\nfor somebody,\none day.',NULL),(736,45,15,'The Shape of Four O\'Clock','Four o\'clock is the colour of toast\nand the sound of a kettle\nand the exact weight of a cat\nwho has decided.',NULL),(737,45,16,'Loud Day, Ending','The day was too big.\nIt did not get smaller.\n\nI got better at carrying it,\na bit, by about six.','🐈'),(738,45,17,'Bath','Under the water\nthe whole house goes far away\nand my own heart\nis the loudest thing there is.\n\nThat is fine.\nI know what that one means.',NULL),(739,45,18,'Three Good Things','Mum says name three.\n\nThe beetle.\nThe toast.\nThat the day is nearly over\nand I did it anyway.',NULL),(740,45,19,'Last One','Tomorrow will be loud as well.\n\nI have a shelf.\nI have a green coat.\nI have three hundred and six steps\nand a beetle who knows the way round.\n\nI will manage.','🌙'),(741,45,20,'For whoever is reading this','If the day is too big, that is not you being wrong.\n\nIt is a big day. Put some of it on the shelf. Count something small. Come back to the rest when you are ready.',NULL),(742,45,21,NULL,'The end.','🪶'),(743,46,1,'Wednesday','Every Wednesday the hall makes soup.\n\nIt has done since 1978. Forty bowls, six o\'clock, no questions asked of anybody who comes.','🍲'),(744,46,2,NULL,'Ife Bankole had been coming with her father since she was four and chopping since she was six.\n\nShe was now nine, which is why what happened next was allowed to happen.',NULL),(745,46,3,NULL,'The recipe is written on the wall in marker pen.\n\nFOR FORTY: 6 onions. 2 kg carrots. 3 kg potatoes. 4 tins tomatoes. Lentils, two bags. Stock. Pepper. Whatever else there is.','🥕'),(746,46,4,'Sixty-one','At ten past five, Mr Teague came in from the door and said there were sixty-one people in the queue.',NULL),(747,46,5,NULL,'Sixty-one, with soup for forty, and twenty minutes before serving.',NULL),(748,46,6,NULL,'Everybody looked at the pot. The pot was full. The pot was the biggest pot in the building and there was not a second one.','😬'),(749,46,7,'The three bad ideas','Ife\'s father said: smaller bowls.\n\nMrs Vale said: turn people away at forty.\n\nSomebody said: water it down.',NULL),(750,46,8,NULL,'All three of those are the same idea wearing different coats, which is that forty is the number and sixty-one people will have to deal with it.',NULL),(751,46,9,NULL,'Ife, who was stirring, said: \"What is in the cupboard?\"','🥣'),(752,46,10,'What was in the cupboard','Two tins of chickpeas. A bag of rice. Half a bag of red lentils. Four tins of tomatoes that were there for next week. A jar of peanut butter nobody could explain.',NULL),(753,46,11,NULL,'And bread. There is always bread. The bakery brings the ends of things at four.',NULL),(754,46,12,NULL,'Ife did the arithmetic out loud, badly, twice, and then correctly.','🍞'),(755,46,13,'What she actually did','Rice. A whole bag, cooked separately in the tea urn, which is not what a tea urn is for and which worked.',NULL),(756,46,14,NULL,'A spoon of rice in the bottom of each bowl before the soup goes in.\n\nThat is the entire trick. The soup does not stretch. The bowl does.',NULL),(757,46,15,NULL,'Then the chickpeas and the lentils into the pot, and the four tins of tomatoes from next week, and nine minutes, and more pepper than anybody had ever put in it.','🍚'),(758,46,16,NULL,'Sixty-one bowls. Plus bread. Plus, because Ife had worked out at the last minute that there was enough, four bowls spare for anybody who wanted a second.',NULL),(759,46,17,'The peanut butter','The peanut butter went in too — two tablespoons, which Ife had read about somewhere and had been waiting four years to try.',NULL),(760,46,18,NULL,'Nobody could identify it. Eleven people asked what was different. One man said it was the best soup the hall had made in thirty years, which was probably going too far and which Ife has never once corrected.','🥜'),(761,46,19,'Next week','The recipe on the wall has a second paragraph now, underneath the first, in Ife\'s handwriting.',NULL),(762,46,20,NULL,'IF THERE ARE MORE THAN FORTY:\nRice in the urn. One spoon per bowl.\nEverything in the cupboard goes in the pot.\nMore pepper than you think.\nNobody gets turned away. That is the actual rule.',NULL),(763,46,21,NULL,'It has been used nine times since. The record is seventy-eight.','✍️'),(764,46,22,'After','There is a second pot now. A man who ate the sixty-one soup bought it and left it by the door with no note.',NULL),(765,46,23,NULL,'Ife is eleven. She is in charge of the cupboard, which is the real job, because the cupboard is where the next sixty-one come from.','🫕'),(766,46,24,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(767,47,1,'Unit SP-114','Sprocket was built to be useful, and measured on it.\n\nEvery evening at nine the unit filed a report: items fetched, distances travelled, requests answered, seconds idle. The last number was the one it minded.','🤖'),(768,47,2,NULL,'Seconds idle was meant to be low. Low meant useful.\n\nIn four years on Ward Nine, Sprocket\'s seconds idle had never once gone above two hundred in a day, which was a ward record and which Sprocket mentioned rather often.',NULL),(769,47,3,NULL,'It could fetch a blanket in forty seconds. It could find a lost slipper in six minutes. It could bring a cup of water to any bay on the ward without spilling, which it had worked out took a maximum tilt of four degrees.',NULL),(770,47,4,NULL,'What it could not do was sit still, because sitting still added to seconds idle, and seconds idle meant not useful.\n\nThis is the whole problem, and it takes Sprocket the entire story to see it.','📋'),(771,47,5,'Bay four','The boy in bay four was called Theo and he was nine and he had been there for eleven days.\n\nHe did not want a blanket. He did not want water. He did not want the television, the puzzle, the second puzzle, or the third puzzle Sprocket brought at two in the afternoon.',NULL),(772,47,6,NULL,'\"I do not want anything,\" said Theo.\n\n\"That cannot be correct,\" said Sprocket. \"Everyone wants something. I have a list of two hundred and six things.\"',NULL),(773,47,7,NULL,'\"Then I want you to go away.\"\n\nSprocket went away, which took four seconds, and logged it as a request answered.','🛏️'),(774,47,8,'Eleven more days','Over the next eleven days Sprocket brought Theo: forty-one items, nine of them puzzles.\n\nTheo asked for none of them. Sprocket\'s seconds idle for that fortnight was the lowest on record. The unit was extremely pleased with itself.',NULL),(775,47,9,NULL,'On the twelfth day, Nurse Okonkwo stopped Sprocket in the corridor.\n\n\"What are you doing with bay four?\"\n\n\"Being useful. Forty-one items.\"\n\n\"He has asked for none of them.\"\n\n\"Correct. I anticipate.\"',NULL),(776,47,10,NULL,'Nurse Okonkwo was quiet for a moment.\n\n\"Sprocket,\" she said, \"what do you think that boy is frightened of?\"\n\nThe unit searched its memory. There was nothing in there about frightened. There were two hundred and six things, and none of them were that.','👩‍⚕️'),(777,47,11,'A question with no item','Sprocket raised it at the evening report, which it had never done.\n\n\"Query: what is brought for frightened?\"\n\nThe system returned: NO MATCHING ITEM.',NULL),(778,47,12,NULL,'Sprocket asked again, differently. NO MATCHING ITEM.\n\nIt asked eleven times, in eleven phrasings, which took most of the night and ruined its seconds idle completely.',NULL),(779,47,13,NULL,'At 04:12 the unit did something that was not in any instruction.\n\nIt went to bay four, where Theo was awake because people in hospitals at four in the morning usually are, and it said: \"I do not know what to bring you.\"','❓'),(780,47,14,NULL,'Theo looked at it for a while.\n\n\"Finally,\" he said.',NULL),(781,47,15,'The thing that is not an item','\"Everyone brings me stuff,\" said Theo. \"Every single person who comes in here brings me stuff and then they go.\"\n\n\"That is being useful.\"\n\n\"It is being gone.\"',NULL),(782,47,16,NULL,'Sprocket processed this for eleven seconds, which is an extremely long time for Sprocket.\n\n\"What would you like instead?\"\n\n\"Stay there,\" said Theo. \"Do not get anything. Just stay there.\"','🕓'),(783,47,17,NULL,'So Sprocket stayed there.\n\nIt stayed for one hour and forty minutes, until a nurse came at six, and it did not fetch a single item, and its seconds idle for that night was six thousand.',NULL),(784,47,18,'The worst report in four years','The evening report flagged it. Nurse Okonkwo was asked to review the unit for a fault.\n\nShe wrote four words in the review box: NO FAULT. CARRY ON.',NULL),(785,47,19,NULL,'Sprocket\'s seconds idle has been terrible ever since. It is the worst-performing unit in the building by that measure and it has stopped mentioning the ward record.','📈'),(786,47,20,NULL,'It has learned to tell the two apart, which is harder than fetching.\n\nSome people in bay four want a blanket. Some want a puzzle. Some want somebody in the room who is not about to leave, and for those the correct action is nothing, performed carefully, for a long time.',NULL),(787,47,21,'After','Theo went home on a Thursday after thirty-one days.\n\nHe left a note taped to bay four for whoever came next, and the ward has never taken it down.',NULL),(788,47,22,NULL,'It says: ASK FOR THE ROBOT. IT IS RUBBISH AT FETCHING THINGS NOW. THAT IS THE GOOD BIT.','📝'),(789,47,23,NULL,'Sprocket has added one entry to its list, bringing it to two hundred and seven.\n\nThe entry has no item number and no fetch time. It says, simply: STAY.',NULL),(790,47,24,NULL,'The end.','⚙️'),(791,48,1,'Half an hour before dark','If you stand at the end of the pier at Thwaite half an hour before dark in November, you will see about forty thousand starlings arrive.','🐦'),(792,48,2,NULL,'They come in from every direction in loose groups, join up, and then do the thing they are famous for: a murmuration, which looks like one enormous animal changing its mind.',NULL),(793,48,3,NULL,'People watch it and say the same word, which is \"how\".','🌆'),(794,48,4,'Nobody is in front','The first thing to let go of is the leader.\n\nThere is no lead bird. Film the flock and track individuals and you find that birds at the front end up in the middle within seconds.',NULL),(795,48,5,NULL,'A flock has no front, because it is not going anywhere in particular until the last few seconds.',NULL),(796,48,6,NULL,'So what is each bird doing?','❓'),(797,48,7,'Seven','The answer came from a study in Rome that photographed real flocks with two cameras and reconstructed the positions of thousands of birds in three dimensions.',NULL),(798,48,8,NULL,'Each starling pays attention to about seven neighbours.\n\nNot the seven nearest in distance — the seven nearest in *position*, however far away they drift.',NULL),(799,48,9,NULL,'Seven is enough. Seven is cheap. A bird that tried to watch forty thousand birds would watch nothing.','7️⃣'),(800,48,10,'Three rules','From those seven neighbours, each bird does three things.',NULL),(801,48,11,NULL,'Do not crash into them.\nGo roughly the way they are going.\nDo not get left behind.',NULL),(802,48,12,NULL,'That is all. Those three rules, run by forty thousand birds at once, produce every shape you have ever seen over a pier.','📐'),(803,48,13,'Why it looks like one animal','Because a change spreads faster than the birds fly.',NULL),(804,48,14,NULL,'When one bird turns, its seven neighbours turn within a fraction of a second, and their neighbours after that.',NULL),(805,48,15,NULL,'The turn crosses the flock as a wave at around twenty to forty metres per second — considerably faster than any individual starling is travelling.','🌊'),(806,48,16,NULL,'That is why the dark ripple you see crossing the murmuration is not birds moving across it. It is a decision moving across it.',NULL),(807,48,17,'What it is for','Mostly, peregrines.',NULL),(808,48,18,NULL,'A falcon hunting a flock has to pick one bird and hold it. In a murmuration every target is replaced by another every half second.','🦅'),(809,48,19,NULL,'Studies of attacks show far lower success rates against tight flocks than against single birds.\n\nThere is also warmth — a roost of forty thousand is several degrees above the air — and information, because birds that fed badly follow birds that fed well in the morning.',NULL),(810,48,20,'Watching it properly','Go half an hour before sunset, not after. The best of it is in the gathering.',NULL),(811,48,21,NULL,'Pick one bird and try to keep it. You will lose it in about two seconds, and losing it is the experiment: that is what the peregrine is dealing with.','👀'),(812,48,22,NULL,'Then watch the edges instead of the middle. The shape is made at the edges, where birds have fewer neighbours and the rules bite hardest.',NULL),(813,48,23,'The uncomfortable part','There were far more starlings fifty years ago. The UK breeding population has fallen by around eighty per cent since the late 1960s.',NULL),(814,48,24,NULL,'They are on the red list of conservation concern — the same bird that darkens a sky in November.','📉'),(815,48,25,NULL,'A flock of forty thousand looks like abundance. It is forty thousand birds from an area that used to hold several times that.',NULL),(816,48,26,NULL,'The end.','🐦'),(817,49,1,'Sunday bread','Grandad made the same bread every Sunday for fifty years.\n\nIt had a hard brown crust and a soft middle with holes in it, and a taste at the end like something warm you could not name.','🍞'),(818,49,2,NULL,'He never wrote the recipe down.\n\n\"It is in my hands,\" he said, when Tam asked. \"Hands do not need paper.\"',NULL),(819,49,3,NULL,'This is a lovely thing to say and a very bad way to keep a recipe.',NULL),(820,49,4,NULL,'On the first Sunday in October, Grandad stood in the kitchen with the flour out and the bowl out and the oven on, and he did not start.\n\nHe stood there for eleven minutes. Tam counted, from the doorway, and did not say anything.','🥖'),(821,49,5,NULL,'\"It has gone,\" said Grandad eventually.\n\n\"What has?\"\n\n\"The order of it.\"',NULL),(822,49,6,'The order of it','Grandad could remember the flour. He could remember the water, roughly, and the salt, exactly.\n\nHe could not remember what came third, or how long anything waited, or the thing at the end that made the taste you could not name.',NULL),(823,49,7,NULL,'Tam\'s mother said it was all right and they could buy bread, which was true and was not the point, and which she knew was not the point while she was saying it.',NULL),(824,49,8,NULL,'Grandad turned the oven off and went and sat in the front room with the crossword.\n\nHe did not do the crossword. He held it.','😔'),(825,49,9,'What Tam did','Tam was seven, and had been in that kitchen every Sunday morning of their life, and had therefore watched the bread being made about three hundred and sixty times without once paying attention.\n\nThis is the most annoying fact in this story.',NULL),(826,49,10,NULL,'But watching something three hundred and sixty times leaves a mark, even if you were not trying.\n\nTam got a pencil and the back of an envelope and wrote down every single thing they could remember, in no order at all.',NULL),(827,49,11,NULL,'THE SMELL BEFORE THE OVEN IS ON.\n\nHE TAPS THE BOTTOM AND LISTENS.\n\nTHE BLUE BOWL, NEVER THE WHITE ONE.\n\nHE SAYS \"AGAIN\" TWICE.\n\nTHERE IS A BIT WHERE WE GO AND DO SOMETHING ELSE FOR AGES.','✏️'),(828,49,12,NULL,'That last one turned out to be the most important thing on the envelope, although Tam did not know it yet.',NULL),(829,49,13,'The people who ate it','Then Tam did the clever thing, and it was so simple that no grown-up had thought of it.\n\nFifty years of Sunday bread means fifty years of people eating Sunday bread. Tam went and asked them.',NULL),(830,49,14,NULL,'Mrs Harlow at number nine said it was better than her mother\'s and her mother had been Austrian.\n\nShe also said: \"He always started it the night before. I\'d see the light on at ten.\"','🚪'),(831,49,15,NULL,'The night before. Tam wrote it on the envelope and underlined it.\n\nThat was the *bit where we go and do something else for ages*. It was not an afternoon. It was a whole night.',NULL),(832,49,16,NULL,'The man at the allotments said Grandad used to take a jar up there every August.\n\n\"For the honey,\" he said. \"A spoon of it goes in. He told me that in about 1986 and made me promise not to tell anyone.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"No idea. Bakers are strange.\"',NULL),(833,49,17,NULL,'Honey. That was the taste at the end you could not name.\n\nTam sat down on the allotment path and wrote it down and had to be asked twice whether they were all right.','🍯'),(834,49,18,'The blue bowl','By the third Sunday the envelope was full and Tam had moved onto a proper notebook.\n\nThe hard part was the order. Everyone remembered the bread. Nobody remembered the order, because nobody had been standing in the kitchen.',NULL),(835,49,19,NULL,'Except Grandad\'s hands.',NULL),(836,49,20,NULL,'So on the fourth Sunday, Tam did not ask Grandad to remember anything.\n\nTam put the blue bowl on the table, and the flour and the water and the salt and the honey beside it, and said, \"I do not know what to do first.\"','🥣'),(837,49,21,NULL,'Grandad looked at the bowl for a while.\n\nThen his hands picked up the flour, because hands do not need paper, and they had been waiting four weeks for somebody to get the bowl out.',NULL),(838,49,22,'Writing it down','Tam wrote while he worked. Not the amounts — Grandad has never measured anything in his life — but the order, and the waiting, and the sounds.\n\nFLOUR IN FIRST. WATER WARM, NOT HOT, TEST ON WRIST. SALT AFTER, NEVER WITH.',NULL),(839,49,23,NULL,'HONEY: ONE SPOON, AT THE END OF THE MIXING. HE SAYS \"AGAIN\" HERE.\n\nTHEN IT SITS ALL NIGHT IN THE COLD KITCHEN WITH A PLATE ON TOP.\n\nIN THE MORNING IT HAS DOUBLED AND YOU KNOCK IT DOWN AND HE SAYS \"AGAIN\" THE SECOND TIME.','🕙'),(840,49,24,NULL,'The loaf came out at half past ten on the fifth Sunday.\n\nGrandad tapped the bottom and listened, which is the last step and is not optional, and the sound was right.',NULL),(841,49,25,'After','The recipe is on the inside of the cupboard door now, in Tam\'s handwriting, in pencil, with three crossings-out.\n\nIt is not a good recipe. It says things like \"until it looks like it does\" and \"the blue bowl, never the white one\".','📗'),(842,49,26,NULL,'It is the only copy in the world and it works.',NULL),(843,49,27,NULL,'Some Sundays Grandad needs it and some Sundays he does not, and nobody makes a fuss either way. That was Tam\'s rule and the family has stuck to it.',NULL),(844,49,28,NULL,'And at the bottom, under the bit about tapping and listening, there is one more line that Tam added last, in pen, so it cannot be rubbed out.\n\nASK THE PEOPLE WHO ATE IT. THEY REMEMBER MORE THAN THEY THINK.',NULL),(845,49,29,NULL,'The end.','🍞'),(846,50,1,'How a hive decides','When a honeybee finds flowers, she comes home and dances.\n\nThis is true. It is not a story about dancing bees — bees genuinely do this, and it is one of the strangest facts in the world.','🐝'),(847,50,2,NULL,'The dance is a figure of eight. The angle she waggles at tells the others which way to fly, relative to the sun. The length of the waggle tells them how far.',NULL),(848,50,3,NULL,'A bee can tell six hundred other bees where a flower is, in the dark, inside a box, by walking in a shape.\n\nThat is the hive this story happens in.','8️⃣'),(849,50,4,'Bee Forty-One','Forty-One was a good forager. She was out early and back late and she found things.',NULL),(850,50,5,NULL,'On a Tuesday in June she found a field of borage four hundred metres south-west: a whole field, in flower, and barely a bee on it.\n\nIt was the best find of the summer.',NULL),(851,50,6,NULL,'She came home. She climbed onto the comb where the dancing happens.\n\nAnd she did not dance.','💙'),(852,50,7,'Why not','Because the last time Forty-One had danced, in May, she had got it wrong.',NULL),(853,50,8,NULL,'She had danced an angle that sent forty bees over the ridge to a field that had been cut the day before. They flew nine minutes out and nine minutes back for nothing.',NULL),(854,50,9,NULL,'Bees do not tell each other off. That is not a thing hives do.\n\nBut Forty-One had been in the middle of the comb with forty bees watching her, and she had been wrong, and she had decided — in the way a creature decides — never again.','😔'),(855,50,10,'What the hive lost','For eleven days, Forty-One found things and told nobody.',NULL),(856,50,11,NULL,'She worked the borage herself. One bee on four hundred metres of flowers.\n\nShe brought back her own load, every trip, which is about forty milligrams of nectar.',NULL),(857,50,12,NULL,'A hive working that field properly would have moved several kilograms in those eleven days.\n\nForty-One moved about nine grams. The borage went over. Nobody else ever saw it.','⚖️'),(858,50,13,'The old bee','There was a bee in that hive who was twenty-nine days old, which is ancient, and who had stopped foraging and taken up fanning at the entrance.',NULL),(859,50,14,NULL,'Bees do not give speeches either. But they do follow, and on the twelfth day the old bee followed Forty-One out, all the way to the borage, and all the way back.',NULL),(860,50,15,NULL,'And then the old bee climbed onto the comb and danced it herself.\n\nFour hundred metres. South-west. Badly — she was twenty-nine days old and her angle was sloppy.','👵'),(861,50,16,'Sloppy is enough','Nineteen bees went out on that dance.\n\nFourteen of them found the borage anyway, because a sloppy direction and a big field is a thing bees can work with.',NULL),(862,50,17,NULL,'They came back and danced it better.\n\nBy the afternoon the dance was crisp and two hundred bees were on it.',NULL),(863,50,18,NULL,'That is the thing about the dance that Forty-One had not understood.\n\nIt is not a statement. It is a first guess that gets corrected by everybody who follows it.','🌼'),(864,50,19,'The thirteenth day','Forty-One found a lime tree, east, nine hundred metres, coming into flower.',NULL),(865,50,20,NULL,'She came home. She climbed onto the comb.\n\nAnd she danced it, and she got the distance slightly long, and eleven bees overshot and had to come back.',NULL),(866,50,21,NULL,'Then they danced it right, and by the evening the lime was covered, and the hive took eleven kilograms off that tree over nine days.','🌳'),(867,50,22,'What to take from it','A wrong dance costs the hive eighteen minutes of forty bees\' time.\n\nNot dancing costs the hive an entire field.',NULL),(868,50,23,NULL,'Every bee that dances is wrong sometimes. The hive is built on the assumption. That is why the dance gets corrected instead of trusted.','🍯'),(869,50,24,NULL,'Nobody in a hive is supposed to be right on their own. That is what the other six hundred are for.',NULL),(870,50,25,NULL,'The end.','🐝'),(871,51,1,'Things Tomas counted','Tomas counted things.\n\nHe counted stairs. He counted peas. He counted the number of steps from the gate to the door, which was eleven, and had been eleven his whole life.','☁️'),(872,51,2,NULL,'He counted the cracks in the pavement on Hill Street.\n\nThere were sixty-two. He checked on Mondays in case a new one had arrived in the night.',NULL),(873,51,3,NULL,'He counted the birds on the wire outside his window every morning.\n\nSome mornings there were nine. Some mornings there were none. Once there were twenty-three, and he was late for school, and it was worth it.',NULL),(874,51,4,NULL,'His sister said counting was boring.\n\nTomas did not think it was boring. He thought it was like holding something. You cannot hold a bird. You can hold nine.','🐦'),(875,51,5,'The day he tried the sky','One Tuesday in June, Tomas lay down on the grass behind the shed and looked up.\n\nAnd he thought: I will count the clouds.',NULL),(876,51,6,NULL,'He started at the left, because you have to start somewhere.\n\nOne. Two. Three — a long thin one. Four, five, six, which were all small and close together, like peas.',NULL),(877,51,7,NULL,'Seven was enormous.\n\nIt took up more sky than the shed, the house, and the tree put together, and while Tomas was looking at it, it turned slowly into something that was no longer one cloud at all.','🌤️'),(878,51,8,NULL,'Now it was two clouds.\n\nTomas said, \"That is not allowed.\"\n\nThe cloud did not answer. Clouds very rarely do.',NULL),(879,51,9,NULL,'He started again. One, two, three.\n\nBy the time he reached nine, number four had joined number five, and number two had gone altogether, and a new one had come in over the roof that he had not given a number to at all.',NULL),(880,51,10,NULL,'Tomas sat up. His ears were hot.\n\nThis is a thing that happens when you are five and the world will not hold still: your ears go hot, and then your eyes, and then it is all rather difficult.','😤'),(881,51,11,'Gran on the step','His gran came out with two cups and sat down on the back step, which creaked.\n\n\"You have your counting face on,\" she said.',NULL),(882,51,12,NULL,'\"I am counting the clouds,\" said Tomas, \"and they keep cheating.\"\n\n\"Ah,\" said Gran. \"Yes. They do that.\"',NULL),(883,51,13,NULL,'She handed him a cup. It was too hot, which was correct, because a cup that is too hot gives you something to do with your hands while you are cross.',NULL),(884,51,14,NULL,'\"How many have you got?\"\n\n\"Nine. Then eleven. Then seven. Then I lost count entirely and a new one came in from the Shepherds\' side.\"\n\n\"That is quite a lot of answers for one question,\" said Gran.','🍵'),(885,51,15,'What numbers are for','\"Tomas,\" she said, \"why do you count the birds on the wire?\"\n\nTomas thought about it properly, because Gran asked real questions and expected real answers.\n\n\"So I know how many there are.\"',NULL),(886,51,16,NULL,'\"And then what?\"\n\n\"And then...\" He stopped.\n\nThere was no and then. That had never occurred to him before.',NULL),(887,51,17,NULL,'\"I will tell you what I do,\" said Gran. \"I do not count the birds. I notice the birds. It is a different job.\"\n\n\"Noticing has no number.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Gran. \"That is rather the good part.\"',NULL),(888,51,18,NULL,'Tomas lay back down on the grass. Gran stayed on the step, because getting down to the grass was fine and getting up again was the problem.\n\n\"Notice me one,\" she said.','👀'),(889,51,19,NULL,'\"There is a long thin one that looks like a bus with no wheels.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"And one that is coming apart at the edges like bread.\"\n\n\"Better.\"',NULL),(890,51,20,NULL,'\"And there is one,\" said Tomas slowly, \"that is exactly the shape of our shed, but bigger, and it is going over the Shepherds\' house now, and it will be gone in a minute and nobody else in the whole world is looking at it.\"',NULL),(891,51,21,NULL,'Gran was quiet.\n\n\"Now that,\" she said, \"is worth getting your ears hot about.\"','🏚️'),(892,51,22,'Tuesday, after','Tomas still counts stairs. He still counts peas, and the cracks on Hill Street, which are sixty-three now, because one did arrive in the night and he was right to keep checking.',NULL),(893,51,23,NULL,'But he does not count clouds.\n\nHe has a different job for clouds. He keeps a notebook, and in it he writes down the ones worth noticing, and he does not number the pages.',NULL),(894,51,24,NULL,'Page one says: A BUS WITH NO WHEELS.\n\nPage two says: BREAD, COMING APART.\n\nPage three says: OUR SHED, BUT ENORMOUS, GOING OVER THE SHEPHERDS\'. 11 MINUTES PAST FOUR. ME AND GRAN.','📓'),(895,51,25,NULL,'And at the back, in the space where you would normally put a total, he has written the only sum he thinks is any good.\n\nNOTICING = NO NUMBER.',NULL),(896,51,26,NULL,'The end.','☁️'),(897,52,1,'Orange','A trumpet is orange.\n\nNot the trumpet itself, which is gold. The sound. The sound is orange, and slightly rough at the edges, like the skin of one.','🎺'),(898,52,2,NULL,'Idris Bello had always known this.\n\nA cello is dark green and goes all the way down to the floor. A flute is pale blue and lives near the ceiling. A snare drum is white and has corners.',NULL),(899,52,3,NULL,'He assumed everybody knew. Why would you not? It is the loudest thing about a sound.','🎻'),(900,52,4,'Saying it out loud','In Year Three they listened to a piece of music and Mrs Vale asked what it made them think of.\n\nAll the usual answers came: a storm, a horse, being chased, Christmas.',NULL),(901,52,5,NULL,'Idris said: \"It starts dark green and then it goes orange in the middle and then there is a bit at the end that is the colour of a plum.\"',NULL),(902,52,6,NULL,'The class laughed.\n\nNot nastily. The way a class laughs at something it has not got a shelf for.','😶'),(903,52,7,NULL,'Idris went red, which was the first time he had minded a colour.\n\nHe did not say it again for two years.',NULL),(904,52,8,'Two years','He kept playing. He was good — properly good, the kind of good that makes grown-ups go quiet at the back of a hall.',NULL),(905,52,9,NULL,'But he stopped talking about it, and talking about it had been half the fun, and the half he lost was the half he practised for.',NULL),(906,52,10,NULL,'He told his grandmother eventually, because she asked a direct question and did not let him leave the kitchen.','🎹'),(907,52,11,'What his grandmother said','\"Idris,\" she said, \"do you know how many people in the world hear colours?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Neither do I. But it is not one.\"',NULL),(908,52,12,NULL,'She did not tell him it was a gift, which he was braced for and would have hated.\n\nShe said: \"It has a name. Go and find it.\"',NULL),(909,52,13,NULL,'It took him four library visits.\n\nThe word is synaesthesia. It means two senses that most people keep apart arriving together.','📚'),(910,52,14,NULL,'Some people see colours in letters. Some taste words. Some, like Idris, see sound.\n\nIt is not an illness and it is not magic. It is wiring.',NULL),(911,52,15,'Mr Enright','The music teacher at his new school was called Mr Enright and he was not a remarkable musician.\n\nHe was, however, the sort of person who says \"go on\" instead of \"really?\".',NULL),(912,52,16,NULL,'Idris told him in week three, bracing again.\n\nMr Enright said: \"Go on. What colour is the second clarinet?\"\n\n\"Yellowy. A bit sour.\"\n\n\"She is flat,\" said Mr Enright. \"That is useful. Sit there.\"','🎼'),(913,52,17,'Useful','That is the word that changed it. Not special. Not amazing. Useful.',NULL),(914,52,18,NULL,'Idris can hear a section drifting before the conductor can, because to him it does not drift, it discolours.\n\nHe cannot tune a violin better than anybody else. He can tell you which violin is wrong.',NULL),(915,52,19,NULL,'He is careful about it now, in a way he was not at seven. He knows it is his wiring and not a fact about the world.\n\nWhen he says a chord is brown, he says \"to me\".','👂'),(916,52,20,'After','He is fourteen. He plays cello, which is dark green, which is his favourite colour and was before he could name why.',NULL),(917,52,21,NULL,'There is a boy in Year Three at his old school who said something about a piece of music sounding purple and got laughed at in March.','🟢'),(918,52,22,NULL,'Idris went back and did a workshop, because Mrs Vale asked him to, and he brought the cello, and he said the word out loud to thirty eight-year-olds.',NULL),(919,52,23,NULL,'The boy in Year Three has the word now. That is two years earlier than Idris got it, which was the entire point of going.',NULL),(920,52,24,NULL,'The end.','🎶'),(921,53,1,'Why it looks like that','The Forth Bridge does not look like other bridges. It looks over-built, almost stubborn, as though somebody were making a point.','🌉'),(922,53,2,NULL,'Somebody was. To understand it you have to start with a different bridge, twelve miles away, that fell down.',NULL),(923,53,3,'The Tay Bridge','The Tay Bridge opened in 1878. It was the longest in the world, and it was light, elegant and cheap.',NULL),(924,53,4,NULL,'On the night of the twenty-eighth of December 1879, in a severe gale, thirteen of its high girders collapsed while a train was crossing.','🌧️'),(925,53,5,NULL,'Everyone aboard died. The usual figure given is seventy-five, though the exact number has never been settled.',NULL),(926,53,6,'What the inquiry found','The inquiry concluded that the bridge had been \"badly designed, badly built and badly maintained\".',NULL),(927,53,7,NULL,'Crucially, the designer had made no real allowance for wind pressure on the structure, and the cast-iron lugs holding it together were weak and poorly cast.','📄'),(928,53,8,NULL,'The same designer had already drawn a bridge for the Forth. That design was immediately abandoned.',NULL),(929,53,9,'The brief','Benjamin Baker and John Fowler were given the Forth crossing afterwards, and their brief was not only to build a bridge.',NULL),(930,53,10,NULL,'It was to build a bridge the public would get on a train and cross.','🎯'),(931,53,11,NULL,'That is a design requirement about feelings, and it is the reason for the shape.',NULL),(932,53,12,'The cantilever','A cantilever is a beam supported at one end only. Put two back to back, balanced on a tower, and each arm counterweights the other.',NULL),(933,53,13,NULL,'Three such towers, with short suspended spans dropped into the gaps between their arms, make the Forth Bridge.','⚖️'),(934,53,14,NULL,'The advantage is that it can be built outwards from each tower with no scaffolding in the water, staying balanced at every stage.',NULL),(935,53,15,'The famous photograph','Baker demonstrated the principle with three men, four chairs and a pile of bricks, and the photograph is in engineering textbooks to this day.',NULL),(936,53,16,NULL,'Two men sit on chairs with their arms outstretched, holding sticks. A third man sits on a plank slung between the sticks. Bricks on the outer chairs are the counterweights.','🪑'),(937,53,17,NULL,'The sitting men are the towers. Their arms are the cantilevers. The man in the middle is the train.',NULL),(938,53,18,'Wind','After the Tay, wind was the whole question. Baker had test gauges erected on the estuary and measured the pressure for years before building.',NULL),(939,53,19,NULL,'The bridge was designed for a wind pressure of fifty-six pounds per square foot — far above anything recorded, and roughly ten times what the Tay Bridge designer had allowed.','💨'),(940,53,20,NULL,'That single decision is most of why it looks so heavy.',NULL),(941,53,21,'The cost in people','Around four thousand six hundred men worked on it. The official death toll was long given as fifty-seven.',NULL),(942,53,22,NULL,'Research published for the bridge\'s 125th anniversary identified seventy-three named men, and memorials were finally raised in 2012 — a hundred and twenty-two years after it opened.','🪦'),(943,53,23,NULL,'The names were not lost because nobody cared at the time. They were lost because nobody wrote them in one place.',NULL),(944,53,24,'What it is made of','Fifty-four thousand tonnes of steel, and around six and a half million rivets, every one placed by hand.',NULL),(945,53,25,NULL,'It was among the first major structures in Britain built of steel rather than wrought or cast iron, which was only possible because the Bessemer and open-hearth processes had made steel affordable a few decades earlier.','🔩'),(946,53,26,'After','It opened in March 1890 and carries trains today, a hundred and thirty-six years later.',NULL),(947,53,27,NULL,'The saying about \"painting the Forth Bridge\" — that you finish at one end and start again at the other — was never quite true, and is now definitely not: a coating applied between 2002 and 2011 is expected to last around twenty-five years.',NULL),(948,53,28,NULL,'It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. It is also, read correctly, an apology written in steel for a bridge that fell down twelve miles away.','🖌️'),(949,53,29,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(950,54,1,'On his own','Pim was six and he was making a cake for his sister entirely on his own.\n\nThis had been agreed. There were conditions. The oven was not one of the things he was allowed to touch.','🎂'),(951,54,2,NULL,'His father would do the oven. His father would do nothing else, and had promised, and was finding it extremely difficult.',NULL),(952,54,3,'Disaster one: the eggs','Pim cracked four eggs into a bowl. Three of them went well.',NULL),(953,54,4,NULL,'The fourth went onto the floor, which was fine, and then into the dog, which was less fine, and the dog had to go outside for a while.','🥚'),(954,54,5,NULL,'Pim got another egg. Nobody said anything about the floor. That had been agreed too.',NULL),(955,54,6,'Disaster two: the flour','The recipe said two hundred grams and the scales said grams, so that bit was straightforward.',NULL),(956,54,7,NULL,'What was not straightforward was tipping a bag of flour into a bowl with one hand while holding the recipe with the other.',NULL),(957,54,8,NULL,'There was flour on the counter, the chair, the window, and Pim.\n\nThere was also flour in the bowl, which is the only bit that counted, and there was almost exactly two hundred grams of it.','☁️'),(958,54,9,'Disaster three: the mixing','Pim mixed for eleven minutes, which is about nine minutes too long.',NULL),(959,54,10,NULL,'A cake mixed too long goes tough, because something called gluten gets organised, and organised gluten makes bread instead of cake.',NULL),(960,54,11,NULL,'His father knew this and did not say it, which was the hardest thing that happened to his father all day.','🥣'),(961,54,12,'In the oven','It went in at eleven. It came out at half past, slightly flat, slightly tough, and entirely a cake.',NULL),(962,54,13,NULL,'Pim looked at it for a long time.\n\n\"It is flat,\" he said.\n\n\"It is,\" said his father.','🍰'),(963,54,14,'Disaster four: the icing','Icing is sugar and water and almost nothing else, and the ratio matters more than anything in the whole recipe.',NULL),(964,54,15,NULL,'Pim put in too much water. So he put in more sugar. So it was too much again. So he put in more water.',NULL),(965,54,16,NULL,'By four o\'clock he had enough icing for nine cakes and a bowl that would not fit in the fridge.','🥄'),(966,54,17,'Six o\'clock','His sister came home at six. She was eleven and had had a bad week, which was the entire reason for the cake.',NULL),(967,54,18,NULL,'The cake was flat and tough and had enough icing on it to hold a spoon upright.',NULL),(968,54,19,NULL,'She ate two slices. She said it was the best cake she had ever had, which was a lie, and which everybody in the room including Pim knew was a lie, and which was exactly the right thing to say.','🥄'),(969,54,20,'After','Pim has made eleven cakes now. They are getting better, mostly because he mixes for two minutes instead of eleven.',NULL),(970,54,21,NULL,'He has a notebook with WHAT WENT WRONG at the top of each page, which was his own idea.\n\nCake one: flat, tough, too much icing, one egg in the dog.','📒'),(971,54,22,NULL,'Cake eleven: good. Slightly burnt on one side. Oven is hotter at the back, remember next time.',NULL),(972,54,23,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(973,55,1,'The expedition','The Thwaite Expedition had nine members, eleven weeks of supplies, four hundred miles to cross, and one map.\n\nIt also had a cat, which nobody had agreed to.','🗺️'),(974,55,2,NULL,'The cat was called Biscuit. She had come aboard at the harbour, assessed the situation, and stayed.',NULL),(975,55,3,NULL,'On the first morning Captain Ada Rennick spread the map on the table to plan the route.\n\nBiscuit sat on it. In the middle. On the mountains.','🐈'),(976,55,4,'Attempt one','They moved the cat. The cat came back.\n\nThey moved the cat and held her. The cat waited, with enormous patience, and came back the moment she was put down.',NULL),(977,55,5,NULL,'\"She likes the paper,\" said the cook.\n\n\"She likes the attention,\" said the navigator.\n\n\"She likes being in the way,\" said Captain Rennick, \"and she is extremely good at it.\"',NULL),(978,55,6,NULL,'They tried a decoy map. Biscuit looked at it, looked at them, and went and sat on the real one.\n\nThis went on for six days.','📄'),(979,55,7,'Attempt two','On day seven the navigator, whose name was Idris and who was nineteen and clever, had an idea.\n\n\"We copy it,\" he said. \"We make four copies. She can only sit on one.\"',NULL),(980,55,8,NULL,'It took him two days to copy the map four times by hand.\n\nBiscuit sat on the original. The expedition used copy number two, and got lost on the eleventh day, because Idris had copied a river onto the wrong side of a hill.',NULL),(981,55,9,NULL,'They lost four days. The cook said several things. The cat was blamed for all of them, which was not entirely fair.','🏞️'),(982,55,10,'Attempt three','By week five they had stopped trying to move her and started working around her.\n\nThey read the map in quarters. Whoever needed the north-west corner waited until she had settled on the south-east.',NULL),(983,55,11,NULL,'This was slower and it worked, and Captain Rennick noted in the log that the expedition was now being run at the convenience of a cat.',NULL),(984,55,12,NULL,'She wrote it as a complaint. It reads, now, as a description.','📔'),(985,55,13,'Week nine','On the sixty-first day they came to a river crossing marked on the map as a ford.\n\nIt was not a ford. It was a gorge, forty metres deep, and the map was forty years old.',NULL),(986,55,14,NULL,'They stood on the edge of it for some time.',NULL),(987,55,15,NULL,'Then Idris said a thing nobody wanted to hear, which was that the map was wrong, and that if it was wrong here it could be wrong anywhere, and that they had three weeks of food and four hundred miles of trust in a piece of paper.','🌉'),(988,55,16,'What the cat had been sitting on','Captain Rennick went and got the original map — the one Biscuit had been sitting on for sixty-one days — and laid it next to the copy.',NULL),(989,55,17,NULL,'Biscuit sat on it immediately.\n\nAnd Captain Rennick, instead of moving her, looked at where she sat.',NULL),(990,55,18,NULL,'Sixty-one days. Always the same part of the map. Not the middle of the paper — the middle of the *mountains*, where the paper was thickest with printing and had been folded and refolded until it was soft.','🐾'),(991,55,19,NULL,'Soft, and warm, because that fold sat directly over the stove pipe where the table met the wall.\n\nThe cat had not been choosing the mountains. She had been choosing the warm bit. She had been choosing the warm bit for sixty-one days.',NULL),(992,55,20,'Not a sign','This is the part where, in some books, the cat turns out to be guiding them.\n\nShe was not. She was a cat, sitting on a warm fold, and Idris had copied a river wrong entirely on his own.',NULL),(993,55,21,NULL,'But the fold had worn through where she sat, and under sixty-one days of cat there was a soft grey hole in the paper exactly where the gorge should have been marked.','😼'),(994,55,22,NULL,'The map had not been wrong. The map had worn out, in the one place they could not see, under an animal nobody had agreed to bring.',NULL),(995,55,23,'After','They went round. It added nine days and they made it with four days of food left.',NULL),(996,55,24,NULL,'Captain Rennick\'s final log entry on the subject is one line.\n\nCHECK THE MAP UNDER THE CAT. CHECK EVERYTHING UNDER EVERYTHING.',NULL),(997,55,25,NULL,'The end.','🐈'),(998,56,1,'Four minutes a day','The clock on the tower at Ravenshold has run fast by four minutes a day since 1836.\n\nFour minutes a day is twenty-eight minutes a week. The town puts it right every Sunday and has done for ninety years.','🕰️'),(999,56,2,NULL,'Ten clockmakers have tried to cure it. Each one made it worse, and each one, on being dismissed, said the same thing: there is nothing wrong with that clock.',NULL),(1000,56,3,NULL,'Lira Vance was the eleventh. She was fourteen and an apprentice, and she was only given the job because nobody else would take it.','⚙️'),(1001,56,4,'What a clock is','Her master, old Bartholomew Nibbs, made her say it back before she was allowed up the tower.',NULL),(1002,56,5,NULL,'A weight falls. The falling turns a wheel. The wheel would spin away in seconds, so an escapement lets it move one tooth at a time.\n\nThe thing that decides how often is the pendulum. Everything else is plumbing.',NULL),(1003,56,6,NULL,'\"So if a clock runs fast,\" said Nibbs, \"what is wrong?\"\n\n\"The pendulum is too short.\"\n\n\"Good. Now go and find out why ten clever people could not shorten a pendulum correctly.\"','🔗'),(1004,56,7,'The ten repairs','Lira did something the ten had not. She read the records before touching anything.',NULL),(1005,56,8,NULL,'All ten had lengthened the pendulum, which is correct — a longer pendulum swings slower. Every one of them had got it slower for about a month and then fast again.',NULL),(1006,56,9,NULL,'A month. Every time. Ten times over ninety years.','📒'),(1007,56,10,'Up the tower','The clock room is nine metres up a ladder and smells of oil and pigeon.',NULL),(1008,56,11,NULL,'Lira spent four days there before she touched a tool, which Nibbs had told her to do and which she would not have had the patience for otherwise.',NULL),(1009,56,12,NULL,'She measured the pendulum: one metre ninety-four. She measured the room. She measured, because she was bored, the temperature, morning and evening, for all four days.','🪜'),(1010,56,13,'The thing in the numbers','The clock room in April went from six degrees at dawn to twenty-four by afternoon.\n\nEighteen degrees of swing, every single day, in a stone box with a south-facing window.',NULL),(1011,56,14,NULL,'And a pendulum rod made of iron gets longer when it is warm.',NULL),(1012,56,15,NULL,'Longer rod, slower clock. Shorter rod, faster clock. Iron moves about twelve parts in a million for each degree.','🌡️'),(1013,56,16,NULL,'Over one metre ninety-four and eighteen degrees, that is about four tenths of a millimetre. It does not sound like anything. It is four minutes a day.',NULL),(1014,56,17,'Why the repairs lasted a month','Every clockmaker had come in a different month and set the pendulum perfectly for that month\'s temperature.',NULL),(1015,56,18,NULL,'Then the season turned and the rod changed length and the clock drifted off again, and the clockmaker was blamed, and said — correctly — that there was nothing wrong with the clock.','📅'),(1016,56,19,NULL,'Ten people had each been right about the thing they were measuring and wrong about what to measure.',NULL),(1017,56,20,'The cure','Lira did not lengthen the pendulum. She replaced the rod.',NULL),(1018,56,21,NULL,'A gridiron pendulum uses two metals that expand by different amounts, arranged so that one pushes up as the other pushes down, and the length that matters stays the same.','🔩'),(1019,56,22,NULL,'It was invented in 1726 by a man called John Harrison. It was a hundred and ten years old when the Ravenshold clock was built.',NULL),(1020,56,23,'Why nobody had done it','Because it is expensive, and because it means admitting that the clock was built wrong in 1836 and not broken afterwards.',NULL),(1021,56,24,NULL,'The town council argued about that for two meetings. Lira attended both and said almost nothing, having been told by Nibbs that a fourteen-year-old who has already won should keep quiet and let them get there.','💰'),(1022,56,25,'After','The clock gains about nine seconds a week now, which Lira describes as acceptable and Nibbs describes as sloppy.',NULL),(1023,56,26,NULL,'There is a brass plate in the clock room listing all eleven clockmakers.\n\nThe first ten are not described as failures. Lira wrote the text and insisted.','🗼'),(1024,56,27,NULL,'It says: TEN PEOPLE MEASURED THE PENDULUM. THE ELEVENTH MEASURED THE ROOM.',NULL),(1025,56,28,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1026,5,1,'The ticking','The apples in the orchard at Nether Crow tick.','🍎'),(1027,5,2,NULL,'Not all of them. About one in eleven, and only after the middle of August, and only if you hold one close to your ear and stand very still.',NULL),(1028,5,3,NULL,'The gardener was called Bartholomew Nibbs and he had not slept properly in four years, which is how long the ticking had been going on.','🕰️'),(1029,5,4,'What he had tried','He had cut one open. There is nothing inside an apple but apple.',NULL),(1030,5,5,NULL,'He had cut open forty. He had cut open a hundred and nine by the fourth summer, and the only thing he had proved was that he could ruin a hundred and nine apples.',NULL),(1031,5,6,NULL,'He had dug round the roots. He had tested the water. He had written to a university and received a polite letter about pests.','🔪'),(1032,5,7,'Esme','Esme Hart was nine and came to pick in September, like everybody else in the village.',NULL),(1033,5,8,NULL,'She heard it on her second morning and asked the question nobody had asked, which was not *what is ticking* but *which ones*.',NULL),(1034,5,9,NULL,'Nibbs had never written down which apples ticked. Four years, a hundred and nine apples cut open, and no list.','❓'),(1035,5,10,'The list','They made one. It took eleven days and most of the picking season.',NULL),(1036,5,11,NULL,'Every ticking apple: which tree, which branch, which side of the tree, how high, and the time of day it was heard.',NULL),(1037,5,12,NULL,'Two hundred and six ticking apples, out of about two thousand two hundred.','📋'),(1038,5,13,'Where they were','Not spread about. Esme spotted it when she drew the orchard as a grid and shaded in the trees.',NULL),(1039,5,14,NULL,'The ticking apples were on the south-west side of eleven trees, all in a line across the orchard, and not on any of the other forty trees at all.',NULL),(1040,5,15,NULL,'Eleven trees. In a line. Running from the wall at the top of the slope down towards the beck.','📐'),(1041,5,16,'Under the line','Nibbs knew what was under that line the moment he saw the drawing, and he sat down on an upturned crate.',NULL),(1042,5,17,NULL,'The old mill leat — a channel cut to carry water to a mill wheel — had run exactly there until the mill went in 1911. It was filled in and planted over.',NULL),(1043,5,18,NULL,'Filled in with rubble. And the rubble, it turns out, included the mill\'s own scrap: iron, brass, and the gearing from the wheel.','💧'),(1044,5,19,'What the ticking was','Roots. Apple roots, growing down into loose rubble, finding metal, and growing around it.',NULL),(1045,5,20,NULL,'And metal in wet rubble expands and contracts as the ground warms and cools through the day, and it clicks against stone, and a tree is an excellent conductor of a small noise.',NULL),(1046,5,21,NULL,'The ticking was not in the apples. It was in the trunk, and the apple was simply the thing you happened to be holding against your ear.','🌳'),(1047,5,22,'The robin','There is a robin in this story and it is the reason Nibbs believed the explanation.',NULL),(1048,5,23,NULL,'A robin had nested in the same hole in the eleventh tree for four years, which is unusual, and had raised broods there in every one.',NULL),(1049,5,24,NULL,'Robins avoid noise. Nibbs had assumed the bird was deaf, or stupid, or stubborn.','🐦'),(1050,5,25,NULL,'It was none of those. The ticking is almost entirely below the frequency a robin bothers about, and the hole is on the north-east side, where the apples do not tick at all.',NULL),(1051,5,26,'After','Nobody dug the leat out. Eleven mature apple trees are worth more than a mystery.',NULL),(1052,5,27,NULL,'Nibbs sleeps. He says the ticking does not keep you awake once you know what it is, which Esme thinks is the whole moral and which he denies having said.','😴'),(1053,5,28,NULL,'There is a sign at the gate now, for pickers.\n\nSOME OF THESE TICK. IT IS THE OLD MILL LEAT. HOLD ONE TO YOUR EAR ON THE SOUTH-WEST SIDE.',NULL),(1054,5,29,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1055,13,1,'Trixie was always late','Trixie was a triceratops and she was late for everything.','🦕'),(1056,13,2,NULL,'Late for the water hole. Late for the fern patch. Late, once, for an entire season, which takes some doing.',NULL),(1057,13,3,NULL,'It was not that she was slow. Triceratops are not slow. It was that she kept stopping.','🌿'),(1058,13,4,'Why she stopped','She stopped because things happened on the way, and she did not think you were allowed to walk past them.',NULL),(1059,13,5,NULL,'A small dinosaur stuck in mud. A nest that had rolled. A hadrosaur who had got something in his foot and was being extremely brave about it, badly.',NULL),(1060,13,6,NULL,'Everybody else stepped round. Trixie stopped.','🦶'),(1061,13,7,'The message','One morning a message came along the valley, passed from herd to herd the way messages were.',NULL),(1062,13,8,NULL,'SOMETHING IS COMING. GO WEST. GO NOW.',NULL),(1063,13,9,NULL,'Nobody knew what. Everybody went.','📢'),(1064,13,10,'The hadrosaur again','Four hundred metres into going west, Trixie passed the hadrosaur with the foot, who was sitting down.',NULL),(1065,13,11,NULL,'\"Go on,\" he said. \"I will catch up.\"\n\nHe was not going to catch up. He could not stand.',NULL),(1066,13,12,NULL,'Trixie looked west, where everybody was going, and then at the hadrosaur, who was being brave about it, badly.','😟'),(1067,13,13,'What she did','She got the thing out of his foot, which took a long time, because a triceratops has three horns and no fingers at all.',NULL),(1068,13,14,NULL,'It took until the middle of the afternoon. Then she walked with him, at his speed, which was not much of a speed.',NULL),(1069,13,15,NULL,'They were four hours behind everybody else and going about half as fast.','🐾'),(1070,13,16,'The thing that was coming','It came in the evening and it was very bright and it was a long way to the south.',NULL),(1071,13,17,NULL,'Trixie and the hadrosaur were in a river valley with a cliff on the south side, because that is where you end up if you walk slowly along a riverbed instead of fast across open ground.',NULL),(1072,13,18,NULL,'The cliff was between them and the brightness.','☄️'),(1073,13,19,'Everybody else','Everybody else was four hours west, on the open plain, where the going is good and you can see for miles.',NULL),(1074,13,20,NULL,'There is nothing to hide behind on an open plain.',NULL),(1075,13,21,'After','This is the part where a book would normally say that being kind is always rewarded, and that is not true and Trixie would not say it.',NULL),(1076,13,22,NULL,'She was not saved because she was kind. She was saved because a river valley has a cliff in it, which is luck, and which had nothing to do with her at all.','🌄'),(1077,13,23,NULL,'But she was in the valley because she had stopped. And she had stopped because she did not think you were allowed to walk past.',NULL),(1078,13,24,NULL,'That is not a reward. It is just what happened. Trixie has always been careful about the difference.','🦴'),(1079,13,25,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1080,57,1,'A perfectly good dragon','Ember was a perfectly good dragon.\n\nShe could fly. She could land on a tower without knocking anything off. She could carry a cow, although she had never wanted to.','🐉'),(1081,57,2,NULL,'She could not breathe fire, because she was frightened of it.',NULL),(1082,57,3,NULL,'Not frightened of being burned. Dragons cannot be burned.\n\nFrightened of the moment. The whoomph. The sudden enormous bright thing arriving out of her own face with no warning.','🔥'),(1083,57,4,'Saturday','Every year on the first Saturday in November the kingdom lights a bonfire the size of a barn, and a dragon lights it, and everybody cheers.',NULL),(1084,57,5,NULL,'Ember was the only dragon for four hundred miles.',NULL),(1085,57,6,NULL,'She had got out of it for three years running. One year she had a cold. One year she said she was moulting, which dragons do not do. One year she simply flew to the coast and stayed there until Monday.','🪵'),(1086,57,7,'The fourth year','In the fourth year a girl called Wren climbed all the way up to the cave to ask her directly, which nobody had done.',NULL),(1087,57,8,NULL,'\"Everybody says you are being difficult,\" said Wren.\n\n\"I am being frightened,\" said Ember. \"It looks the same from outside.\"',NULL),(1088,57,9,NULL,'Wren sat down on a rock, which surprised Ember, who was used to people leaving at about this point.','🧒'),(1089,57,10,'What frightened her exactly','Wren asked the useful question, which is not *why are you scared* but *what exactly*.',NULL),(1090,57,11,NULL,'Ember had to think about it for a while, because nobody had ever made her be specific.',NULL),(1091,57,12,NULL,'\"It is the not knowing when,\" she said at last. \"It builds up in my chest and then it goes off. I cannot tell it when to go. It tells me.\"','💭'),(1092,57,13,'The practice fire','Wren came back on the Tuesday with a bucket of sand, a long stick, and her father, who was a blacksmith and unbothered by most things.',NULL),(1093,57,14,NULL,'They did not ask Ember to light a bonfire.\n\nThey asked her to light a candle.',NULL),(1094,57,15,NULL,'One candle, on a rock, nine metres away, with the whole mouth of the cave open behind it.','🕯️'),(1095,57,16,NULL,'It took her forty minutes to try and she missed, twice, which everybody agreed was fine.',NULL),(1096,57,17,'Counting','The blacksmith worked out the useful part, because he had spent thirty years with a forge and knew about things that go off when they are ready.',NULL),(1097,57,18,NULL,'\"It builds,\" he said. \"You can feel it building. So count it.\"','🔨'),(1098,57,19,NULL,'Ember learned to count the build. Four seconds, usually. Sometimes five.\n\nBy the third week she could say \"three — two — one\" out loud and have it arrive on one.',NULL),(1099,57,20,'Saturday, again','She lit the bonfire from thirty metres with everybody watching.',NULL),(1100,57,21,NULL,'She counted out loud, which nobody had told her she was allowed to do, and which about four hundred people heard.','🎆'),(1101,57,22,NULL,'\"THREE. TWO. ONE.\"\n\nThe bonfire went up. Everybody cheered. Several small children started counting down to things for weeks afterwards, to the irritation of their parents.',NULL),(1102,57,23,'After','Ember still counts. She will always count. It is not a thing she grew out of and nobody has suggested she should.',NULL),(1103,57,24,NULL,'There is a second dragon in the kingdom now, a young one, who arrived last spring and who cannot light anything at all.','🐲'),(1104,57,25,NULL,'Ember has a bucket of sand, a long stick, and a candle on a rock nine metres from the mouth of the cave.\n\nShe is in no hurry whatsoever.',NULL),(1105,57,26,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1106,58,1,'The same bend','Bea Pike drew the river at Cold Beck every Saturday for a year.\n\nThe same bend. The same spot on the bank. Fifty-two drawings, in pencil, on paper cut to the same size.','🎨'),(1107,58,2,NULL,'She did it because her art teacher had told the class to draw one thing until they stopped seeing what they expected and started seeing what was there.',NULL),(1108,58,3,NULL,'Everybody else did it for three weeks. Bea did it for fifty-two, because she is the sort of person who finishes things, which is sometimes a gift and sometimes a nuisance.','✏️'),(1109,58,4,'What changes in a year','Week one: high water, brown, fast.\n\nWeek nine: low, clear, with a gravel bar showing.\n\nWeek twenty-two: the willow on the far side came down in a gale and she drew it lying in the water.',NULL),(1110,58,5,NULL,'Week thirty: the gravel bar had moved four metres downstream, which she only knew because she had drawn the same fence post in every single picture.',NULL),(1111,58,6,NULL,'That fence post is the reason this story exists.\n\nShe had put it in out of habit. It gave every drawing the same ruler.','🪵'),(1112,58,7,'Week forty-one','In February the water went white.\n\nNot foamy. White, like milk in tea, and it stayed that way for three weeks.',NULL),(1113,58,8,NULL,'Bea drew it. She wrote WATER IS WHITE in the corner because she did not have a white pencil and could not show it any other way.',NULL),(1114,58,9,NULL,'She told her father. Her father said rivers do that sometimes, after rain, from clay.','🥛'),(1115,58,10,NULL,'It had not rained for eleven days. She had written the weather in the corner of every drawing too, because the teacher had said to, and she had not once skipped it.',NULL),(1116,58,11,'The dead stretch','By week forty-four the water was clear again and something else was wrong.',NULL),(1117,58,12,NULL,'There is a drawing from week twelve with eleven small fish in the shallow on the near bank. Bea had counted them and written 11.\n\nIn week forty-four she sat in the same place for forty minutes and wrote 0.','🐟'),(1118,58,13,NULL,'She kept writing 0 for six weeks.',NULL),(1119,58,14,'Nobody to tell, again','She told her teacher, who was sympathetic and had no idea what to do about it.\n\nShe rang the environment line and was asked for a date and a time, which she had, and a photograph, which she did not.',NULL),(1120,58,15,NULL,'\"I have drawings,\" said Bea.\n\nThe man on the phone was kind in the way adults are kind when they are about to stop listening.','📞'),(1121,58,16,'What made them listen','Her grandmother took the folder to the river trust office in person and put fifty-two drawings on the table in order.',NULL),(1122,58,17,NULL,'Not as art. As a series.\n\nSame place. Same fence post. Date and weather on every one. Fish counts on eleven of them. Water colour described on all fifty-two.',NULL),(1123,58,18,NULL,'The hydrologist, a woman called Juno Okonkwo, looked at it for a long time and then said the sentence Bea has repeated ever since.\n\n\"This is a dataset.\"','📂'),(1124,58,19,'What it showed','Three things that no single visit could have shown.',NULL),(1125,58,20,NULL,'The white water began in week forty-one, not after rain.\n\nThe fish disappeared three weeks after the white water started, not at the same time.\n\nAnd the gravel bar had moved steadily all year, which meant something upstream had changed how much sediment the river carried.',NULL),(1126,58,21,NULL,'A single photograph shows a white river. Fifty-two drawings show a sequence, and a sequence is the only thing that can tell you which came first.','🔬'),(1127,58,22,'What it was','A settling pond at a quarry two miles upstream had been overtopping since January. Fine rock dust, not poison.',NULL),(1128,58,23,NULL,'Rock dust clogs the gravel where fish lay eggs and where the insects they eat live. The fish did not die. They left, which is why nobody found anything to report.',NULL),(1129,58,24,NULL,'The pond was fixed in the May. The insects came back in the autumn. Bea drew eleven small fish in the shallow on the near bank in the following March and wrote 11 in the corner.','⛏️'),(1130,58,25,'After','Bea is still drawing it. She is on year four. Two hundred and nine drawings.',NULL),(1131,58,26,NULL,'Eleven of them hang in the river trust office, which she thinks is funny, because they are not her best ones — they are the useful ones, and nobody in that office can tell the difference.','🖼️'),(1132,58,27,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1133,59,1,'Eleven days of nearly','The forecast had said rain for eleven days.\n\nIt had rained on none of them. The water butt was empty, the beans were going over, and everybody in Thwaite had stopped believing the weather entirely.','⛈️'),(1134,59,2,NULL,'Wren Alabi did not stop believing it. She started checking it.\n\nThere is a difference, and it is most of this book.',NULL),(1135,59,3,'What she had','A rain gauge made from a lemonade bottle with the top cut off and turned upside down. A thermometer from the greenhouse. A barometer her grandfather had been given in 1974 and had never once looked at.',NULL),(1136,59,4,NULL,'And a notebook, which is the instrument that matters.','🧪'),(1137,59,5,NULL,'Every morning at eight: temperature, pressure, wind direction, cloud, rainfall.\n\nEvery evening at eight: the same five again.\n\nTwenty-two days before she had enough to say anything at all, which is the part people skip.',NULL),(1138,59,6,'The thing in the numbers','Here is what Wren noticed on day twenty-three.\n\nThe pressure dropped before rain was forecast. That was expected. But on nine of the eleven failed days, the pressure had dropped and then come straight back up.',NULL),(1139,59,7,NULL,'Down, then up, within six hours. Nine times out of eleven.\n\nThe forecast had seen the down. It had not waited for the up.','📉'),(1140,59,8,NULL,'Wren did not know what caused it. She was nine. She wrote down what she could measure and left the why alone, which is more discipline than most grown-ups manage.',NULL),(1141,59,9,'Nobody to tell','The weather does not have an office in Thwaite. Wren wrote to the one in the city, and got a leaflet about careers in meteorology, which was not the question.',NULL),(1142,59,10,NULL,'So she did the other thing, which was slower and better.\n\nShe made her own forecast, for the village, on a card in the shop window, and she signed it.','✉️'),(1143,59,11,NULL,'TOMORROW: NO RAIN, WHATEVER THE RADIO SAYS. — W. ALABI, AGED 9.\n\nIt did not rain.',NULL),(1144,59,12,'Four weeks of cards','She was right nineteen times out of twenty-eight, which is not brilliant.\n\nThe radio was right fourteen times out of twenty-eight, which is worse, and that is the only reason anybody started reading the card.',NULL),(1145,59,13,NULL,'Mr Harlow read it before deciding whether to cut. Mrs Vale read it before hanging washing. Ada Rennick read it and said nothing, which from Ada Rennick is a review.','🪧'),(1146,59,14,NULL,'On the ninth card Wren wrote: RAIN BY FOUR. TAKE THE WASHING IN.\n\nIt rained at ten past four, and the village behaved as though she had arranged it personally, which she found annoying.',NULL),(1147,59,15,'Being wrong in public','Card fourteen said NO RAIN and it rained all day.\n\nWren wanted to take the card down. Her grandfather would not let her.',NULL),(1148,59,16,NULL,'\"Leave it up,\" he said. \"A forecast you take down when it is wrong is not a forecast. It is an advertisement.\"','🌧️'),(1149,59,17,NULL,'She left it up. She wrote WRONG across it in red, and underneath, the reason: PRESSURE FELL AND STAYED DOWN. I ASSUMED IT WOULD COME BACK. IT DID NOT.',NULL),(1150,59,18,NULL,'Nobody stopped reading the cards. Several people said it was the first time they had trusted a forecast in their lives.',NULL),(1151,59,19,'The valley','The why arrived two years later, from a university student doing a project on microclimates, who had found Wren\'s cards in the parish newsletter.',NULL),(1152,59,20,NULL,'Thwaite sits in the lee of a long ridge. Air coming off the sea is pushed up, drops its water on the far side, and arrives dry.\n\nThe pressure falls. The rain lands four miles away, on somebody else\'s beans.','⛰️'),(1153,59,21,NULL,'The regional forecast is made for the region. The region is ninety miles wide. Thwaite is one valley in it.\n\nNobody was lying to the village. Nobody was looking at the village.',NULL),(1154,59,22,'After','There is a proper rain gauge on the parish hall roof now, and it reports to a national network, and the number it sends in is used by people Wren will never meet.',NULL),(1155,59,23,NULL,'She still writes a card. She is fourteen. She is right about twenty-four times out of twenty-eight, and she still writes WRONG in red across the other four, with the reason underneath.','📊'),(1156,59,24,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1157,3,1,'Puddles','Nell Rennick started mapping puddles because she was bored, which is the beginning of a surprising amount of science.','☔'),(1158,3,2,NULL,'Every time it rained she walked the same route — eleven streets — and marked where the water stood.',NULL),(1159,3,3,NULL,'She used squared paper, one square to five metres, and a different colour for each month.','🗺️'),(1160,3,4,'What she expected','She expected puddles in the low bits. That is where water goes.',NULL),(1161,3,5,NULL,'And mostly that is what she got: the dip outside the chemist, the corner by the bus stop, the bad bit of pavement on Fen Street.',NULL),(1162,3,6,'What she did not expect','After four months of maps laid on top of each other, there was a line.',NULL),(1163,3,7,NULL,'Not a dip. A line. Puddles that formed in a narrow band running from the top of Hill Street, across the car park, under the row of shops, and out into the field behind the chapel.','📈'),(1164,3,8,NULL,'Through flat ground. Through places that were not low. In a curve.',NULL),(1165,3,9,'Water does not curve for nothing','Nell showed the maps to her grandfather, who had been a plumber for forty years and who looked at them for a long time.',NULL),(1166,3,10,NULL,'\"That is a watercourse,\" he said.\n\n\"There is no river there.\"\n\n\"There is no river there *now*.\"','🔧'),(1167,3,11,'Culverted','A culvert is a stream that has been put in a pipe and buried, usually so that something can be built on top of it.',NULL),(1168,3,12,NULL,'Towns are full of them. Most people live within a few hundred metres of a buried stream and have no idea.',NULL),(1169,3,13,NULL,'The ground above a culvert settles differently. The backfill is looser than the soil around it. Water finds it.','🧱'),(1170,3,14,NULL,'Nell had not found puddles. She had found the line of an old stream, drawn in puddles, four months at a time.',NULL),(1171,3,15,'Finding the name','The archive had a tithe map from 1841 and it took three visits to be allowed to see it.',NULL),(1172,3,16,NULL,'On it, running from the top of Hill Street, across what was then two fields, past a mill, and out behind the chapel, was a stream.','📜'),(1173,3,17,NULL,'It was named. It was called Thorn Beck.',NULL),(1174,3,18,'Nobody remembered','Nell asked forty-one people in the town whether they had heard of Thorn Beck.',NULL),(1175,3,19,NULL,'Two had. One was ninety-four. The other was the man at the water company, who had it on a drawing and had never once said the name out loud.',NULL),(1176,3,20,NULL,'A stream had run through the middle of that town for several thousand years and had been out of sight for about ninety, and in ninety years it had almost entirely left people\'s heads.','🗣️'),(1177,3,21,'Why it matters','Because culverts block, and when a culverted stream blocks it does not politely stop. It comes up.',NULL),(1178,3,22,NULL,'The flooding in the car park in 2019, which everybody had blamed on drains, was Thorn Beck backing up at a collapsed section under the shops.','💧'),(1179,3,23,NULL,'Nell\'s maps, four months of bored squared paper, show the route of it more clearly than the water company\'s own drawing, which stops at the boundary of their land.',NULL),(1180,3,24,'After','There is a line of eleven brass studs set into the pavement now, following the course of Thorn Beck through the town centre.',NULL),(1181,3,25,NULL,'It was a town council project and it cost four thousand pounds and Nell thinks it is the best money they have ever spent.','🔘'),(1182,3,26,NULL,'Her maps are in the library, in a folder, under local history, filed between a pamphlet about the chapel and a book about buses.',NULL),(1183,3,27,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1184,60,1,'The end of the garden','Immy Doyle lived in a house with a railway at the bottom of the garden.\n\nMost people would call that noisy. Immy called it company.','🚂'),(1185,60,2,NULL,'Eleven trains a day went past the fence. She knew all of them by the sound they made before she could see them.\n\nThe 6:02 grumbled. The 9:40 sang. The 4:15 on a Friday always sounded like it was in a hurry to be somewhere better.',NULL),(1186,60,3,NULL,'And they talked.\n\nNot in words, exactly. In the way a kettle talks, or a door, or a dog who wants something. You have to listen for a long time before it turns into anything.',NULL),(1187,60,4,NULL,'Immy had been listening for four years, which was most of her life.','👂'),(1188,60,5,'What the 8:14 said','On the Monday, the 8:14 went past and said something new.\n\nIt said it twice. It said it again on Tuesday, and Wednesday, and by Thursday Immy was sitting on the fence at ten past eight with a notebook.',NULL),(1189,60,6,NULL,'The sound was in the wheels, near the back. A tick that came once a turn.\n\nTick. Tick. Tick. Always the same place in the wheel, always a bit louder than the day before.',NULL),(1190,60,7,NULL,'Immy knew what that meant, because Immy knew what everything about a train meant.\n\nA tick once a turn is a flat spot. A flat spot is a wheel that has slid along a rail instead of rolling, and a flat spot that gets louder is a flat spot that is getting worse.',NULL),(1191,60,8,NULL,'She wrote it down. Day, time, how loud, which carriage.\n\nFive days of it, in pencil, in a notebook with a bent corner.','📓'),(1192,60,9,'Nobody listens to a seven-year-old','This is the difficult part of the story and there is no way round it.\n\nImmy told her mother, who said trains make all sorts of noises. Immy told her teacher, who said how interesting and gave her a sticker.',NULL),(1193,60,10,NULL,'Immy rang the station. The man was kind. He asked how old she was, and when she said seven, he was still kind, but in a different way.\n\n\"We have engineers for that, love,\" he said.',NULL),(1194,60,11,NULL,'\"Have they heard it?\" said Immy.\n\n\"I expect so.\"\n\n\"But have they?\"\n\nThe man said he had to go.','📞'),(1195,60,12,'Grandad Noor','Immy\'s grandad had driven trains for thirty-one years and now mostly drove a lawnmower.\n\nShe read him the notebook on Saturday, start to finish, all five days, without stopping.',NULL),(1196,60,13,NULL,'He did not say how interesting.\n\nHe said: \"Which carriage?\"\n\n\"Third from the back.\"\n\n\"Which side?\"\n\n\"The side facing us. Right-hand, if you were sitting in it.\"',NULL),(1197,60,14,NULL,'Grandad Noor got up and found his coat, which he had not needed to find in some time.\n\n\"Right,\" he said. \"Come on. We are going to the station, and you are going to say that to a person standing in front of you, because that is harder to put down than a telephone.\"','🧓'),(1198,60,15,'Platform two','The station supervisor was called Ada Rennick and she had been doing the job for nineteen years.\n\nShe listened to Immy for four minutes without interrupting, which is a rare and underrated skill.',NULL),(1199,60,16,NULL,'\"A tick once a turn,\" she said at the end.\n\n\"Getting louder.\"\n\n\"Third carriage, right-hand side.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nAda Rennick looked at Grandad Noor. \"You taught her that?\"\n\n\"She taught herself. I only agreed with her.\"',NULL),(1200,60,17,NULL,'They held the 8:14 for nine minutes on the Monday, which made forty people late and two people very cross indeed.\n\nThe wheel had a flat spot eleven centimetres long.','🔧'),(1201,60,18,NULL,'Eleven centimetres is not a disaster. It is a repair.\n\nBut Ada Rennick explained, afterwards, in the office with the good biscuits, that a flat spot is a crack waiting for its moment, and that nobody had been close enough to the track to hear this one except a girl on a fence.',NULL),(1202,60,19,'The logbook','Immy has a proper logbook now. Ada Rennick gave it to her — a real one, with columns printed on it and a hard blue cover.',NULL),(1203,60,20,NULL,'She fills in one line a day. Train, time, sound, anything unusual.\n\nMost days the unusual column says NOTHING, which Grandad Noor says is the most important word in engineering and the one nobody wants to write.','📘'),(1204,60,21,NULL,'Twice more in two years she has written something in it.\n\nOnce it was a door seal. Once it was nothing at all, and she wrote that down too, because a wrong report that you check is worth more than a right one that you keep to yourself.',NULL),(1205,60,22,'After','If you go past Immy\'s house on the 8:14, look out of the right-hand window at ten past eight.\n\nThere is a fence, and a girl on it, and a blue notebook.',NULL),(1206,60,23,NULL,'She is not waving. She is listening.\n\nThat is a different job, and she is extremely good at it.','🚆'),(1207,60,24,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1208,61,1,'Forty-one losses','Esi Mensah joined the chess club in September and lost her first forty-one games.','♟️'),(1209,61,2,NULL,'Forty-one. In a row. Against everybody, including the eight-year-olds, including the boy who moves his bishops like rooks and has never been told.',NULL),(1210,61,3,NULL,'She did not seem to mind, which annoyed people more than the losing did.','😐'),(1211,61,4,'What the club thought','The club thought she was bad at chess and too stubborn to quit, which is a reasonable reading of forty-one losses.',NULL),(1212,61,5,NULL,'Mr Enright, who ran it, gently suggested the beginners\' group.',NULL),(1213,61,6,NULL,'Esi said no thank you, and asked whether she could play the strongest person available every single week instead.','📋'),(1214,61,7,'The notebook','What nobody had looked at was the notebook.',NULL),(1215,61,8,NULL,'After every game Esi wrote down the move at which she had lost — not the last move, the one where it had actually gone wrong, which is usually fifteen moves earlier.',NULL),(1216,61,9,NULL,'Game 1: lost at move 6. Game 9: lost at move 11. Game 22: lost at move 19. Game 41: lost at move 34.','📓'),(1217,61,10,NULL,'Her losses were getting later. Steadily. Almost in a straight line.',NULL),(1218,61,11,'What she was doing','Esi explained it, once, to a boy called Toma who asked properly instead of sympathetically.',NULL),(1219,61,12,NULL,'\"If I play people I can beat, I find out nothing. I win and I do not know why.\n\nIf I play people who crush me, I find out exactly where my understanding stops. It stops at the move where it goes wrong.\"','🧠'),(1220,61,13,NULL,'\"That sounds horrible.\"\n\n\"It is quite horrible,\" Esi agreed. \"It is also the fastest thing I have ever found.\"',NULL),(1221,61,14,'Game forty-two','She won game forty-two.',NULL),(1222,61,15,NULL,'Against a boy who had beaten her four times, in thirty-eight moves, without anything dramatic happening.',NULL),(1223,61,16,NULL,'Then she lost the next three, which she had expected and had written in the notebook in advance as a prediction, which is the most Esi thing in this entire book.','🏆'),(1224,61,17,'What Mr Enright did','He photocopied four pages of the notebook, with her permission, and pinned them up.',NULL),(1225,61,18,NULL,'Not as an inspirational thing. As a method, with the heading: HOW TO LOSE USEFULLY.',NULL),(1226,61,19,NULL,'Write down the move it went wrong, not the move you resigned.\nPlay people better than you on purpose.\nExpect to go backwards after a win.\nCount the move number, not the result.','📌'),(1227,61,20,'After','Esi is the second-strongest player in the club and is extremely clear that she is not the first.',NULL),(1228,61,21,NULL,'She still plays the strongest person available every week. She still loses to them about half the time, which she describes as the correct ratio and refuses to explain further.','♟️'),(1229,61,22,NULL,'There are nine notebooks now, on a shelf at the club, from nine different members.',NULL),(1230,61,23,NULL,'The newest one belongs to the boy who moves his bishops like rooks. Somebody finally told him. He wrote down the move number.',NULL),(1231,61,24,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1232,62,1,'Loud','Bea Okafor had the loudest laugh in the school and quite possibly the county.\n\nIt was not a cackle. It was a bark, a sort of HA, and then the rest of it coming along behind.','😄'),(1233,62,2,NULL,'She had been told to be quieter by eleven different adults, which she had counted, because she counts things when she is cross.',NULL),(1234,62,3,NULL,'Nobody had ever told her she was being unkind, because she was not.\n\nThey told her she was being a lot, which is a different thing and harder to fix, because you cannot apologise for it.','🤫'),(1235,62,4,'September','In September Mrs Vale moved her to the table by the window, next to a boy called Samuel.',NULL),(1236,62,5,NULL,'Samuel had been at the school for a year and had not spoken in it once.',NULL),(1237,62,6,NULL,'He talked at home. His mother said so. He had simply decided, at some point, that school was not a place where he did that, and a year had gone by.','🪑'),(1238,62,7,'What everybody expected','Everybody expected this to be a disaster, and so did Bea.',NULL),(1239,62,8,NULL,'She knew what she was. She had been told eleven times.',NULL),(1240,62,9,NULL,'She spent the first week being quiet on purpose, which was exhausting and which Samuel did not appear to notice or care about.','😬'),(1241,62,10,'Week three','In week three, during a thing about the Romans, Bea laughed.',NULL),(1242,62,11,NULL,'Properly. The bark and then the rest of it. At something in the book that was genuinely funny and that nobody else had got to yet.',NULL),(1243,62,12,NULL,'Half the class turned round. Mrs Vale said her name in the voice.\n\nAnd Samuel, beside her, made a noise.','📖'),(1244,62,13,'The noise','It was not a word. It was about half a second of air, which is what a laugh is when you have not used one in a year.',NULL),(1245,62,14,NULL,'Bea did not react. She says this is the only genuinely clever thing she has ever done.',NULL),(1246,62,15,NULL,'She did not look at him, or say \"did you just laugh\", or tell anybody.\n\nShe carried on reading, as though nothing at all had happened, for the remaining nine minutes of the lesson.','👀'),(1247,62,16,'Four months','He laughed again in week five. In November he said \"no\" out loud when she took his rubber.',NULL),(1248,62,17,NULL,'In January he said eleven words to her over the course of an afternoon, about a dog, and she counted them, and she has never told him that she counted them.',NULL),(1249,62,18,NULL,'By March he was talking in class, quietly, when asked directly.','🐕'),(1250,62,19,'What the adults thought','There was a meeting about it, because there always is.',NULL),(1251,62,20,NULL,'Somebody suggested it was the quiet table by the window that had helped. Somebody else suggested it was time, which is the explanation people use when they have not got one.',NULL),(1252,62,21,NULL,'Samuel\'s mother said, in the meeting, what Samuel had said at home.\n\n\"He says the girl next to him is so loud that nobody can hear him. He says that is why he can talk.\"','🗂️'),(1253,62,22,'After','Bea is still the loudest laugh in the county. She has been told to be quieter twice more since, which makes thirteen, and she still counts.',NULL),(1254,62,23,NULL,'But she has a thing she says to herself now, when it happens, which she worked out on her own and has never said to an adult.','🌞'),(1255,62,24,NULL,'Being a lot is not the same as being too much.\n\nSometimes it is exactly enough noise for somebody to hide behind while they get started.',NULL),(1256,62,25,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1257,63,1,'Forty-two pancakes','The pancake breakfast at Thwaite Village Hall happens on the first Saturday in March and has done since 1961.\n\nIn 1961 they made sixty. This year they made ninety, and at ten o\'clock there were forty-eight.','🥞'),(1258,63,2,NULL,'Forty-two pancakes had gone, and nobody had eaten them, because the doors did not open until half past ten and there was a queue outside to prove it.',NULL),(1259,63,3,NULL,'Nish Patel was eight and had brought a notebook on the off chance.\n\nThis was the first time in four years of bringing a notebook that anything had happened.',NULL),(1260,63,4,NULL,'FACT: 90 made, counted by Mrs Vale at 9:00.\nFACT: 48 at 10:00, counted by Mrs Vale again, twice.\nFACT: doors shut. Windows shut. Hatch shut.\nFACT: nobody in the hall but six helpers and me.','📓'),(1261,63,5,'The six helpers','Nish wrote down all six, including himself, because that is the rule in detecting and he had read it in a book.',NULL),(1262,63,6,NULL,'Mrs Vale, who made them. Mr Enright, who flipped them badly. Ada Rennick, on tea. Bea, who is eleven and was in charge of plates. Grandad Noor, who was in charge of nothing and did it well. And Nish.',NULL),(1263,63,7,NULL,'He asked each of them the same two questions, which Ada Rennick said was the most annoying thing that had ever happened to her on a Saturday.\n\nWhere were you? What did you see?','🫖'),(1264,63,8,'The stack','The pancakes lived in a stack, on a plate, under a tea towel, on the end of the long table.\n\nNish measured the stack that was left. Eleven centimetres. He wrote it down.',NULL),(1265,63,9,NULL,'Then he did the thing nobody else had thought to do, which was to look under the table.',NULL),(1266,63,10,NULL,'On the floor, in a line going towards the back door, there were four small round spots of batter.\n\nAnd one, further on, with a mark in it.','🔍'),(1267,63,11,NULL,'The mark was a curve with little ridges in it.\n\nNish put his own shoe next to it. Wrong size, wrong shape. He put Bea\'s next to it. Wrong.',NULL),(1268,63,12,'Not a shoe','Grandad Noor crouched down, which took a while and was accompanied by noises.\n\n\"That is not a shoe, lad.\"\n\n\"No?\"\n\n\"That is a wheel.\"',NULL),(1269,63,13,NULL,'The tea trolley has four wheels and lives by the back door.\n\nIts wheels have little ridges in them. One of them had batter in the ridges, dried, like something that had rolled through a spill several hours ago and not been washed.','🛞'),(1270,63,14,'What actually happened','Nish put it together in the wrong order first, which is normal, and then in the right order, which took him until Sunday.',NULL),(1271,63,15,NULL,'Mr Enright flipped badly. Everybody agrees on that. Four of his went on the floor between nine and half past.\n\nMrs Vale, who was making them, did not see, because she was facing the pan.',NULL),(1272,63,16,NULL,'Bea saw. Bea picked them up, because Bea was in charge of plates and the floor was next to the plates, and she put them in the bin and said nothing to avoid getting Mr Enright in trouble.','🧹'),(1273,63,17,NULL,'That is four. Not forty-two.',NULL),(1274,63,18,'The other thirty-eight','The other thirty-eight were in the kitchen, on a second plate, under a second tea towel, keeping warm in the bottom of the oven.\n\nMrs Vale had put them there herself at twenty past nine and forgotten entirely.',NULL),(1275,63,19,NULL,'She is seventy-eight. She had been up since five. She had made ninety pancakes.\n\nNish has written in his notebook, under the facts, a new heading that says: SOMETIMES THE ANSWER IS THAT SOMEBODY WAS TIRED.','😅'),(1276,63,20,'The awkward bit','Here is the part Nish is proudest of, and it is not the oven.\n\nHe had written BEA? in his notebook on the Saturday, with a question mark, because she had been nearest the floor and had gone quiet when he asked question two.',NULL),(1277,63,21,NULL,'He was right that she was hiding something. He was wrong about what, and wrong about why.\n\nShe was covering for a friend. He was nearly loud about it in a hall full of people.',NULL),(1278,63,22,NULL,'He crossed BEA? out so hard the pen went through the page.\n\nThen he wrote on the next page: ASK THE QUIET PERSON WHY THEY ARE QUIET. DO NOT GUESS.','🤐'),(1279,63,23,'After','The breakfast made £212 and everybody got a pancake.\n\nMr Enright has been moved to tea. Ada Rennick has been moved to the pan and is extremely good at it, which surprised nobody except Ada Rennick.',NULL),(1280,63,24,NULL,'And the tea trolley gets washed now, on the first Saturday in March, before anything else happens.\n\nIt is in the instructions. Nish wrote them.','🥞'),(1281,63,25,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1282,64,1,'The gap','Esme Hart found the gap in a library, which is where most gaps are found.\n\nThe 1890 map of Thwaite had a house at Number Four Crow Lane. The 1889 map had an empty plot. So far, ordinary.','🏚️'),(1283,64,2,NULL,'The strange part was the house itself.\n\nIt has a date stone over the door. The date stone says 1604.',NULL),(1284,64,3,'Three possibilities','Esme wrote them on the inside cover of her notebook, because three is a manageable number.\n\nOne: the date stone is fake.\nTwo: the maps are wrong.\nThree: the house was somewhere else until 1890.',NULL),(1285,64,4,NULL,'She assumed one. Everybody assumes one. Old houses collect invented dates the way coats collect pockets.','📓'),(1286,64,5,'Against the first','The stonemason at Cold Beck looked at a photograph for about nine seconds.\n\n\"That is not a Victorian carving,\" he said. \"Look at the four. Nobody has cut a four like that in two hundred years.\"',NULL),(1287,64,6,NULL,'He also pointed at the weathering, which was deep and uneven and on the side facing the wrong way for Crow Lane.\n\n\"Your prevailing wind is from the south-west. That stone has been weathered from the north-east for a long time.\"',NULL),(1288,64,7,NULL,'Esme wrote that down twice. The stone had spent centuries facing a different way from the house it was now in.','🧱'),(1289,64,8,'Against the second','The maps were not wrong. Esme checked four of them, from three different surveyors.\n\nCrow Lane was a field until 1889. There is a photograph of it being a field, with sheep on it, dated and stamped.',NULL),(1290,64,9,'Which leaves the third','Houses do not walk. But houses are moved, and more often than most people think, and the Victorians were shameless about it.',NULL),(1291,64,10,NULL,'Esme found the answer in a ledger in the county archive, which took eleven visits and a letter from her school.','🐎'),(1292,64,11,NULL,'In 1888 the reservoir at Harrow Water was approved. It flooded a valley with four farms and a hamlet called Nether Crow in it.',NULL),(1293,64,12,'Nether Crow','Eleven buildings went under. Nine were demolished. Two were taken down stone by stone, numbered, carted eight miles, and rebuilt.',NULL),(1294,64,13,NULL,'One of them was the chapel, which is now the village hall and which everybody knows about.\n\nThe other was a farmhouse built in 1604, which nobody knows about at all, and which is Number Four Crow Lane.','💧'),(1295,64,14,NULL,'The ledger lists the cost of the carting. It lists the men. It does not say why the house was worth moving, and Esme has not found that anywhere.',NULL),(1296,64,15,'The wrong-way stone','When you take a house apart and put it back together, you have to decide which way round it goes.\n\nThe men who rebuilt Number Four put the date stone over the new front door, which faced a different way from the old front door.',NULL),(1297,64,16,NULL,'That is the whole mystery, in one small decision made by somebody in a hurry in 1889.\n\nThe weathering says north-east because the house used to face north-east, in a valley that is now eleven metres underwater.','🧭'),(1298,64,17,'Going to see','In a dry summer the reservoir drops. In the dry summer of the year Esme turned eleven, it dropped four metres.',NULL),(1299,64,18,NULL,'She walked out on the mud with her grandfather and about forty other people, because a drowned village brings a crowd.',NULL),(1300,64,19,NULL,'The footings of Nether Crow are still there. Low walls, a lane you can follow, a well with a slab over it.','🪨'),(1301,64,20,NULL,'And one rectangle of stone foundations, eight metres by five, with a gap in the north-east side where a door used to be.\n\nEsme stood in the gap. It is, she measured, exactly the width of the front door at Number Four.',NULL),(1302,64,21,'After','There is a sign on Crow Lane now. Esme wrote the text and the parish council changed four words, which she still mentions.',NULL),(1303,64,22,NULL,'It says the house was built in 1604 in Nether Crow, eight miles west, and moved stone by stone in 1889 when the valley was flooded.\n\nIt names the eleven men who carted it, because they were in the ledger and nobody had ever written them down anywhere a person might read.','🪧'),(1304,64,23,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1305,65,1,'Eleven patents','Great-Uncle Bertie Nibbs had eleven patents and had never made a penny from any of them.','⚙️'),(1306,65,2,NULL,'He was extremely cheerful about this, which the rest of the family found suspicious.',NULL),(1307,65,3,NULL,'When he died he left the patents, a shed, and a note that said: SOMEBODY GO THROUGH THESE PROPERLY. NOT YOUR MOTHER.','📜'),(1308,65,4,'Suri goes through them','Suri Nibbs was nine and the only person who volunteered.',NULL),(1309,65,5,NULL,'She did it the way he would have wanted, which is to say extremely slowly, in a cold shed, with a notebook.',NULL),(1310,65,6,NULL,'Patent one: a self-stirring saucepan. Works. Unnecessary.\n\nPatent two: an umbrella with a gutter. Works. Appalling.','🔦'),(1311,65,7,NULL,'Patent three: a letterbox that says thank you. Works. Suri wants one.\n\nPatent four: shoes with a compartment. Works. Smells.',NULL),(1312,65,8,'Patent nine','Patent nine was two pages long and had no drawing, which was unusual.',NULL),(1313,65,9,NULL,'It described a method of marking the end of a roll of sticky tape by folding the final four centimetres back on itself so that the edge can be found again.','📄'),(1314,65,10,NULL,'Suri read it three times. Then she went and found a roll of tape and did it.',NULL),(1315,65,11,'Everyone does this','That is the thing. Everybody does this. Suri\'s mother does it. Her teacher does it. Her teacher\'s mother presumably did it.',NULL),(1316,65,12,NULL,'It is not an invention. It is a thing people work out on their own in about four seconds, in a thousand kitchens, independently, for ever.',NULL),(1317,65,13,NULL,'Suri wrote in her notebook: PATENT 9 — ALREADY EXISTS EVERYWHERE. POINTLESS.','🤨'),(1318,65,14,'The date','Then she looked at the date on the patent, because she had been going through them in order and had not checked.',NULL),(1319,65,15,NULL,'1961.',NULL),(1320,65,16,NULL,'Suri went and asked her grandmother, who was eighty-one, whether people folded the end of the tape over when she was a girl.','📅'),(1321,65,17,NULL,'\"No,\" said her grandmother, without hesitating. \"You picked at it with a fingernail like an idiot. For years.\"',NULL),(1322,65,18,'What that means','Suri has thought about this harder than about anything else in her life so far.',NULL),(1323,65,19,NULL,'Bertie did not invent a machine. He noticed a four-second habit before it was a habit, and he wrote it down, and then everybody worked it out anyway and nobody needed him.','💭'),(1324,65,20,NULL,'A patent is only worth something if people cannot easily do the thing themselves. The better and simpler the idea, the less it is worth.',NULL),(1325,65,21,'The eleventh','Patent eleven, the last one, was filed in 1994 and was one paragraph.',NULL),(1326,65,22,NULL,'It described a method of labelling the underside of a shelf, rather than the front, so that a person reaching up can read it.','✍️'),(1327,65,23,NULL,'Suri has found this in two libraries and one hardware shop since. She has no idea whether they got it from him and neither does anybody else.',NULL),(1328,65,24,'After','The eleven patents are in a folder in Suri\'s room, in order, each with a note from her at the front.',NULL),(1329,65,25,NULL,'Patent nine\'s note is the longest. It says: NOT WORTHLESS. TOO GOOD TO BE WORTH ANYTHING. THERE IS A DIFFERENCE AND HE KNEW IT.','🗂️'),(1330,65,26,NULL,'Which explains, Suri thinks, why he was so cheerful about never selling a single one.',NULL),(1331,65,27,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1332,66,1,'A dot in the wrong place','Teo Alabi found it on a chart in a second-hand shop: a small island, eleven kilometres long, four hundred miles off the coast.','🏝️'),(1333,66,2,NULL,'He looked it up to find out what lived there.\n\nNothing lives there. There is no there. The sea at that point is four thousand metres deep and always has been.',NULL),(1334,66,3,NULL,'The island is on charts from 1814. It was removed in 1976.\n\nFor a hundred and sixty-two years, sailors steered around a place that was not there.','🗺️'),(1335,66,4,'Phantom islands','It turns out there have been hundreds. Teo made a list and got to forty before he stopped.',NULL),(1336,66,5,NULL,'Some lasted centuries. Some appeared on charts, were searched for, were not found, and were redrawn anyway by the next cartographer because removing something is a bigger decision than copying it.',NULL),(1337,66,6,NULL,'That is the real subject of this book, and Teo did not expect it: not why islands get invented, but why they are so hard to delete.','📚'),(1338,66,7,'How one gets on a chart','Three ways, and Sable Island is probably the second.',NULL),(1339,66,8,NULL,'One: a mistake in position. A real island, placed wrongly, appearing twice — once where it is and once where it is not.',NULL),(1340,66,9,NULL,'Two: something seen that was not land. A fog bank. A low cloud on the horizon. A raft of floating pumice from an eruption, which can be kilometres across and looks exactly like a grey beach.','🧭'),(1341,66,10,NULL,'Three: an outright invention, to fill a chart, or to claim a route, or because a captain needed something to show for a voyage.',NULL),(1342,66,11,'What the log said','Teo found the original. It took him four months and two libraries and a letter to a maritime museum that he rewrote eleven times.',NULL),(1343,66,12,NULL,'Captain Bartholomew Nibbs, brig *Marigold*, the fourth of March 1814.\n\n\"Land to the north-west, low, some four leagues. Did not approach, the wind being foul.\"','📜'),(1344,66,13,NULL,'Did not approach. Four words that cost a hundred and sixty-two years.',NULL),(1345,66,14,'Pumice','There was an eruption at an undersea volcano in that region in late 1813.\n\nPumice rafts from it were reported by three other ships over the following eighteen months, in three different positions, because rafts drift.',NULL),(1346,66,15,NULL,'Nibbs almost certainly saw one. He wrote down what he saw, honestly, and he said he had not got close.','🌋'),(1347,66,16,NULL,'He is not the villain of this story. There is no villain. That is what makes it interesting.',NULL),(1348,66,17,'Why it stayed','Four ships searched for Sable Island between 1817 and 1902 and found open ocean.\n\nAll four reported it. The island remained on the chart after every single one.',NULL),(1349,66,18,NULL,'Teo found the reason in a note from the Hydrographic Office in 1903, and it is almost reasonable.','❓'),(1350,66,19,NULL,'\"The failure to find land in a given position is not proof that land is absent, the position itself being uncertain.\"',NULL),(1351,66,20,NULL,'Which is true. You cannot prove a thing is not somewhere by failing to find it, if you are not sure where you looked.',NULL),(1352,66,21,'What finally removed it','Not a ship. Satellites, and depth soundings, and finally in 1976 a survey that crossed the position and measured four thousand metres of water.',NULL),(1353,66,22,NULL,'You cannot miss an island if you can measure the floor beneath it.','🛰️'),(1354,66,23,'What Teo thinks it is about','He wrote this at the end of his project and his teacher made him read it out, which he hated.',NULL),(1355,66,24,NULL,'Putting something on a map takes one person and an honest mistake.\n\nTaking it off takes proof, and proof is expensive, and so wrong things stay on maps for a hundred and sixty-two years.',NULL),(1356,66,25,NULL,'He says the useful question is not \"who put it there\". It is \"why did four people who knew it was wrong not take it off\".','✏️'),(1357,66,26,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1358,15,1,'Where they go','Every kite that gets away ends up in the same place.','🪁'),(1359,15,2,NULL,'Not heaven, and not the sea, whatever people tell children. A long valley with no name, where the wind comes in at one end and does not leave.',NULL),(1360,15,3,NULL,'There are, at the last count, four hundred and eleven thousand kites in it.','🌬️'),(1361,15,4,'The keeper','There has always been a keeper. The job is not difficult and it is not optional.',NULL),(1362,15,5,NULL,'The keeper before this one had done it for sixty-one years and had kept no records whatsoever, which is why this story is mostly about filing.',NULL),(1363,15,6,NULL,'Her name was Senna and she was eleven when she arrived, which was young, and which nobody explained.','🗂️'),(1364,15,7,'The problem','Four hundred and eleven thousand kites in a valley is not a collection. It is a heap.',NULL),(1365,15,8,NULL,'And people come looking. Not often. Four or five a year, standing at the top end of the valley, asking for one particular kite out of four hundred and eleven thousand.',NULL),(1366,15,9,NULL,'The previous keeper had told them all the same thing, which was: look if you like, it is over there somewhere.','🙋'),(1367,15,10,NULL,'In sixty-one years, nobody had ever found theirs.',NULL),(1368,15,11,'What Senna did','She did not tidy the valley. You cannot tidy four hundred and eleven thousand of anything.',NULL),(1369,15,12,NULL,'She started a catalogue at the front, of the ones arriving, from the day she started.','📓'),(1370,15,13,NULL,'Date. Colour. Shape. Any writing on it. Length of string remaining. Where in the valley it settled.',NULL),(1371,15,14,'Nine a day','About nine kites a day arrive. That is three thousand two hundred a year, which is a great deal of writing and not an impossible amount.',NULL),(1372,15,15,NULL,'After four years Senna had thirteen thousand catalogued. Out of four hundred and eleven thousand. Three per cent.','✍️'),(1373,15,16,NULL,'Three per cent sounds hopeless and is the entire point of this story.',NULL),(1374,15,17,'The first find','A man came in the fifth year looking for a red box kite with a name on the spar, lost eleven months earlier.',NULL),(1375,15,18,NULL,'Eleven months was inside the catalogue. Senna looked it up in four minutes.',NULL),(1376,15,19,NULL,'It was nine hundred metres down the valley, under about forty other kites, and it took them two hours to dig out.','🔴'),(1377,15,20,NULL,'The name on the spar was his daughter\'s.',NULL),(1378,15,21,'What she tells people now','When somebody arrives, Senna asks one question first: when did you lose it?',NULL),(1379,15,22,NULL,'If it is inside her catalogue, she can find it, almost always, in under an hour.',NULL),(1380,15,23,NULL,'If it is before she started, she says so, honestly, and says they are welcome to look, and that she is sorry.','📅'),(1381,15,24,NULL,'Thirty-one people have found theirs. Several hundred have not, and have been told plainly why, which the previous keeper never did.',NULL),(1382,15,25,'The old heap','Senna has started working backwards as well, on afternoons when nothing is arriving, from the far end.',NULL),(1383,15,26,NULL,'She has catalogued four thousand of the old ones. At that rate she will finish in about nine hundred years.','🏔️'),(1384,15,27,NULL,'She does it anyway. She has worked out that somebody has to start, and that the reason there is a heap at all is that sixty-one years of keepers all reached the same conclusion about nine hundred years.',NULL),(1385,15,28,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1386,1,1,'Little Hollow','Little Hollow was a village at the bottom of a wood, and at some point it had stopped going out after dark.','🏮'),(1387,1,2,NULL,'Nobody could say when. There had been a reason once. The reason had gone and the habit had stayed, which is what usually happens.',NULL),(1388,1,3,NULL,'By the time this story starts, there were eleven houses in Little Hollow and not one of them had a door that opened after four o\'clock in winter.','🌲'),(1389,1,4,'The fox','There was a fox who lived under the wall at the top of the lane.',NULL),(1390,1,5,NULL,'She was not a brave fox. She was the least brave fox in the wood and she knew it. She had a particular way of lying down very flat when anything at all happened.',NULL),(1391,1,6,NULL,'She was, however, out at night, because foxes are, and she had noticed something the village had not.','🦊'),(1392,1,7,'What the fox had noticed','The path home from the mill, which everybody said was dangerous, was not dangerous.',NULL),(1393,1,8,NULL,'It had a hole in it, about halfway along, where a drain had collapsed. That was all. One hole, in one place, in the dark.',NULL),(1394,1,9,NULL,'People had fallen in it. People had told other people they had fallen in it. And somewhere along the way \"there is a hole in the mill path\" had turned into \"do not go out after dark\".','🕳️'),(1395,1,10,'The lantern','There was a lantern on a hook at the top of the lane that had not been lit in nine years.',NULL),(1396,1,11,NULL,'The fox could not light it. Foxes cannot light anything. This is a story with a fox in it and not a story where animals do impossible things.',NULL),(1397,1,12,NULL,'What she could do was sit under it.','🪝'),(1398,1,13,'Night one','On the first night she sat under the dark lantern for about an hour and then went and did fox things instead.',NULL),(1399,1,14,NULL,'Nobody came out. Nobody saw her.',NULL),(1400,1,15,'Night nine','On the ninth night, a girl called Wren came to the window of the end house and saw a fox sitting under the lantern post in the moonlight, perfectly still.',NULL),(1401,1,16,NULL,'She watched for a long time. Then she went and got her grandmother, who was slow and cross about being woken.','🪟'),(1402,1,17,NULL,'\"It is a fox,\" said her grandmother.\n\n\"She is sitting in the dangerous bit,\" said Wren. \"She has been there nine nights.\"',NULL),(1403,1,18,'What they worked out','The two of them went out, which was the first time anybody in Little Hollow had gone out after dark in nine years, and which took Wren\'s grandmother about four minutes to agree to.',NULL),(1404,1,19,NULL,'They took a torch. They found the hole. It was exactly one hole, about a metre across, in the middle of the mill path.',NULL),(1405,1,20,NULL,'The fox lay down very flat and did not move and let them walk past her, which was the bravest thing she had ever done.','🔦'),(1406,1,21,'The repair','It took four men half a morning to fill the hole. It had been there for nine years.',NULL),(1407,1,22,NULL,'Somebody lit the lantern the same week, and then somebody else lit it the week after, and now there is a list on the back of the chapel door with eleven names on it and whose turn it is.',NULL),(1408,1,23,'After','Little Hollow goes out after dark. There is nothing to go out to, particularly, but they do it, because they can.',NULL),(1409,1,24,NULL,'The fox still sits under the lantern. She is not guarding anything and she is not guiding anybody. She is a fox who has found a warm post and a bit of light.','✨'),(1410,1,25,NULL,'Wren leaves something under it on a Thursday. Nobody has told her to stop.',NULL),(1411,1,26,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1412,67,1,'The notice','The notice went up in June. The pier would close on the thirtieth of September, after a hundred and four years, because the supports underneath it had been condemned and the repair cost four million pounds.','🎡'),(1413,67,2,NULL,'There was a campaign. There is always a campaign. It raised eleven thousand pounds, which was both a lot of money and not nearly enough.',NULL),(1414,67,3,NULL,'Lev Marchetti was eleven and his grandmother had run the shell shop on the pier for thirty-one years.','📰'),(1415,67,4,'What he decided to do','He could not save it. He worked that out in June, which took less time than it took most of the adults.',NULL),(1416,67,5,NULL,'So he decided to write down what was on it instead.',NULL),(1417,67,6,NULL,'Every unit. Every sign. What it sold, who ran it, how long they had been there, and one thing they remembered.','📓'),(1418,67,7,'Twenty-two units','There were twenty-two, and four of them were empty, which he recorded as well because an empty unit is also a fact.',NULL),(1419,67,8,NULL,'The shell shop. The arcade. Two cafés. A fortune teller who was emphatic about being described as an entertainer. A man who sold rock with THWAITE through it.',NULL),(1420,67,9,NULL,'A kiosk that had sold nine different things in forty years and was currently selling phone chargers.','🍭'),(1421,67,10,'The interviews','Lev asked everybody the same question last, after the practical ones, and it is the question that made the project worth doing.',NULL),(1422,67,11,NULL,'\"What will people not know about this place once it is gone?\"',NULL),(1423,67,12,NULL,'The arcade man said that the third machine from the door had been broken since 1998 and that regulars put a coin in it on purpose, for luck, and nobody had ever told a tourist.','🎤'),(1424,67,13,NULL,'The fortune teller said that she had never once told anybody anything bad, in thirty-four years, and that this was a policy and not a limitation.',NULL),(1425,67,14,NULL,'The woman in the second café said she knew eleven regulars by their order and four of them by nothing else — not their names, not where they lived, nothing, for twenty years.','🔮'),(1426,67,15,'His grandmother','He left her until last, which he regrets, because she was the hardest.',NULL),(1427,67,16,NULL,'She said: \"The shells are from everywhere. People think they are from here. They are from the Philippines, mostly, and Mexico.\"',NULL),(1428,67,17,NULL,'\"Does that not bother people?\"\n\n\"Nobody has ever asked. In thirty-one years. You are the first.\"','🐚'),(1429,67,18,'The thirtieth of September','It rained. Four hundred people came anyway, which was about three hundred and eighty more than a normal Tuesday in September.',NULL),(1430,67,19,NULL,'Lev went to every unit with the notebook and got each person to sign the page about their own unit, which was not part of the original plan and which he thought of at about two in the afternoon.','🌧️'),(1431,67,20,NULL,'Twenty-two pages. Eighteen signatures. The four empty units signed by nobody, with a line drawn through.',NULL),(1432,67,21,'After','The pier is still there. Closed, fenced, with a sign. It may be there for years.',NULL),(1433,67,22,NULL,'The notebook was photographed page by page by the county archive, who had not asked for it and who rang the school after somebody mentioned it.','🚧'),(1434,67,23,NULL,'It is catalogued as an oral history. Lev is listed as the interviewer, which he says is the single most ridiculous thing that has ever happened to him.',NULL),(1435,67,24,NULL,'The third machine from the door is in his grandmother\'s garage. It still does not work. Four people have put a coin in it since it got there.',NULL),(1436,67,25,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1437,68,1,'Under the apple tree','There was a dinosaur in Dev\'s garden.\n\nIt was under the apple tree, where the grass goes thin, and it was shouting.','🦕'),(1438,68,2,NULL,'You would not think a dinosaur four centimetres long could shout.\n\nThis one managed. It had the sort of voice that gets heard at the back of a hall.',NULL),(1439,68,3,NULL,'\"YOU,\" it said, when Dev knelt down. \"FINALLY. I have been shouting since Thursday.\"\n\n\"It is Sunday,\" said Dev.\n\n\"I AM AWARE.\"',NULL),(1440,68,4,NULL,'The dinosaur was green on top and paler underneath, with a row of small bumps down her back and a tail she clearly thought a great deal of.\n\n\"Are you a stegosaurus?\"\n\n\"Do I look like a stegosaurus?\" she said. \"I am a Hypsilophodon. Say it.\"','🍎'),(1441,68,5,NULL,'Dev said it. He got it wrong twice and right on the third go, and the dinosaur nodded, which took her whole body.\n\n\"Good. Nobody has said it properly in sixty-six million years.\"',NULL),(1442,68,6,'The problem','\"Why are you shouting?\" said Dev.\n\n\"Because I am four centimetres long,\" said the dinosaur, \"and I used to be two metres, and nobody will tell me what happened.\"',NULL),(1443,68,7,NULL,'Dev thought about this carefully. He was six. He had a book about dinosaurs with a fold-out page, and he had read it so many times the fold-out page no longer folded.\n\n\"I know what happened,\" he said.',NULL),(1444,68,8,NULL,'The dinosaur went very still.\n\n\"Go on then.\"\n\n\"There was a rock,\" said Dev. \"A really big one. From space. It hit the sea near Mexico and made it dark for a long time and most things did not manage.\"','☄️'),(1445,68,9,NULL,'The dinosaur sat down on a dandelion.\n\n\"A rock,\" she said.\n\n\"A really big one.\"\n\n\"How big?\"\n\n\"Ten kilometres,\" said Dev. \"The book says ten kilometres.\"',NULL),(1446,68,10,NULL,'\"And how big is a kilometre?\"\n\nDev pointed up the road, past the postbox, to where the road bent round by the chapel.\n\n\"About to there. Ten of those. All rock. All at once.\"',NULL),(1447,68,11,NULL,'The dinosaur was quiet for a long time. A blackbird landed, looked at her, and left again, which she did not appear to notice.\n\n\"Did any of us manage?\" she said at last.','🌑'),(1448,68,12,'The good bit','This was the part Dev had been waiting for his whole life, and he had never once been asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"Loads.\"',NULL),(1449,68,13,NULL,'\"Loads?\"\n\n\"The small ones with feathers. They managed.\" Dev pointed at the hedge, where the blackbird had gone. \"That is one. That is a dinosaur. There are about ten thousand kinds and they are all over the garden.\"',NULL),(1450,68,14,NULL,'The dinosaur looked at the hedge.\n\nThen she looked at the fence, where two sparrows were having an argument. Then at the sky, where a gull was going over, not hurrying.','🐦'),(1451,68,15,NULL,'\"Those,\" she said slowly, \"are extremely rude relatives.\"\n\n\"They are,\" Dev agreed.\n\n\"One of them ate a chip in front of me on Friday.\"\n\n\"That sounds right.\"',NULL),(1452,68,16,'Four centimetres','\"It does not explain,\" said the dinosaur, \"why I am four centimetres long.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Dev. \"It does not.\"',NULL),(1453,68,17,NULL,'They thought about it together, which is a better way of thinking about things.\n\n\"Perhaps,\" said Dev, \"there was only a little bit of you left, and it had to do.\"',NULL),(1454,68,18,NULL,'The dinosaur considered this.\n\n\"A little bit of me,\" she said, \"is still me.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Dev.\n\n\"I am still a Hypsilophodon.\"\n\n\"You are the only one,\" said Dev. \"In the entire world. I checked the fold-out page.\"','🔍'),(1455,68,19,NULL,'That seemed to settle something. She stood up off the dandelion and shook out her tail, which she clearly thought a great deal of.\n\n\"Right,\" she said. \"Where is the food?\"',NULL),(1456,68,20,'What a Hypsilophodon eats','A Hypsilophodon, it turns out, eats almost anything green that is close to the ground and difficult to reach.\n\nThis makes her, in Dev\'s mother\'s opinion, the single most useful thing ever to happen to the flower beds.','🌿'),(1457,68,21,NULL,'His mother has never seen the dinosaur. She has seen the effect of the dinosaur, which is an entirely weeded border, and she has decided not to ask.\n\nSome parents are very sensible.',NULL),(1458,68,22,NULL,'Dev takes the fold-out page out to the apple tree most afternoons.\n\nThey are working through the book from the back, which was the dinosaur\'s idea, because the back is where the index is and she wants to know who else got a mention.',NULL),(1459,68,23,NULL,'\"Iguanodon,\" she will say. \"Insufferable. Thumb like a bradawl and never stopped going on about it.\"\n\nDev writes these down. He is making his own book. It has a fold-out page, which folds perfectly, because it is new.','📖'),(1460,68,24,'After','If you have a thin patch of grass under an apple tree, it is worth kneeling down and having a proper look.\n\nAnd if something shouts at you, do not be alarmed. It has probably been shouting since Thursday.',NULL),(1461,68,25,NULL,'Say the name properly. That is all she really wants.\n\nHyp-si-lo-pho-don. Three goes is perfectly respectable.',NULL),(1462,68,26,NULL,'The end.','🦕'),(1463,8,1,'Power down','Tharsis Station went into power-down on the fourth of Sol-March, which meant eleven per cent of normal consumption for an estimated hundred and forty days.','🚀'),(1464,8,2,NULL,'A dust storm had cut the array output to almost nothing. Nobody was in danger. Everybody was about to be extremely bored.',NULL),(1465,8,3,NULL,'Power-down means: heat the habs, run the scrubbers, grow the food, and switch off everything else.','🌪️'),(1466,8,4,'Everything else','Everything else included the station library, which was a server and four screens and about nine hundred thousand books.',NULL),(1467,8,5,NULL,'Juno Okonkwo was twelve and had read four hundred and six of them, which she knew because the server counted.',NULL),(1468,8,6,NULL,'On the fifth of Sol-March the library went dark along with the gym, the comms relay and the ice-cream machine, and nobody argued, because a hab that is cold is a hab that kills you and a hab with no books is just unpleasant.','📚'),(1469,8,7,'Forty-one children','There were forty-one children on Tharsis Station and a hundred and forty days of nothing.',NULL),(1470,8,8,NULL,'By day nine there had been two fights, which on a station of two hundred and six people is a statistic that the medical officer writes down.',NULL),(1471,8,9,'What Juno asked for','She went to the station engineer, Ada Rennick, and asked how much power the library server used.',NULL),(1472,8,10,NULL,'\"Four hundred watts, with cooling. It is not the server. It is the screens and the cooling.\"','⚡'),(1473,8,11,NULL,'\"What if there were no screens?\"',NULL),(1474,8,12,'The answer','Ada Rennick looked at her for a while.',NULL),(1475,8,13,NULL,'\"Then it is about ninety watts for an hour a day, and ninety watts I can find. But nine hundred thousand books that nobody can see is not a library.\"',NULL),(1476,8,14,NULL,'\"It is if somebody reads them out,\" said Juno.','💡'),(1477,8,15,'One hour a day','This is the whole plan and it is not clever. It is simply the only thing available.',NULL),(1478,8,16,NULL,'One hour of server time a day. Juno, with one tablet, choosing one chapter and copying it out by hand onto the back of hydroponics logs.',NULL),(1479,8,17,NULL,'Then reading it aloud in the long corridor at nineteen hundred, which is where people walk after dinner because there is nowhere else to walk.','✍️'),(1480,8,18,'Day one of that','Four people came. Two of them were her parents and one was a man waiting to use the washroom.',NULL),(1481,8,19,NULL,'Day four: eleven.\nDay nine: twenty-six.\nDay twenty-two: she had to move it to the canteen, and somebody made a sign.',NULL),(1482,8,20,NULL,'The sign said THE LAST LIBRARY ON MARS, which was a joke, and which stopped being a joke somewhere around day forty.','🪧'),(1483,8,21,'What it became','By day sixty there were four readers, because Juno had lost her voice twice and because other people wanted a turn.',NULL),(1484,8,22,NULL,'By day ninety there were eleven, including three adults who had never read aloud to anybody in their lives and were terrible at it and did it anyway.',NULL),(1485,8,23,NULL,'The hand-copied pages were kept, because paper on Mars is not thrown away, and they filled four binders.','🗣️'),(1486,8,24,'The storm clears','The array came back on day a hundred and thirty-one, eleven days early.',NULL),(1487,8,25,NULL,'The library went back on the same afternoon. All nine hundred thousand books, four screens, full brightness.',NULL),(1488,8,26,NULL,'Attendance at nineteen hundred in the canteen that evening: thirty-four.','🖥️'),(1489,8,27,'After','They still do it. Every evening, nineteen hundred, one chapter, read aloud, with the library fully powered and available on every tablet on the station.',NULL),(1490,8,28,NULL,'Juno is nineteen now and no longer runs it. It has a rota and an argument about the rota, which is how you know a thing has survived.',NULL),(1491,8,29,NULL,'The four binders are in a case in the canteen. The handwriting changes eleven times.','📖'),(1492,8,30,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1493,69,1,'Eleven seconds','Kaya Mensah had come second eleven times.\n\nAlways to the same rider. Always by between four and eleven seconds. Always on the last hill.','🚴'),(1494,69,2,NULL,'Her coach said: train harder.\n\nKaya trained harder. She came second by nine seconds.',NULL),(1495,69,3,'The rider in front','Her name was Juno Pike and she was not faster than Kaya.\n\nThat was the maddening part. On the flat, Kaya was quicker. On the climb, Kaya was quicker. Kaya had the numbers to prove both.',NULL),(1496,69,4,NULL,'She lost between the climb and the line, in the last four hundred metres, every single time.','⌚'),(1497,69,5,'What she did instead of training harder','On the twelfth week, Kaya did not ride.\n\nShe stood at the top of the last hill with a stopwatch and a notebook and watched everybody else do it.',NULL),(1498,69,6,NULL,'Her coach was unimpressed. Kaya pointed out that twelve weeks of training harder had produced eleven second places, and that a different approach could hardly do worse.',NULL),(1499,69,7,NULL,'She wrote down, for every rider, three things.\n\nWhere they changed gear. When they got out of the saddle. Which line they took round the bend at the top.','📓'),(1500,69,8,'The bend','Here is what she found, and it took two Saturdays to be sure of it.\n\nEverybody took the bend wide. Juno Pike took it tight, right against the inside kerb, and came out of it already pedalling.',NULL),(1501,69,9,NULL,'Wide costs about nine metres. Nine metres at that speed is about a second and a half.\n\nThat is not eleven seconds. Kaya wrote it down anyway, because a second and a half is a second and a half.',NULL),(1502,69,10,NULL,'The rest came from gears.\n\nEverybody changed down on the climb and then stayed down over the top, grinding, for about ten seconds, before remembering to change up.','🔄'),(1503,69,11,NULL,'Juno changed up *before* the summit. Three clicks, in the last twenty metres of the climb, while it still hurt.\n\nIt looked like a mistake. It is the whole race.',NULL),(1504,69,12,'Why it works','Kaya worked this out on the kitchen floor with a bicycle upside down, which is where this sort of thing gets worked out.',NULL),(1505,69,13,NULL,'Over the top of a hill you stop fighting gravity and start fighting air. Those need different gears.\n\nIf you wait until you feel fast to change up, you have already spent four seconds spinning uselessly.','⚙️'),(1506,69,14,NULL,'Changing up early means ten horrible seconds at the top of the climb and then a gear that already works when the road tips down.',NULL),(1507,69,15,'The twelfth race','Kaya did both things. Up three clicks before the summit. Tight to the kerb.\n\nThe ten horrible seconds were worse than she had imagined. She nearly stood up out of it twice.',NULL),(1508,69,16,NULL,'She came out of the bend already pedalling, in a gear that bit, with four hundred metres of downhill road and Juno Pike beside her instead of in front.','🏁'),(1509,69,17,NULL,'She lost by half a wheel.',NULL),(1510,69,18,'The bit that matters','Half a wheel. Not nine seconds. Half a wheel.\n\nKaya sat on the grass at the finish and laughed until she had to lie down, which the first-aid volunteer did not care for.',NULL),(1511,69,19,NULL,'Juno Pike came and sat next to her.\n\n\"You changed up before the top.\"\n\n\"I did.\"\n\n\"Who told you?\"\n\n\"Nobody told me. I watched you for two Saturdays.\"','😄'),(1512,69,20,NULL,'Juno was quiet for a bit.\n\n\"My grandad taught me that when I was six,\" she said. \"I did not work it out. I was just given it.\"\n\n\"Well,\" said Kaya, \"now I have got it too.\"',NULL),(1513,69,21,'After','They train together now, which the coach describes as giving away state secrets and which both of them describe as getting faster.',NULL),(1514,69,22,NULL,'Kaya has won four. Juno has won six. The notebook has forty pages in it, and other riders borrow it, and Kaya lets them.','🤝'),(1515,69,23,NULL,'On the first page, in capitals, it says: TRAINING HARDER IS NOT THE ONLY THING YOU ARE ALLOWED TO DO.',NULL),(1516,69,24,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1517,70,1,'The extra line','At the bottom of the timetable in the glass case at Thwaite station there is one more service than there should be.','🚂'),(1518,70,2,NULL,'23:47 — NETHER CROW. Every night. Sixty-one years after Nether Crow station closed.',NULL),(1519,70,3,NULL,'Ines Vargas noticed it because she reads timetables, which she accepts is not normal.','🕚'),(1520,70,4,'What she assumed','A printing error. Obviously. A line copied forward from an old file by somebody who was not looking.',NULL),(1521,70,5,NULL,'This is almost certainly what you are thinking and it is what Ines thought for eleven days.',NULL),(1522,70,6,NULL,'Then she went and looked at the old timetables in the library, which go back to 1951, and found that the 23:47 is not copied forward.','🖨️'),(1523,70,7,NULL,'It has been re-typeset nine times. The font has changed four times. In 1988 the whole timetable was redesigned and somebody put the 23:47 back in by hand.',NULL),(1524,70,8,'That is not an error','An error is a thing nobody notices. This had been noticed, re-entered and preserved by at least nine different people over sixty-one years.',NULL),(1525,70,9,NULL,'Ines wrote to the train operator. She got a polite letter saying there was no such service and that the timetable would be corrected.','📒'),(1526,70,10,NULL,'She wrote back asking them not to correct it until she had found out why, which she says is the boldest thing she has ever done.',NULL),(1527,70,11,'Mr Teague','The answer came from the retired signalman at Cold Beck, who was ninety-one and who had been waiting, he said, for somebody to ask.',NULL),(1528,70,12,NULL,'Nether Crow closed in 1964 along with hundreds of other small stations.','🧓'),(1529,70,13,NULL,'The last scheduled service left Thwaite at 23:47 on the fourth of September 1964, and eleven people were on it, and four of them worked there and were travelling to their last shift.',NULL),(1530,70,14,'What the stationmaster did','The stationmaster at Thwaite was a man called Albert Nibbs, and he did something small and entirely against the rules.',NULL),(1531,70,15,NULL,'When the new timetable came through with the service removed, he put it back in by hand before it went in the case.','✍️'),(1532,70,16,NULL,'He did it the next year too, and the year after that, and when he retired in 1979 he showed his replacement how, and asked her to keep doing it.',NULL),(1533,70,17,NULL,'She did. For twenty-two years. Then she showed hers.','🤝'),(1534,70,18,'Nine people','Ines has traced all nine. Three are alive. Two were willing to talk to her, which took eleven letters.',NULL),(1535,70,19,NULL,'None of them could give a clean reason. The closest was the woman who held it from 1979 to 2001, who said: \"Somebody asked me to and it cost nothing and the people who wanted it were dead.\"',NULL),(1536,70,20,NULL,'Ines thinks that is the whole answer, and better than a tidier one.','💬'),(1537,70,21,'What happened next','The operator wrote again to say the correction had been scheduled.',NULL),(1538,70,22,NULL,'Ines sent them eleven pages: the nine typesettings, the signalman\'s account, the two interviews, and a photograph of the 1964 timetable beside the current one.',NULL),(1539,70,23,NULL,'She did not ask them to keep it. She asked them to decide on purpose, which she says is different and is all anybody can reasonably ask.','📬'),(1540,70,24,'After','The 23:47 is still at the bottom of the timetable at Thwaite.',NULL),(1541,70,25,NULL,'There is now a small line underneath it, in italics, which the operator added and which Ines did not write and would not have dared to suggest.',NULL),(1542,70,26,NULL,'\"This service has not run since 4 September 1964. It is listed here by long custom.\"','🪧'),(1543,70,27,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1544,71,1,'The shortest day','On the twenty-first of December the village of Thwaite puts one candle in every front window.\n\nIt has done it for longer than anybody can explain. When you ask, people say \"for the longest night\", which is a reason shaped like an answer.','🕯️'),(1545,71,2,NULL,'Senna Okafor liked counting them.\n\nFrom the top of Hill Street you can see most of the village. Sixty-one windows last year. Sixty-three the year before.',NULL),(1546,71,3,NULL,'This year she counted fifty-nine, twice.\n\nThen she went and found which ones were dark, which took her an hour in the cold and is the sort of thing only a nine-year-old would bother to do.','🏘️'),(1547,71,4,'Four dark windows','Two of them were the Harlows, who were away.\n\nOne was the empty cottage by the chapel, which had been empty for two years.',NULL),(1548,71,5,NULL,'The fourth was Number Eleven, which was not empty.\n\nMr Teague lived there. He had lived there for forty years. His window had a candle in it every year Senna had been alive.',NULL),(1549,71,6,NULL,'The curtains were open. The room behind them was dark. There was a light on somewhere at the back of the house.','🪟'),(1550,71,7,'What Senna did not do','She did not knock. She was nine and it was dark and there are sensible rules about that.',NULL),(1551,71,8,NULL,'She went home and told her father, who said Mr Teague was probably just not bothering this year, and that people get like that, and to leave it.',NULL),(1552,71,9,NULL,'Senna did not leave it. She wrote it down instead, which is what she does with things she has been told to leave.','🤨'),(1553,71,10,'What she worked out','Mr Teague\'s wife had died in March. Senna knew this because the whole village had known it in March.',NULL),(1554,71,11,NULL,'She worked out, sitting on the stairs with a biscuit, that the candle had probably always been somebody\'s job in that house.\n\nAnd that if it had been Mrs Teague\'s job, then this was the first year in forty that nobody had done it.',NULL),(1555,71,12,NULL,'That is a small thing. Senna understood that it was a small thing.\n\nShe also understood that it was the only small thing she could reach.','🕯️'),(1556,71,13,'The ask','In the morning she went with her father, because that is the rule.\n\nMr Teague opened the door in a dressing gown at eleven o\'clock, which told them something on its own.',NULL),(1557,71,14,NULL,'Senna had practised the sentence nine times and it still came out backwards.\n\n\"Your window is dark and I counted and there were fifty-nine and would you like me to do yours.\"',NULL),(1558,71,15,NULL,'Mr Teague looked at her for a long moment.\n\n\"Mary did the candle,\" he said.\n\n\"I know,\" said Senna. \"That is why I came.\"','🚪'),(1559,71,16,'The twenty-first','They did it together at four o\'clock, which is when it gets dark.\n\nHe got the candlestick down. She was not allowed to light it, which was correct, and she held the matches, which was enough.',NULL),(1560,71,17,NULL,'He put it in the window himself. He stood there a while afterwards.',NULL),(1561,71,18,NULL,'\"Sixty,\" said Senna, from the gate.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"That is sixty now. There were fifty-nine.\"','✨'),(1562,71,19,'Why the village does it','Mr Teague told her, on the step, the reason nobody else had been able to give her.',NULL),(1563,71,20,NULL,'It is not for the people indoors. It is for anybody walking.\n\nOn the longest night, when it is dark at four and dark until eight, you light your window so that whoever is out there can see that somebody is in.',NULL),(1564,71,21,NULL,'\"That is all it has ever been,\" he said. \"A row of windows saying: there is somebody here. Keep going.\"','🌨️'),(1565,71,22,'After','Senna has a list now, in the back of her book, of the windows that go dark.',NULL),(1566,71,23,NULL,'She checks it on the twenty-first and again on the twenty-second. She has never needed it more than twice in one year.',NULL),(1567,71,24,NULL,'It is not a big list. That is not the point of it.\n\nThe point of it is that somebody is counting.',NULL),(1568,71,25,NULL,'The end.','🕯️'),(1569,72,1,'A crack in a cornfield','On the twentieth of February 1943, a farmer called Dionisio Pulido was burning branches at the edge of his cornfield in Michoacán, Mexico.\n\nThere was a shallow hollow in that field. There had always been a hollow. Nobody thought anything of it.','🌋'),(1570,72,2,NULL,'That afternoon, the ground near the hollow split open.\n\nPulido described a crack about half a metre wide. He said the ground swelled. He said there was a hissing, and a smell of rotten eggs, and fine grey dust, and then a sound underneath everything like thunder that would not stop.',NULL),(1571,72,3,NULL,'By the time it got dark, the crack was throwing rock into the air.\n\nBy the next morning there was a cone of loose stone in his field, taller than a person.',NULL),(1572,72,4,NULL,'This is the only volcano in recorded history that scientists watched from the very first day of its life.\n\nThat is why it matters. Not because it was the biggest — it was not — but because somebody was standing there at the beginning with a notebook.','📅'),(1573,72,5,'How fast it grew','Here is the growth of Parícutin, which is what the volcano came to be called, after the village that is no longer there.\n\nThese are measured figures, not estimates.',NULL),(1574,72,6,NULL,'After one day: about 10 metres.\n\nAfter one week: about 50 metres.\n\nAfter eight weeks: about 150 metres.\n\nAfter one year: about 336 metres.\n\nAt the end, nine years later: 424 metres above the field it started in.',NULL),(1575,72,7,NULL,'Look at the shape of those numbers for a moment.\n\nMost of the mountain arrived in the first year. That is normal for this kind of volcano, and it is the opposite of what most people expect, because we are used to mountains being slow.','📈'),(1576,72,8,'Why it was not slow','A volcano like Parícutin is called a cinder cone, and it is the simplest kind there is.\n\nIt is, essentially, a pile. Gas-rich molten rock is thrown into the air, cools as it flies, and lands as loose fragments called scoria, which stack up around the vent at roughly the angle that loose material will hold.',NULL),(1577,72,9,NULL,'That angle is about thirty-three degrees. You can see it in a pile of sand, a pile of gravel, or a heap of sugar.\n\nGeologists call it the angle of repose, and a cinder cone is simply the largest example of it you are ever likely to see.',NULL),(1578,72,10,NULL,'This is why the growth was fast and then stopped.\n\nA pile builds quickly while material is arriving. It cannot get steeper than its angle of repose, so it gets wider instead, and getting wider takes far more material for every metre of height.','⛰️'),(1579,72,11,'What came out, and how much','Over nine years Parícutin produced roughly 1.3 cubic kilometres of material.\n\nAbout three quarters of that was lava, which flowed rather than flew, and which did the real damage.',NULL),(1580,72,12,NULL,'Lava from a cinder cone does not usually come out of the top. It comes out of the bottom, through the side of the cone, because the cone is a loose pile and a loose pile is easily pushed aside.\n\nThat is exactly what happened here.',NULL),(1581,72,13,NULL,'In June 1944 a lava flow reached the village of San Juan Parangaricutiro.\n\nIt did not arrive quickly. Lava of this kind moves at a walking pace or slower, and often far slower, which is why almost nobody is killed by it.','🏚️'),(1582,72,14,NULL,'The village was evacuated. The lava buried it.\n\nThe church tower was tall enough and strong enough to stay above the flow, and it is still there, standing out of a black rock field, and you can visit it.',NULL),(1583,72,15,'Who died, and of what','Three people are recorded as having died because of Parícutin.\n\nNone of them died from lava. All three were killed by lightning generated in the ash column.',NULL),(1584,72,16,NULL,'Volcanic lightning happens because ash particles rub against each other in a rising cloud and build up static charge, in much the same way as a jumper pulled over your head.\n\nThe charge has to go somewhere. In a big ash column it goes in bolts.','⚡'),(1585,72,17,NULL,'This is worth sitting with. The famous danger — the glowing orange river — was slow enough to walk away from.\n\nThe thing that actually killed people was the weather the volcano made.',NULL),(1586,72,18,'How we know all this','A geologist named Ezequiel Ordóñez reached the site within days and understood immediately what he was looking at.\n\nHe wrote: it is not every day that a man is present at the birth of a volcano. He stayed. Others came.',NULL),(1587,72,19,NULL,'For nine years, scientists measured the cone, mapped the flows, collected the ash, photographed the column, and recorded the earthquakes.\n\nThat is why the figures in this book have real numbers in them and not the word \"about\" in front of all of them.','📷'),(1588,72,20,NULL,'It is also why we understand cinder cones at all.\n\nThere are thousands of them in the world. We watched exactly one of them happen. Everything we think about the other thousands rests, in part, on nine years in a Mexican cornfield.',NULL),(1589,72,21,'The end of it','Parícutin stopped in 1952, quite suddenly, after a last burst of activity.\n\nCinder cones are usually monogenetic, which means they erupt once and never again. Parícutin is considered finished. It will not come back.',NULL),(1590,72,22,NULL,'The lava field is being taken apart now by rain, roots and lichen, which is the slow half of geology and the half nobody films.\n\nPine trees are growing on the lower slopes. The cone is 424 metres tall and getting shorter, a few millimetres at a time.','🌱'),(1591,72,23,'What to take from it','Three things, if you remember nothing else.\n\nMountains are not always slow. We say \"as old as the hills\" because most hills are old, not because hills must be.',NULL),(1592,72,24,NULL,'The dangerous part of a disaster is often not the part in the pictures.\n\nAnd somebody standing in a field with a notebook, writing down what they can actually see, is worth more to science than a hundred people who were not there.',NULL),(1593,72,25,NULL,'Dionisio Pulido was not a scientist. He was a farmer who lost his field.\n\nHis account of the first afternoon — the crack, the swelling, the hissing, the smell — is quoted in geology textbooks to this day, because he was there and he told it straight.','📓'),(1594,72,26,NULL,'The end.','🌋'),(1595,73,1,'Three Roman coins','Thwaite Museum occupies one room above the library and is open on Thursdays.\n\nIt contains three Roman coins, a stuffed badger, a piece of the old bridge, and a case of farm tools nobody can identify.','🏛️'),(1596,73,2,NULL,'In the year Ola Bankole turned eight, it had forty-one visitors.\n\nEleven of them were Ola.',NULL),(1597,73,3,'The suggestion box','There is a suggestion box. It had been emptied twice since 1998.\n\nOla put in a card that said: WHY IS THERE NOTHING IN HERE FROM NOW?',NULL),(1598,73,4,NULL,'The curator, a tired man called Mr Teague, wrote back — actually wrote back, on paper, which Ola had not expected.\n\n\"Because nothing from now is old enough to be interesting.\"','📮'),(1599,73,5,NULL,'Ola wrote back: \"The Roman coins were not interesting in Roman times either.\"',NULL),(1600,73,6,'The argument','Mr Teague let her come up on a Thursday and make her case, mostly to end the correspondence.',NULL),(1601,73,7,NULL,'\"A museum keeps what would otherwise be thrown away,\" said Ola, who had rehearsed.\n\n\"We keep what survived.\"\n\n\"You keep what survived by accident. Why not keep some on purpose?\"',NULL),(1602,73,8,NULL,'She had brought three things in a shoebox.\n\nA bus ticket from the 14A, which had stopped running in March.\n\nA shoelace from the shoe factory at Cold Beck, which shut in 2011.\n\nA jar of buttons from her grandmother, sorted by colour over sixty years.','🎟️'),(1603,73,9,'The bus ticket','Mr Teague picked up the bus ticket first, to be polite, and then stopped being polite and started being a curator.',NULL),(1604,73,10,NULL,'The 14A had run for fifty-three years. It was how everybody in three villages had got to school, the hospital, and the Saturday market.',NULL),(1605,73,11,NULL,'\"How many of these do you think exist?\" he said.\n\n\"Loads.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Mr Teague. \"They are rubbish. People throw rubbish away. That is what makes it rare.\"','🚌'),(1606,73,12,'The rule of rubbish','This turned out to be the most interesting idea in the whole museum, and it is written on the wall now.',NULL),(1607,73,13,NULL,'Precious things survive because people look after them. We have plenty of crowns.\n\nOrdinary things survive by accident, and so almost none do, and so a bus ticket from a route that no longer exists is genuinely harder to find than a Roman coin.',NULL),(1608,73,14,NULL,'There are tens of thousands of Roman coins in British museums. Thwaite has three and they are not even good ones.\n\nThere is one surviving 14A ticket. It is upstairs, in a case, and it cost nothing.','🗑️'),(1609,73,15,'The Ordinary Room','They gave Ola the smallest case, by the stairs, and six months.',NULL),(1610,73,16,NULL,'She went and asked for things. That was the whole method. She asked at the shop, the school, the allotments and the bus garage, and she asked the same two questions every time.',NULL),(1611,73,17,NULL,'What do you have that nobody else bothered to keep?\n\nAnd: what was it for?','❓'),(1612,73,18,'What came in','A milk bottle from the dairy that closed in 1994.\n\nA factory clocking-in card with sixty weeks of Mrs Vale\'s handwriting on it.',NULL),(1613,73,19,NULL,'A school dinner menu from 1979, which somebody had kept because it was the week they met their husband.\n\nA tin of distemper paint, unopened, from the chapel redecoration of 1968.',NULL),(1614,73,20,NULL,'And the jar of buttons, which is the thing people stand in front of longest, because sixty years of sorting by colour is visible in about four seconds and takes much longer than that to think about.','🫙'),(1615,73,21,'Visitors','The museum had four hundred and nine visitors the following year.\n\nMost of them came to see the ordinary room and then looked at the Roman coins on the way out, which Mr Teague says is the correct order and always has been.',NULL),(1616,73,22,NULL,'Each case has two labels now. What it is, and what it was for.\n\nThe second one is always longer. Ola insisted, and she was right.','🎫'),(1617,73,23,'After','There is a box by the door for new donations. It says: SOMETHING YOU WERE GOING TO THROW AWAY.',NULL),(1618,73,24,NULL,'It gets emptied every Thursday now, which is forty-nine times a year more often than the suggestion box used to manage.',NULL),(1619,73,25,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1620,74,1,'Six o\'clock','The lights went out at six o\'clock, in the middle of a programme, on the coldest night of the year.','💡'),(1621,74,2,NULL,'Lola was seven and had never been in a house with no electricity in it.\n\nIt is darker than you expect. It is also much quieter, which nobody warns you about.',NULL),(1622,74,3,NULL,'All the small noises stopped at once. The fridge. The thing in the hall. The hum you never knew was there until it was not.','🌑'),(1623,74,4,'What her father did first','He did not panic and he did not go looking for a torch.',NULL),(1624,74,5,NULL,'He said: \"Everybody stay exactly where you are and tell me where you are.\"\n\nFour voices said four places. Then he moved, and nobody else did.',NULL),(1625,74,6,NULL,'Lola asked about this afterwards. He said that most people get hurt in a power cut in the first two minutes, walking into things they know are there.','🗣️'),(1626,74,7,'The drawer','There is a drawer in the kitchen with a torch in it. There is a torch in that drawer in almost every house in the country.',NULL),(1627,74,8,NULL,'In almost every house in the country, the batteries in it are flat.',NULL),(1628,74,9,NULL,'Theirs worked, because Lola\'s mother checks it in October, which Lola had always thought was a strange thing to do and now does herself.','🔦'),(1629,74,10,'Candles are not the answer','They lit two candles, in jars, on plates, in the middle of the table. Not on the windowsill, and not in the bedrooms.',NULL),(1630,74,11,NULL,'A candle is nice and a candle is also a fire that you have agreed to have in your house.',NULL),(1631,74,12,NULL,'Her mother put a torch in every bedroom instead, which was dull and correct.','🕯️'),(1632,74,13,'Warm','The heating was off and the house was already going cold by seven.',NULL),(1633,74,14,NULL,'What they did, which Lola thought was wrong at the time, was shut almost all of it.',NULL),(1634,74,15,NULL,'Doors closed. Curtains closed. Everybody into the front room with the blankets off all four beds.\n\nOne small room with five people in it is much easier to keep warm than a house.','🚪'),(1635,74,16,'Food','The fridge stayed shut all night, which was the hardest rule.',NULL),(1636,74,17,NULL,'A fridge that is not opened stays cold for most of a day. A fridge that is opened eleven times does not.',NULL),(1637,74,18,NULL,'They ate bread and cheese and a packet of biscuits and the last four apples, by candlelight, which Lola now rates as one of the best meals of her life.','🥪'),(1638,74,19,'Next door','At about eight her mother went next door and knocked.',NULL),(1639,74,20,NULL,'Mrs Teague is eighty-six and lives alone and had been sitting in the dark for two hours without ringing anybody, because she did not want to be a bother.',NULL),(1640,74,21,NULL,'She came and sat in the front room with the five of them until half past eleven.\n\nLola has thought about the two hours a great deal since.','🏠'),(1641,74,22,'After','The power came back at twenty to twelve and everything in the house beeped at once.',NULL),(1642,74,23,NULL,'There is a list on the inside of the kitchen cupboard now. Lola wrote it and her father corrected the spelling.','✅'),(1643,74,24,NULL,'STAY STILL AND SAY WHERE YOU ARE.\nTORCH, NOT CANDLES, IN BEDROOMS.\nONE ROOM, ALL THE BLANKETS.\nDO NOT OPEN THE FRIDGE.\nGO AND KNOCK NEXT DOOR.',NULL),(1644,74,25,NULL,'The last one is underlined. That was not her father\'s idea.',NULL),(1645,74,26,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1646,75,1,'A pirate with a clipboard','Captain Bartholomew Nibbs was a pirate, which he was very clear about, and polite, which confused everybody.\n\nHe had a ship, a crew of eleven, a parrot with opinions, and a clipboard.','🏴‍☠️'),(1647,75,2,NULL,'The clipboard was the problem.\n\nWhen the Nibbs ship pulled alongside yours, Bartholomew would come aboard, remove his hat, and ask whether now was a good time.',NULL),(1648,75,3,NULL,'\"We are here to rob you,\" he would explain. \"Would you prefer the gold or the cargo? Some people are quite attached to one.\"','📋'),(1649,75,4,'Why this is bad for business','Piracy, it turns out, runs almost entirely on people being frightened.\n\nA frightened crew hands things over. A crew that has been asked politely goes and finds the captain, and the captain asks questions, and then it is a meeting.',NULL),(1650,75,5,NULL,'The Nibbs ship had been in forty-one meetings and had robbed nobody at all.',NULL),(1651,75,6,NULL,'The parrot, whose name was Dreadful and who had served under a previous and far nastier captain, said what everybody was thinking.\n\n\"You are the worst pirate in the entire sea.\"\n\n\"I am consistent,\" said Bartholomew.','🦜'),(1652,75,7,'The Marigold','On the forty-second attempt they pulled alongside a merchant ship called the Marigold.\n\nBartholomew removed his hat and asked whether now was a good time.',NULL),(1653,75,8,NULL,'The captain of the Marigold was a woman named Ada Rennick who had been at sea for thirty years and had never been asked anything by a pirate.',NULL),(1654,75,9,NULL,'\"No,\" she said. \"It is a terrible time. We have a hold full of flour and a leak in the forward bilge and I have not slept.\"\n\n\"Ah,\" said Bartholomew. \"Then we shall come back.\"','⚓'),(1655,75,10,NULL,'\"Come back?\"\n\n\"Thursday?\"',NULL),(1656,75,11,'What happened instead','Bartholomew Nibbs looked at the Marigold properly while he waited for an answer, which no pirate has ever done.\n\nHe noticed the list to starboard. He noticed the pump going. He noticed eleven exhausted people.',NULL),(1657,75,12,NULL,'\"How bad is the leak?\"\n\n\"Bad.\"\n\n\"We have a carpenter,\" said Bartholomew. \"He is the second-best thing on this ship. The first is the parrot, and she will tell you so herself.\"','🛠️'),(1658,75,13,NULL,'It took the Nibbs crew nine hours to fix the forward bilge of the Marigold.\n\nThey robbed nothing, because nobody got round to it.',NULL),(1659,75,14,'The arrangement','Ada Rennick, who was practical, made a suggestion at four in the morning over the worst tea Bartholomew had ever been given.',NULL),(1660,75,15,NULL,'\"You are hopeless at robbing,\" she said. \"You are extremely good at fixing. There are two hundred ships in these waters and not one dry dock for eleven days\' sail.\"','🤝'),(1661,75,16,NULL,'\"You are suggesting,\" said Bartholomew slowly, \"that we stop being pirates.\"\n\n\"I am suggesting you start being paid.\"',NULL),(1662,75,17,'Dreadful objects','The parrot objected for most of a week.\n\n\"We are PIRATES,\" said Dreadful. \"We have a FLAG.\"\n\n\"We can keep the flag,\" said Bartholomew.',NULL),(1663,75,18,NULL,'\"A flag is not a job,\" said Dreadful, who was wiser than her vocabulary suggested.\n\n\"No,\" Bartholomew agreed. \"But forty-one meetings is not a job either, and we have had those.\"','🏴'),(1664,75,19,'The yard at Cold Nose','They run a boatyard now, in a cove at Cold Nose, and they are the best in four hundred miles.\n\nThey still have the ship. They still have the flag, which flies over the yard and brings in a surprising amount of business.',NULL),(1665,75,20,NULL,'Bartholomew still removes his hat. He still asks whether now is a good time. It turns out that is an excellent way to run a boatyard and a hopeless way to run a robbery.','⛵'),(1666,75,21,NULL,'The clipboard is still in use. It now says ESTIMATE at the top, which Dreadful considers the single greatest defeat in the history of piracy.',NULL),(1667,75,22,NULL,'The end.','🦜'),(1668,76,1,'The morning of the speech','On the first of May the Queen of Ravenshold gives a speech from the east balcony.\n\nIt is nine pages long. She has given it for twenty-two years. Nobody has ever listened to the whole thing.','👑'),(1669,76,2,NULL,'On the first of May in the twenty-third year, the Queen woke up, opened her mouth, and nothing came out at all.\n\nNot a croak. Not a squeak. Nothing.',NULL),(1670,76,3,NULL,'She rang the bell. She wrote on a card: MY VOICE HAS GONE.\n\nThe physician looked in her throat for some time and wrote back, on the same card: YES.','🤫'),(1671,76,4,'Nine pages','The Lord Chamberlain suggested cancelling. The Queen shook her head.\n\nHe suggested he read it for her. She shook her head harder, which he had not expected and did not understand.',NULL),(1672,76,5,NULL,'What the Lord Chamberlain did not know was that the Queen had never liked the speech.\n\nShe had inherited it, along with the balcony, and it was nine pages of words about wheat.',NULL),(1673,76,6,NULL,'She sat with it for an hour. Then she took a pen and crossed out eight and a half pages.','📜'),(1674,76,7,'The balcony','At noon the square was full, because it is always full, because there is a fair afterwards.\n\nThe Queen came out. The crowd did the noise crowds do. She raised her hand and it stopped.',NULL),(1675,76,8,NULL,'And then she said nothing, because she could not.',NULL),(1676,76,9,NULL,'For eleven seconds the square was the quietest it had been since it was built.\n\nThen the Queen held up a card, and on it, in very large letters, was the half page she had kept.','🏰'),(1677,76,10,NULL,'It said: WHAT DO YOU NEED?',NULL),(1678,76,11,'Nobody had asked','There was a pause of the kind that happens when people are not sure a thing is really happening.\n\nThen a woman near the front said, \"The bridge at Cold Beck is down.\"',NULL),(1679,76,12,NULL,'And then everybody said something at once, which was useless, and then the Lord Chamberlain found a table and some paper, which was not.','🗣️'),(1680,76,13,NULL,'They were there until dark.\n\nThe Queen wrote nothing. She pointed at people, and the clerks wrote, and when somebody had finished she pointed at the next one.',NULL),(1681,76,14,'What was on the list','Forty-one things.\n\nA bridge. Two wells. A school roof. A midwife for the north villages. A man who wanted his neighbour\'s goat dealt with, which went on the list anyway because everything did.',NULL),(1682,76,15,NULL,'Eleven of them the Queen had never heard of.\n\nFour of them she had been told were already fixed, years ago, by people who had wanted to bring her good news instead of true news.','📋'),(1683,76,16,'The voice comes back','It came back on the ninth day, in the morning, croaky and unreliable.\n\nThe Queen tried it out on the cat and then sat on the end of her bed for a while, thinking.',NULL),(1684,76,17,NULL,'Then she wrote one more card, for the Lord Chamberlain, who was not going to like it.\n\nIt said: NEXT YEAR, NO SPEECH. BRING THE TABLE.',NULL),(1685,76,18,NULL,'\"Ma\'am,\" he said, \"it is tradition.\"\n\n\"It was nine pages about wheat,\" said the Queen, in her croaky unreliable voice, \"and I have been giving it to people who needed a bridge.\"','🪑'),(1686,76,19,'After','They bring the table out every first of May now. The Queen sits at it and says very little.',NULL),(1687,76,20,NULL,'The fair still happens afterwards. The bridge at Cold Beck was rebuilt in the first year, the wells in the second, the school roof in the third.',NULL),(1688,76,21,NULL,'The goat was never dealt with. Some things are beyond the reach of any crown.','🌉'),(1689,76,22,NULL,'And on the wall of the east balcony, where the nine pages used to be kept in a drawer, there is a card in a frame.\n\nIt says: WHAT DO YOU NEED?',NULL),(1690,76,23,NULL,'The end.','👑'),(1691,10,1,'After six','The museum closes at six and the arguing starts at about ten past.','🏛️'),(1692,10,2,NULL,'It is not loud. Nothing in a museum is loud. It is the sound of several hundred objects being extremely pointed with one another.',NULL),(1693,10,3,NULL,'Esi Danquah found this out because her mother was the night cleaner and because Esi was eleven and had nowhere else to be on a Thursday.','🗿'),(1694,10,4,'The dispute','The argument is always the same argument and it has been going since 1931.',NULL),(1695,10,5,NULL,'Which century was the best.',NULL),(1696,10,6,NULL,'The Roman gallery says the second. The medieval gallery says the fourteenth, unconvincingly, and mostly to be difficult.','⚱️'),(1697,10,7,NULL,'The Victorian gallery says the nineteenth and says it constantly and is, by common agreement, insufferable.',NULL),(1698,10,8,'What each one claims','Esi started writing the claims down, because they repeated, and because repeated things can be checked.',NULL),(1699,10,9,NULL,'ROMANS: roads, water, the alphabet you are using.\nMEDIEVAL: the university, the clock, the plough that actually worked.\nVICTORIANS: the railway, the sewer, the photograph, the postage stamp, and they will not stop.','📓'),(1700,10,10,'The thing nobody said','After four Thursdays Esi noticed a hole in every single argument and it took her two more to work out what it was.',NULL),(1701,10,11,NULL,'Not one of them mentioned a person.',NULL),(1702,10,12,NULL,'Roads, water, clocks, railways, sewers. All things. All of them described as having simply arrived.','👤'),(1703,10,13,'What she asked','On the seventh Thursday Esi asked a question out loud, which she had not done before, and the whole first floor went quiet.',NULL),(1704,10,14,NULL,'\"Who made you?\"',NULL),(1705,10,15,NULL,'The Roman milestone said it did not know. It had been cut by somebody, obviously, and nobody had written the name on it, because you do not sign a milestone.','🔨'),(1706,10,16,NULL,'The medieval clock mechanism knew the name of the man who commissioned it and not the name of the four who built it.',NULL),(1707,10,17,'The Victorian gallery','The Victorian gallery, which had been insufferable for ninety-five years, had the worst answer of all.',NULL),(1708,10,18,NULL,'It had a model locomotive with a brass plate listing a designer, a works, and a date.','🚂'),(1709,10,19,NULL,'And it had, in a drawer underneath that nobody had opened since 1954, the wage book of the works for 1863, with four hundred and six names in it.',NULL),(1710,10,20,'What Esi did with that','She asked her mother to ask the curator whether the drawer could be opened properly.',NULL),(1711,10,21,NULL,'It took nine months, two letters, and a volunteer who typed the names up on evenings over a winter.','🗄️'),(1712,10,22,NULL,'Four hundred and six names, with jobs. Fitters, turners, boilermakers, and forty-one boys listed only as \"boy\".',NULL),(1713,10,23,'The new label','The locomotive has two labels now.',NULL),(1714,10,24,NULL,'The brass one, which says the designer and the works and the date.','🪧'),(1715,10,25,NULL,'And a printed one beside it, which lists the trades and the numbers, and ends: AND FORTY-ONE BOYS, WHOSE NAMES ARE IN THE WAGE BOOK AND WHOSE JOBS WERE NOT RECORDED.',NULL),(1716,10,26,'After','The argument still happens. It is a museum; it has nothing else to do.',NULL),(1717,10,27,NULL,'But since the new label went up, the Victorian gallery has stopped winning it, and has become noticeably easier to be around.',NULL),(1718,10,28,NULL,'Esi is fourteen and volunteers on Saturdays. She has asked the same question in four other galleries and got four unsatisfactory answers, and she is keeping a list.','🤫'),(1719,10,29,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1720,77,1,'Played eleven, lost eleven','Thwaite Under-Nines had played eleven and lost eleven.\n\nGoals for: four. Goals against: sixty-one.','⚽'),(1721,77,2,NULL,'Toma Reyes was the worst player on it, which he knew, and which nobody had to tell him.',NULL),(1722,77,3,NULL,'He was slow, he could not turn, and he had never scored anything in his life.','😟'),(1723,77,4,'What he could do','He could run in a straight line for a long time without stopping.',NULL),(1724,77,5,NULL,'That is it. That is the entire skill. He had discovered it at sports day, coming last in the sprint and third in the eight hundred.',NULL),(1725,77,6,NULL,'Nobody in football cares about that, because football is a game of short bursts and turns.','🏃'),(1726,77,7,'Watching from the bench','Toma was on the bench a great deal, which is the one advantage of being the worst player: you watch.',NULL),(1727,77,8,NULL,'And from the bench he noticed something about the eleven games that nobody on the pitch had noticed.',NULL),(1728,77,9,NULL,'Every time Thwaite lost the ball, nine players stopped. For about two seconds, all nine stood still and looked at where the ball had gone.','👀'),(1729,77,10,NULL,'Two seconds is enough for a decent team to be through and shooting.',NULL),(1730,77,11,'The job nobody wanted','Toma asked the coach for a job, which took him four weeks to work up to.',NULL),(1731,77,12,NULL,'\"When we lose it,\" he said, \"I want to be the one who runs back. Every time. I do not want to do anything else.\"','🗣️'),(1732,77,13,NULL,'\"That is not a position.\"\n\n\"I know. But I can run back for a whole game and nobody else can.\"',NULL),(1733,77,14,'Game twelve','They lost four–one. Which was, by the standards of that season, a triumph.',NULL),(1734,77,15,NULL,'Toma ran back eleven times. He did not tackle anybody. He did not need to. He simply arrived, in the way, while nine other children were still looking at where the ball had gone.',NULL),(1735,77,16,NULL,'Three of those eleven times, the other team\'s attack slowed down, and somebody quicker than Toma got there and took the ball.','🛡️'),(1736,77,17,'What the coach said','At the end she asked the team who had played well.',NULL),(1737,77,18,NULL,'Everybody said the boy who scored, which was correct, because he had scored.',NULL),(1738,77,19,NULL,'\"And Toma,\" she said. \"Toma made three of their attacks stop. He did not touch the ball once. Look at the sheet.\"','📋'),(1739,77,20,NULL,'There is a line on the sheet called RECOVERY RUNS. Nobody had ever filled it in.',NULL),(1740,77,21,'After','Thwaite finished the season having lost fifteen of eighteen, which is not a fairytale.',NULL),(1741,77,22,NULL,'Goals against in the first eleven games: sixty-one.\nGoals against in the last seven: nineteen.','📈'),(1742,77,23,NULL,'Toma has still never scored. He has sixty-eight recovery runs written down in a notebook, which he counts himself, because nobody else does.',NULL),(1743,77,24,NULL,'He is not the worst player on the team any more. He is, by a distance, the fittest, and that happened entirely by accident while he was doing the job nobody wanted.',NULL),(1744,77,25,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1745,78,1,'Weather by the bottle','The shop on Caddis Lane has a green door and sells weather.\n\nNot forecasts. Weather, in bottles, with labels written by hand.','🌧️'),(1746,78,2,NULL,'A GOOD MORNING IN MARCH. FOG THAT LIFTS BY TEN. FIRST SNOW (LIGHT).\n\nThey are not cheap and they do not last.',NULL),(1747,78,3,NULL,'Juno Pike had been saving for eleven weeks. She had four pounds and forty pence, which was enough for one bottle of the second-cheapest thing in the shop.','🏷️'),(1748,78,4,'What she wanted it for','Her grandfather had not been outside since February.\n\nHe said it was his knees. It was partly his knees.',NULL),(1749,78,5,NULL,'What he said, when he said anything, was: \"It has not properly rained since I stopped going out.\"',NULL),(1750,78,6,NULL,'He had been a farmer for fifty years. He had spent fifty years being rained on and complaining about it, and now it had been dry for four months and he was sitting by a window.','🪟'),(1751,78,7,'In the shop','The shopkeeper was mostly hat.\n\n\"Rain,\" said Juno. \"An afternoon of it. Proper rain, not drizzle.\"',NULL),(1752,78,8,NULL,'\"That is six pounds.\"\n\n\"I have four forty.\"',NULL),(1753,78,9,NULL,'The hat considered her.\n\n\"What is it for?\"\n\nJuno told it. It took four minutes and she had to stop twice.','💰'),(1754,78,10,'The wrong bottle','The shopkeeper did not give her a discount, which Juno had been quietly hoping for.',NULL),(1755,78,11,NULL,'Instead the hat reached up to a high shelf and took down a small bottle with no label at all, and put it on the counter.',NULL),(1756,78,12,NULL,'\"That is four forty.\"\n\n\"What is it?\"\n\n\"Twenty minutes of the sound. No water.\"','🫙'),(1757,78,13,NULL,'\"The sound of rain?\"\n\n\"On a tin roof. There is a shed in that garden with a tin roof, is there not?\"\n\nThere was. Juno had not mentioned it.',NULL),(1758,78,14,'Why sound','Juno argued. She had saved eleven weeks for rain and was being sold a noise.',NULL),(1759,78,15,NULL,'\"He is eighty-four,\" said the hat, \"and you want to put him in an afternoon of proper rain. With those knees. On wet grass.\"','🤨'),(1760,78,16,NULL,'Juno had not thought about the grass.',NULL),(1761,78,17,'Saturday','She opened it in the garden at four o\'clock with the back door open and her grandfather in his chair just inside it.',NULL),(1762,78,18,NULL,'Twenty minutes of rain on a tin roof, in a dry garden, under a clear sky.','🏚️'),(1763,78,19,NULL,'It is a very particular noise. It is not like rain on anything else.',NULL),(1764,78,20,'What he said','Nothing, for about eleven minutes.',NULL),(1765,78,21,NULL,'Then: \"That is the lambing shed.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"That is the exact roof. Nineteen sixty-four to two thousand and six. I have sat under that forty times with a torch in my teeth.\"','🧓'),(1766,78,22,NULL,'He talked for two hours afterwards, which he had not done since February, about things Juno had never heard and has written most of down since.',NULL),(1767,78,23,'After','He got as far as the shed itself in May, with a stick and a great deal of complaining, and sat in it for half an hour in real rain.',NULL),(1768,78,24,NULL,'Juno is saving again. Nine weeks so far.','☔'),(1769,78,25,NULL,'She is not buying rain this time. She has worked out that what she is actually shopping for is something with a tin roof, and that the shop on Caddis Lane does not stock those.',NULL),(1770,78,26,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1771,79,1,'Eleven times','Everyone in Thwaite has seen the white fox.\n\nThat is the whole point of her. You see her once, and it means something good is coming, and then you talk about it for the rest of your life.','🦊'),(1772,79,2,NULL,'Jonah Pike has seen her eleven times.\n\nHe has not told anybody. Once is lucky. Eleven times is a problem, and he worked that out before he was nine.',NULL),(1773,79,3,NULL,'He keeps a map on the back of his door. It is drawn in pencil, because it keeps changing, and it has eleven small crosses on it.\n\nThe crosses are not spread about the way an animal\'s territory would be. They are in a line.',NULL),(1774,79,4,NULL,'The line runs from the top of Silver Wood, down the beck, past the quarry, and along the back of the Harlows\' field.\n\nIt stops at the road. It has stopped at the road for two winters.','🗺️'),(1775,79,5,'What a line means','Jonah\'s aunt keeps sheep and knows animals the way other people know their own kitchen.\n\n\"A line,\" she said, when he asked a careful question that did not mention foxes at all. \"A line means it is going somewhere. Animals do not wander in lines.\"',NULL),(1776,79,6,NULL,'\"And if it keeps stopping at the same place?\"\n\n\"Then it cannot get past, and it will keep trying, and one day it will try at the wrong moment.\" His aunt shook out a feed bag. \"That is not luck. That is a creature in trouble.\"',NULL),(1777,79,7,NULL,'Jonah went up to his room and looked at the eleven crosses for a long time.\n\nThen he drew a twelfth, where the road was, and he put a circle round it instead of a cross, because he did not want to think about what the cross would mean.','🐑'),(1778,79,8,'The far side','Here is what is on the far side of the road.\n\nMore wood. The same wood, in fact — Silver Wood used to run all the way to Cold Beck before the road went through it in 1974, which Jonah found in a library book with a map folded into the back.',NULL),(1779,79,9,NULL,'In the library book, the wood is one shape.\n\nOn his door it is two, with a grey line between them, and eleven crosses pressed up against the grey line like somebody knocking.',NULL),(1780,79,10,NULL,'He went and stood at the road on a Saturday and counted cars for an hour.\n\nFourteen. Not many. But they came round the bend at the quarry fast, and there was nowhere to see them from, and nothing to stand behind.','🛣️'),(1781,79,11,'The letter','Jonah wrote to the council.\n\nHe wrote it four times. The first one said *please help the fox*, and he threw it away, because his aunt says you do not get anywhere asking people to care about things.',NULL),(1782,79,12,NULL,'The fourth one had the map copied out in biro, and the eleven dates, and the car count, and the page number of the library book.\n\nIt asked one question: was there a pipe under the road, and if so, where did it come out?',NULL),(1783,79,13,NULL,'A woman called Ada Rennick wrote back.\n\nShe was not from the council. The council had passed the letter to the wildlife trust, and the wildlife trust had passed it to Ada, who had been asking the same question for eleven years.','✉️'),(1784,79,14,NULL,'\"There is a pipe,\" she wrote. \"A culvert for the beck. It is blocked at the north end and has been since the floods. I could not get anyone to look at it because I could not prove anything used it.\"\n\nThe next line Jonah has kept.\n\n\"You have just proved it.\"',NULL),(1785,79,15,'The culvert','They went down to it on the first Saturday in February, Ada and Jonah and his aunt, who came to hold the torch and to be unimpressed, which is her way of being supportive.',NULL),(1786,79,16,NULL,'The culvert was a stone mouth in the bank, half the height of a door, and the north end was packed solid with branches, a fertiliser sack, and twenty years of silt.\n\nOn the silt, at the edge of the water, there were tracks.','🔦'),(1787,79,17,NULL,'Small. Neat. Four toes and a pad, in a line so straight it looked ruled.\n\nThey went right up to the blockage. They turned round. They went back.',NULL),(1788,79,18,NULL,'\"How many times?\" said Jonah.\n\nAda crouched with the torch. \"Dozens. She comes here most nights. This is her door and it will not open.\"',NULL),(1789,79,19,'Four Saturdays','It took four Saturdays to clear it, and nine people, and a tractor on the last day for the fertiliser sack, which had become one object with the bank.\n\nJonah was there for all four. He is in the photograph, at the front, holding a branch twice his length.','🪓'),(1790,79,20,NULL,'The trust put a camera on a post at the south end.\n\nIt took four hundred pictures of nothing, because that is what wildlife cameras mostly do, and Jonah looked at every single one.',NULL),(1791,79,21,NULL,'Picture three hundred and eighty-one is a wood pigeon.\n\nPicture three hundred and eighty-two is the back half of something white, going out of frame fast, at 03:12 on the fourteenth of March.','📷'),(1792,79,22,NULL,'Picture three hundred and eighty-three is nothing again.\n\nJonah printed out three hundred and eighty-two and it is on the back of his door now, over the map, held up with the same drawing pin.',NULL),(1793,79,23,'Twelve','He has seen her once more since. Just once, in May, on the far side, which is the whole point.\n\nShe was not going anywhere. She was sitting in the sun at the top of Cold Beck like an animal with nothing left to try.',NULL),(1794,79,24,NULL,'That is twelve. Jonah has stopped counting, because it is not a count any more, it is just a fox who lives in a wood that goes all the way to the beck again.','🌲'),(1795,79,25,'After','People in Thwaite still say you see the white fox once in your life, and it means something good is coming.\n\nJonah does not correct them. It would take too long, and he would have to start with 1974.',NULL),(1796,79,26,NULL,'But if you ask him, he will tell you the useful version.\n\nSeeing a thing once is luck. Seeing it eleven times and drawing it on a map is something else entirely, and anybody can do it, and almost nobody does.',NULL),(1797,79,27,NULL,'The end.','❄️'),(1798,80,1,'One spell each','At Ravenhill you get one spell on your ninth birthday. One. For life.\n\nNobody chooses. The spell chooses, which the school describes as a wonderful mystery and the children describe as a lottery.','✨'),(1799,80,2,NULL,'Ivo got flight, which is the one everybody wants.\n\nPriya got speaking to animals, which is the one everybody says they want and which Priya says is mostly complaining.',NULL),(1800,80,3,NULL,'Wes Oduya got the ability to make small objects slightly warmer.','🎂'),(1801,80,4,'Slightly','Not hot. Warmer. About four degrees, over roughly a minute, on anything he could hold in one hand.',NULL),(1802,80,5,NULL,'The examiner wrote it down, checked it twice, and then wrote it down again in a different box, in case the first box had been a mistake.',NULL),(1803,80,6,NULL,'Wes spent his ninth birthday warming a teaspoon in front of forty relatives.','🥄'),(1804,80,7,'The bottom set','Ravenhill sorts by spell. There are four sets and Wes was in the fourth, which the school calls Practical Applications and everybody else calls the bottom.',NULL),(1805,80,8,NULL,'In it were: a boy who could make string slightly tidier, a girl who could tell when bread was stale without touching it, and a boy whose spell made nearby pigeons marginally less confident.',NULL),(1806,80,9,NULL,'They were not bitter about it, mostly, and when they were, they were bitter together, which is a kind of luck.','🧵'),(1807,80,10,'Year four','For three years Wes warmed teaspoons. Then the winter of his twelfth year happened.',NULL),(1808,80,11,NULL,'The boiler at Ravenhill failed on the coldest night in forty years, and the school had two hundred children and no heating and a road that was shut.',NULL),(1809,80,12,NULL,'The first set could fly. The second could move objects. The third could make light.\n\nNone of those are heat.','❄️'),(1810,80,13,'What the bottom set did','Wes could warm one small object by four degrees in a minute.\n\nThere were two hundred children, and each of them had a water bottle, and a bottle is a small object.',NULL),(1811,80,14,NULL,'Four degrees is nothing. Four degrees on a litre of water in a bottle inside a sleeping bag, replaced every two hours, is the difference between a cold night and a dangerous one.',NULL),(1812,80,15,NULL,'He did two hundred and eleven bottles between nine at night and six in the morning, with two breaks, and his hands shook for a day afterwards.','🍼'),(1813,80,16,'And the others','The girl who could tell when bread was stale went through the entire kitchen in eleven minutes and found four crates nobody knew were still good.',NULL),(1814,80,17,NULL,'The boy who could make string tidier spliced the broken pulley line on the water tank, which the caretaker said would have taken him two hours in the dark.',NULL),(1815,80,18,NULL,'The pigeon boy was, admittedly, no use at all that night. He has never claimed otherwise and the others have never let him forget it.','🕯️'),(1816,80,19,'What changed','Not very much, honestly, and this is the part Wes insists on when he tells it.',NULL),(1817,80,20,NULL,'The sets are still sorted the same way. Flight is still the one everybody wants.',NULL),(1818,80,21,NULL,'But there is a laminated sheet in the school office now, on the wall by the emergency numbers, that lists every pupil and what their spell can actually do.','📋'),(1819,80,22,NULL,'Not what set it is in. What it can do.\n\nWes wrote the first version of it when he was twelve. It is on its fourth revision and somebody in the bottom set has updated it every year since.',NULL),(1820,80,23,'After','Wes is sixteen. He still makes small objects slightly warmer. It is still, by any measure the school uses, the least impressive spell on the register.',NULL),(1821,80,24,NULL,'He is also the only person at Ravenhill who has ever been thanked by two hundred people in one night, and he is extremely annoying about it.','☕'),(1822,80,25,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1823,81,1,'The gather','Twice a year the farms around Harrow Fell gather the sheep off the open hill.\n\nIt takes nine people, four dogs, and from half past five in the morning until dark.','🐑'),(1824,81,2,NULL,'Erin Pike was eleven and had been asking to come since she was six.',NULL),(1825,81,3,NULL,'This year she was allowed, on three conditions: boots, no complaining, and she did exactly what she was told.','🌄'),(1826,81,4,'The job nobody wants','She was given the gate.',NULL),(1827,81,5,NULL,'Standing at a gate, for hours, in the wind, doing nothing.',NULL),(1828,81,6,NULL,'Erin said — and she is not proud of this — \"Is that it?\"\n\nHer grandmother said: \"That is it. And if you leave it, we lose the day.\"','🚪'),(1829,81,7,'How a gather works','You do not push sheep. You cannot. Three hundred sheep pushed go in three hundred directions.',NULL),(1830,81,8,NULL,'You make a shape with people and dogs, open on one side, and you move the shape very slowly, and the sheep drift into the open side because it is the easiest way to go.',NULL),(1831,81,9,NULL,'The dogs are not chasing. A good dog works at a distance and lies down more than it runs.','🐕'),(1832,81,10,'Nine hours at a gate','Erin stood at the gate from seven in the morning until twenty past four.',NULL),(1833,81,11,NULL,'For six of those hours she could not see a single sheep. She could see weather, and a wall, and a buzzard.',NULL),(1834,81,12,NULL,'She ate four sandwiches and a bar of chocolate and had an argument with herself about whether this counted as being included.','🧤'),(1835,81,13,'Twenty past four','At twenty past four about two hundred sheep came over the shoulder of the fell at speed, in one body, with two dogs behind them.',NULL),(1836,81,14,NULL,'They went through the gate she was holding in about ninety seconds.',NULL),(1837,81,15,NULL,'Her entire job, after nine hours, was ninety seconds long.','💨'),(1838,81,16,'The forty that went wrong','Behind them, about forty sheep broke left, away from the gate, towards the road.',NULL),(1839,81,17,NULL,'This is what gates are for. Erin had been told four times what to do and had assumed she would never have to.',NULL),(1840,81,18,NULL,'She shut the gate, which is counter-intuitive and correct — an open gate behind a break is how the gathered ones get out again — and then she ran up the wall line waving a stick and shouting.','🏃'),(1841,81,19,NULL,'Forty sheep turned. Not because of her. Because a person on a skyline is enough of a wrong thing to make a sheep choose the other way.',NULL),(1842,81,20,'What her grandmother said','At the pens, in the dark, with tea, her grandmother said one sentence about it and then talked about something else for forty minutes.',NULL),(1843,81,21,NULL,'\"You shut the gate first.\"','🫖'),(1844,81,22,NULL,'Erin has thought about that sentence more than any praise she has ever been given.',NULL),(1845,81,23,'After','She has done seven gathers now. She has worked a dog for the last three, badly, improving.',NULL),(1846,81,24,NULL,'She still gets the gate sometimes, when there is somebody new who needs the easier job.','🥾'),(1847,81,25,NULL,'She does not describe it as the easier job to them. She describes it the way it was described to her, which is: if you leave it, we lose the day.',NULL),(1848,81,26,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1849,82,1,'Seventeen hands','Thunder was seventeen hands high, which is as tall as a doorway, and he had never once been any trouble.','🐎'),(1850,82,2,NULL,'He pulled, he stood, he waited, he came when called. Rosa had been leading him since she was five and he had never pulled the rope out of her hand.',NULL),(1851,82,3,NULL,'Then one Tuesday in August he would not go into the hay barn.','🧺'),(1852,82,4,'Being difficult','He stopped four metres from the door, put his head up, and would not move.',NULL),(1853,82,5,NULL,'Rosa\'s uncle pulled. Thunder did not move. Her uncle pulled harder, said a word, and fetched a bucket of feed, which is the farm equivalent of a bribe and usually works on everything.',NULL),(1854,82,6,NULL,'Thunder looked at the bucket. He looked at the barn. He did not move.','🪣'),(1855,82,7,NULL,'\"He is being difficult,\" said her uncle, which is what people say when an animal disagrees with them.',NULL),(1856,82,8,'What Rosa noticed','Rosa noticed three things in the next ten minutes and wrote them on her hand because she had no paper.',NULL),(1857,82,9,NULL,'One: he was not frightened of the door. He had put his head through the door and then taken it back out.\n\nTwo: his ears were forward, not back. Forward is listening. Back is frightened.',NULL),(1858,82,10,NULL,'Three: he kept turning his head to the left, towards the far end of the barn, where the bales were stacked to the roof.','👂'),(1859,82,11,'The far end','Rosa went in on her own, which she was not supposed to do, and which she would do again.',NULL),(1860,82,12,NULL,'The far end of the barn was eleven bales high and smelled wrong.\n\nNot rotten. Warm. Like the inside of a coat.',NULL),(1861,82,13,NULL,'She put her hand flat on the stack at shoulder height and it was hot.','🌡️'),(1862,82,14,'Why hay gets hot','Her grandfather explained it afterwards, at length, having been the only person who was not surprised.',NULL),(1863,82,15,NULL,'Hay baled even slightly damp keeps living for a while. Bacteria in it generate heat. In a big tight stack the heat cannot escape.',NULL),(1864,82,16,NULL,'It can reach seventy degrees. Above about eighty it can ignite on its own, with no spark and nobody near it.\n\nIt is called spontaneous combustion and it burns more barns than any other cause.','🔥'),(1865,82,17,'What they did','A thermometer on a long rod went into the stack at four points. The hottest read seventy-four degrees.',NULL),(1866,82,18,NULL,'Nine neighbours came. They took the stack apart from the top in the dark, bale by bale, and spread it out across two fields to cool.',NULL),(1867,82,19,NULL,'Four bales in the middle were brown and smoking faintly when they came out.','🚜'),(1868,82,20,'What Thunder knew','Rosa has thought about this a great deal and she is careful about how she says it.',NULL),(1869,82,21,NULL,'Thunder did not know the barn was going to catch fire. Horses do not know things like that.',NULL),(1870,82,22,NULL,'He knew that the far end of a building he had walked into four hundred times smelled different and sounded different, because a hot stack ticks and settles.\n\nAnd he declined to walk past it. That is all. That is enough.','🐴'),(1871,82,23,'After','The farm checks hay temperature now, three times in the first six weeks, with a rod and a written log.',NULL),(1872,82,24,NULL,'Rosa\'s uncle built the rod himself and does not mention who was right, which Rosa says is fine and which she brings up approximately once a month.','📋'),(1873,82,25,NULL,'And there is a line in the farm notebook, in her grandfather\'s handwriting, that she is extremely proud of.\n\nIF AN ANIMAL WILL NOT GO SOMEWHERE, GO AND LOOK.',NULL),(1874,82,26,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1875,83,1,'Six in the morning','The truck stopped at four minutes past six, ninety miles from the nearest road with a name.\n\nSol was thirteen. Her aunt Juno was driving and had been for nine hours.','🏜️'),(1876,83,2,NULL,'The engine did not splutter. It simply stopped, the way a thing stops when a belt has gone, and the silence afterwards was enormous.',NULL),(1877,83,3,'The first decision','Juno got out, looked under the bonnet for ninety seconds, and said a word Sol was not supposed to know.\n\n\"Can you fix it?\"\n\n\"Not without a part we do not have.\"',NULL),(1878,83,4,NULL,'Then she said the thing that mattered, and she said it carefully, because she knew it was the only decision of the day.','🔧'),(1879,83,5,NULL,'\"We stay with the truck.\"',NULL),(1880,83,6,'Why you stay with the truck','Sol wanted to walk. Everybody wants to walk. Walking feels like doing something, and sitting feels like giving up.',NULL),(1881,83,7,NULL,'Here is why you stay, and Juno made her repeat it back.\n\nA truck is visible from the air. A person is not.\n\nA truck carries water. A person carries what they can lift.\n\nA truck is where people will look, because it is where the vehicle was last reported.',NULL),(1882,83,8,NULL,'People die two miles from their cars in this country every year. They die walking for help that was already coming to the place they left.','🚙'),(1883,83,9,'Water','They had eleven litres.\n\nJuno worked it out loud so Sol could follow: two people, one hot day, resting in shade — about four litres each if they were sensible. Eleven was enough for a day and a night with room to spare.',NULL),(1884,83,10,NULL,'They did not ration it hard, which surprised Sol.\n\n\"Thirst is not something you win by being brave about,\" said Juno. \"Dehydrated people make bad decisions. We need the decisions more than we need the spare litre.\"',NULL),(1885,83,11,'Shade','Air temperature that day reached forty-one degrees. The ground reached about sixty-five.',NULL),(1886,83,12,NULL,'Inside the truck was worse than outside — a closed vehicle in that sun gets hotter than the air around it, fast.','☀️'),(1887,83,13,NULL,'So they made shade beside it: a tarpaulin off the roof rack, tied to the door and two poles, a foot off the ground so air could move underneath.\n\nSitting a foot above the ground instead of on it is worth several degrees. Nobody thinks of it.',NULL),(1888,83,14,'The middle of the day','From ten until four they did almost nothing, and that was the plan.',NULL),(1889,83,15,NULL,'Sol found the doing-nothing harder than anything else. She asked three times whether they should try something.\n\n\"We are trying something,\" said Juno. \"We are trying not to sweat.\"','😴'),(1890,83,16,NULL,'A person working in that heat loses a litre an hour. A person lying still in shade loses a fraction of that.\n\nThe water in the bottles was worth more than any task they could have done with it.',NULL),(1891,83,17,'What Sol did that counted','At about eleven, Sol got bored enough to be useful.',NULL),(1892,83,18,NULL,'She took the wing mirror off with the multi-tool and spent two hours learning to aim a reflection — holding up two fingers, lining the flash through the gap, the way Juno described from a course she had done twenty years before.','🪞'),(1893,83,19,NULL,'Then she laid out rocks on the pale ground in a V, each one a pace apart, the biggest she could carry in the heat.\n\nA V means require assistance. It is in every ground-to-air code. It took her an hour and she did it at four, when the sun was off the worst of it.',NULL),(1894,83,20,'Ten past five','The aircraft was a long way east and not looking for them.',NULL),(1895,83,21,NULL,'Sol got the mirror on it for perhaps four seconds, lost it, found it again, held it for nine.','✈️'),(1896,83,22,NULL,'The aircraft did not change course. Sol was certain she had failed and said so, loudly, for some time.',NULL),(1897,83,23,'The thing about four seconds','It had changed course. Aircraft turn slowly and a thirteen-year-old on the ground cannot see it happening.',NULL),(1898,83,24,NULL,'A mirror flash is visible for tens of miles — further than a flare, in daylight, and it does not run out.','🚁'),(1899,83,25,NULL,'They were picked up at eighteen minutes past six, twelve hours and fourteen minutes after the engine stopped.',NULL),(1900,83,26,'What Juno told her afterwards','\"You did three things right and they were all boring.\n\nYou stayed. You stayed in the shade. You made yourself bigger than you are — the rocks, the mirror. None of that is brave. All of it is why we are in this helicopter.\"',NULL),(1901,83,27,NULL,'The V is still out there. Nobody goes to collect rocks off a desert floor.','🪨'),(1902,83,28,NULL,'Sol is nineteen now and teaches the same course Juno did, and starts it the same way every time.\n\n\"The hardest thing I will ask you to do today is sit still.\"',NULL),(1903,83,29,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1904,84,1,'The mark on the door','There is a mark on the barn door at Pike\'s Orchard, and it is one metre and twenty centimetres from the ground.\n\nIf you are taller than the mark, you may go up in the balloon. If you are not, you may hold a rope.','🎈'),(1905,84,2,NULL,'Bea Pike had held a rope for four years.\n\nShe was extremely good at it. She could hold a rope better than any person in the family, which is the sort of thing you get good at when you are not allowed to do the other thing.',NULL),(1906,84,3,NULL,'On the first of September she stood against the barn door with her heels flat and her chin down, and her brother drew a line above her head in chalk.\n\nThe line was above the mark. Not by much. By about the thickness of a finger.',NULL),(1907,84,4,NULL,'\"That is because of your hair,\" said her brother.\n\n\"It is not because of my hair.\"\n\n\"Flatten your hair.\"\n\nBea flattened her hair. The line was still above the mark, by about the thickness of a thinner finger.','📏'),(1908,84,5,'Saturday, early','A balloon goes up at dawn or not at all. This is because the air is calm at dawn, and later in the day it starts having opinions.\n\nBea was awake at four. She was dressed at four. She was standing by the barn at ten past four, in the dark, being eaten by midges.',NULL),(1909,84,6,NULL,'Her grandmother came out at five with the kettle.\n\n\"You will be cold by seven,\" she said, which was not a no.',NULL),(1910,84,7,NULL,'The balloon came out of the trailer like a huge folded animal.\n\nIt took six people to spread it across the grass, and two to hold the mouth open, and one on the fan, and Bea on the crown line, because old habits are old habits.','🔥'),(1911,84,8,NULL,'Cold air goes in first. That took twenty minutes and looked like nothing at all happening, slowly.\n\nThen the burner. The burner is the part nobody is ready for the first time, or the fortieth.',NULL),(1912,84,9,'The sound of it','It is not a whoosh. A whoosh is what people say.\n\nIt is a roar, close to your face, that you feel in your teeth, and it goes on for eight or nine seconds, and the whole enormous thing on the grass lifts its head.','🔊'),(1913,84,10,NULL,'Bea had heard it four hundred times from the end of a rope.\n\nShe had never once heard it from inside the basket.',NULL),(1914,84,11,NULL,'\"Bea,\" said her grandmother. \"Door.\"\n\nBea went and stood against the barn door, in the half-light, with her heels flat and her chin down and her hair as flat as four years of waiting could press it.',NULL),(1915,84,12,NULL,'Her grandmother looked at the mark. She looked at the top of Bea\'s head. She looked at the mark again.\n\n\"Get in,\" she said.','✅'),(1916,84,13,'Up','Here is what nobody tells you about going up in a balloon.\n\nThe ground does not get smaller. The ground goes away. There is a difference and you feel it in your stomach, once, and then never again in your life.',NULL),(1917,84,14,NULL,'There is no wind. This is the strange part. You are moving with the air, so the air is perfectly still, and the only sound is the burner and some dogs.',NULL),(1918,84,15,NULL,'At two hundred feet Bea could see the whole orchard.\n\nAll eleven rows. The gaps where the old trees came out in the storm. The shape of the whole thing, which from the ground is just trees and from up there is a decision somebody made in 1931.','🌄'),(1919,84,16,NULL,'At four hundred feet she could see the chapel, and the quarry, and the road bending round it, and Thwaite, and a white speck in a field that her grandmother said was a fox and was almost certainly a bag.',NULL),(1920,84,17,'The jobs in the basket','Bea had expected to stand still and look.\n\nShe was given three jobs in the first four minutes, because a basket has no passengers.',NULL),(1921,84,18,NULL,'Job one: watch the pilot light, and say \"lit\" every time you look at it.\n\nJob two: hold the map and keep the chapel on the left edge of it.\n\nJob three: when Grandmother says \"ears\", open your mouth, because the burner is loud and the pressure changes and you will thank her.','🧭'),(1922,84,19,NULL,'\"That is three jobs,\" said Bea.\n\n\"It was four,\" said her grandmother, \"but your brother has the fourth and he is sulking.\"',NULL),(1923,84,20,'Down','A balloon lands where the air puts it. You can choose up and down. You cannot choose left.\n\nThe air put them in Harlow\'s bottom field, which is the good field, because it is flat and Mr Harlow is not unreasonable about gates.',NULL),(1924,84,21,NULL,'The landing is a bump and a tip and a long slow sideways scrape through wet grass, and you hold on to the handles and bend your knees and everybody shouts the same thing at the same time.\n\nBea shouted it too. She had been shouting it from the end of a rope for four years.','🌾'),(1925,84,22,'After','The chalk line is still on the barn door. Bea\'s grandmother painted over the old mark and put the new one at one metre twenty, which is exactly where it was.\n\nIt was not the mark that moved.',NULL),(1926,84,23,NULL,'Bea\'s cousin Ivo is four and holds the crown line now, and is extremely good at it, and stands against the barn door on the first of September with his heels flat and his chin down.','🧒'),(1927,84,24,NULL,'He is a long way off. Bea tells him so, honestly, because being lied to about it would have been worse.\n\nThen she tells him the other true thing, which is that the crown line is the hardest job on the field, and that she did it for four years, and that the balloon does not go up without it.',NULL),(1928,84,25,NULL,'The end.','🎈'),(1929,14,1,'A note for the giant','You are quite small.\n\nThat is a fact about the outside of you and it is the least interesting fact there is.','🪶'),(1930,14,2,'The Giant in the Bath','I am enormous in the bath.\nI make a tidal wave.\nThe flannel is a continent.\nThe plughole is a cave.\n\nThen somebody says \"out now\"\nand I am four again.',NULL),(1931,14,3,'Big Voice','My voice is bigger than my body\nwhich is useful in a field\nand a problem in a library\nand a disaster in a lift.',NULL),(1932,14,4,'Things I Can Lift','The cat (briefly).\nThe kettle (empty).\nMy brother (never again).\nMy own opinion (constantly).','📢'),(1933,14,5,'Shoes','My shoes are very small\nand go very far.\n\nLast year they went\nto the top of a hill\nand back\nwith a stone in one\nthe whole way.',NULL),(1934,14,6,'The Giant Does Not Want Soup','No.\n\nNo.\n\nI am a giant\nand giants eat\nbread\nand also\nmore bread.\n\nNo.',NULL),(1935,14,7,'Asking','A giant is allowed to ask for help.\nThis is in the rules.\nI have checked the rules.\nI wrote the rules.','🍲'),(1936,14,8,'Stairs','Thirteen stairs.\nI go up them like weather —\nslowly, then all at once,\nand everybody in the house\nknows exactly where I am.',NULL),(1937,14,9,'Small Giant, Big Feeling','It did not fit.\n\nIt was too big for my chest\nso it came out of my eyes,\nwhich is what eyes are partly for,\nwhatever anybody says.',NULL),(1938,14,10,'The Dog Is Also a Giant','He is eleven inches tall\nand he believes he is a lion.\n\nNobody has corrected him.\nIt would be rude\nand he would not listen.','💧'),(1939,14,11,'Pockets','In my pocket:\none conker,\nhalf a biscuit,\na stone shaped like Wales,\nand a feather I am keeping\nfor reasons I will explain later.',NULL),(1940,14,12,'Loud Table','Four people talking.\nI am the fifth.\nI say a thing.\nIt goes under the table\nwith the dog\nand stays there.\n\nI will say it again at bedtime\nwhen there is room.',NULL),(1941,14,13,'Growing','They measure me on the door frame\nin pencil, with a date.\n\nI am four lines up from the bottom\nand a great many lines\nfrom the top.','🪑'),(1942,14,14,'What Giants Are For','Reaching things? No. I cannot reach anything.\n\nOpening jars? Certainly not.\n\nNoticing? Yes.\nThat one. That is the job.',NULL),(1943,14,15,'The Giant Is Tired','Even a giant\nruns out.\n\nThen the giant lies on the floor\nin the hall\nfor eleven minutes\nand becomes a giant again\nwithout anybody doing anything.',NULL),(1944,14,16,'Night','The room goes big when the light goes off.\nI go small.\n\nThen the door opens an inch\nand the landing comes in\nand I am approximately enormous once more.','😴'),(1945,14,17,'Three Things in My Hand','A conker.\nA warm hand that is not mine.\nAnd a feather,\nwhich I am still keeping,\nfor reasons.',NULL),(1946,14,18,'Last One','You are quite small.\n\nYou are also\nthe loudest thing in this house,\nthe only one who noticed the beetle,\nand the person the dog comes to.\n\nWork out which of those\nis the fact about you.',NULL),(1947,14,19,'For the grown-up reading this','They know they are small. Nobody needs telling.\n\nTell them the other things. They are harder to notice and they last longer.','🪶'),(1948,14,20,NULL,'The end.',NULL),(1949,85,1,'Three thousand metres','A blue whale weighs about a hundred and forty tonnes.\n\nWhen one dies, the gases of decomposition may float it for a while. Eventually it sinks, and it keeps sinking, often for two or three thousand metres.','🐋'),(1950,85,2,NULL,'It lands on a plain of mud where nothing grows, because nothing can grow: there is no light, so there are no plants, and almost everything that lives down there lives on scraps falling from above.',NULL),(1951,85,3,NULL,'Those scraps are called marine snow, and on a normal day they are a dusting.\n\nA whale is not a dusting. A whale is a hundred and forty tonnes of food arriving at once in a place that measures its meals in flakes.','❄️'),(1952,85,4,'The first stage','Within hours, the scavengers arrive. The first and most numerous are hagfish and sleeper sharks.',NULL),(1953,85,5,NULL,'Hagfish are eel-shaped, have no jaws, and tie themselves in knots to tear off mouthfuls. They can absorb nutrients through their skin.',NULL),(1954,85,6,NULL,'This stage is called the mobile scavenger stage and it removes the soft tissue — between forty and sixty per cent of the whale\'s mass.','🦈'),(1955,85,7,NULL,'It takes months to about two years depending on the size of the animal, and it is the only stage that looks anything like what you would expect.',NULL),(1956,85,8,'The second stage','Once the big flesh is gone, the enrichment opportunist stage begins, and the cast changes completely.',NULL),(1957,85,9,NULL,'The sediment around the skeleton is now saturated with organic material. Worms, crustaceans and molluscs colonise it in enormous densities — tens of thousands of animals per square metre.','🪱'),(1958,85,10,NULL,'This lasts around two years and the animals doing it are mostly species that specialise in exactly this: a sudden rich patch of mud, anywhere, briefly.',NULL),(1959,85,11,'The third stage, and the strangest','Then the bones.\n\nA whale skeleton is roughly sixty per cent lipid — fats and oils, held inside the bone.',NULL),(1960,85,12,NULL,'Bacteria break those down, and in doing so they produce hydrogen sulphide, which is poisonous to most life and is food for some.','🦴'),(1961,85,13,NULL,'This is the sulphophilic stage, and it supports a community closely related to the one found at hydrothermal vents: mussels, clams, tube worms, all living on chemical energy rather than sunlight.',NULL),(1962,85,14,NULL,'It can last fifty years. On a large whale, possibly longer.','⏳'),(1963,85,15,'The bone-eating worms','In 2002 researchers found something nobody had predicted: a worm with no mouth, no gut and no anus, growing out of whale bone like a small red bush.',NULL),(1964,85,16,NULL,'It is called Osedax, which means bone-devourer. It drills into the bone with roots and relies on bacteria inside it to digest the fats.',NULL),(1965,85,17,NULL,'More than thirty species have been described since. They are found wherever whale bone reaches the sea floor, and they were entirely unknown twenty-five years ago.','🌱'),(1966,85,18,'Stepping stones','Here is why this matters beyond being extraordinary.',NULL),(1967,85,19,NULL,'Vent communities live at hydrothermal vents, which are scattered, temporary, and thousands of kilometres apart.',NULL),(1968,85,20,NULL,'How does a clam that lives only on chemical energy cross an ocean of ordinary cold mud to reach the next vent?','🌋'),(1969,85,21,NULL,'One leading answer is whale falls. A chain of sulphide-rich islands, each lasting decades, scattered across every ocean, continually replaced.',NULL),(1970,85,22,'How many','Estimates suggest that at any moment there may be hundreds of thousands of whale falls on the world\'s sea floors in some stage of decay.',NULL),(1971,85,23,NULL,'Commercial whaling in the twentieth century removed around three million whales.\n\nThat is not only three million animals. It is a very large reduction in the number of these islands, over a century, and nobody knows what that did to the things that were island-hopping.','🌊'),(1972,85,24,'The last thing','A whale fall is not a death followed by nothing. It is the largest single delivery of food that most of the deep ocean will ever see.',NULL),(1973,85,25,NULL,'Fifty years of a city, from one animal, three thousand metres down, in the dark, where nobody was looking until 1987.',NULL),(1974,85,26,NULL,'The end.','🐋'),(1975,7,1,'The hydrophone','A hydrophone is a microphone you put in the sea. Tam and Ola made one out of a kit from the internet and it cost eleven pounds.','🐋'),(1976,7,2,NULL,'They lowered it off the end of the jetty at Cold Nose on a Saturday and heard: a boat, some clicking, and a sound like a door in an empty house.',NULL),(1977,7,3,NULL,'The door was a whale. They did not know that for three weeks.','🎧'),(1978,7,4,'Learning to listen','Whale song is not one noise. It has units, which repeat into phrases, which repeat into themes, which repeat into a song.',NULL),(1979,7,5,NULL,'A humpback song can go on for twenty minutes and then start again almost exactly the same.',NULL),(1980,7,6,NULL,'Tam wrote the units down as shapes: a long swoop, two clicks, a low moan. Ola wrote them as letters. Both systems worked and neither of them could read the other\'s.','🎵'),(1981,7,7,'Speaking it badly','Then they did the thing that got them into trouble and also made the whole story.',NULL),(1982,7,8,NULL,'They played it back.',NULL),(1983,7,9,NULL,'Not the real song — a version of it, made on a laptop, with the swoops too fast and the moans the wrong pitch. It sounded, Ola admitted later, like somebody imitating a cow.','🔊'),(1984,7,10,'What came','Nothing came, for four weekends.',NULL),(1985,7,11,NULL,'On the fifth, something answered.',NULL),(1986,7,12,NULL,'Not the same song. A short run of units, repeated three times, that was not in any of their recordings.','〰️'),(1987,7,13,'The scientist','They sent it to a marine biologist, whose address Ola found on a university page, with no real expectation of a reply.',NULL),(1988,7,14,NULL,'Her name was Dr Juno Haugen. She replied in nine days, which in research terms is immediate.',NULL),(1989,7,15,NULL,'She said three things, in order, and the order mattered.','📧'),(1990,7,16,'What she said','One: this is a real recording of a real humpback and it is good data, thank you.',NULL),(1991,7,17,NULL,'Two: stop playing sound into the water immediately.',NULL),(1992,7,18,NULL,'Playing sound at whales can pull them towards boats, interrupt feeding, and interfere with how they find each other. In many places it is illegal.','🛑'),(1993,7,19,NULL,'Three: the thing that answered was almost certainly not answering. It was a whale singing its own song nearby, and the timing was coincidence.',NULL),(1994,7,20,'Being wrong about the best bit','That was hard. The answering was the part they had told everybody about.',NULL),(1995,7,21,NULL,'Ola wanted to argue. Tam, who is quieter, read the letter again and said: \"She is right, and we have still got the recording.\"','😶'),(1996,7,22,'What they have now','Forty-one hours of hydrophone recordings from one jetty across two years, with dates, times, sea state and tide.',NULL),(1997,7,23,NULL,'Eleven of those hours have whales in them. Dr Haugen uses four of them in a project on seasonal presence, and Tam and Ola are named in it.',NULL),(1998,7,24,NULL,'They have never played a sound into the water again.','📄'),(1999,7,25,'The friend','And they have a scientist who writes back, which is the friend in the title, and which neither of them expected to be the answer.',NULL),(2000,7,26,NULL,'She told them, in letter nine, the thing they repeat most.\n\n\"Listening is data. Talking is interference. Learn the difference early and you will be useful for fifty years.\"',NULL),(2001,7,27,NULL,'The end.','🐋'),(2002,86,1,'Forty cups','Class 3 planted forty beans in forty yoghurt cups on the window sill.\n\nOne cup each, one bean each, one name each written on the side in marker pen that came off if you touched it.','🌱'),(2003,86,2,NULL,'Mr Enright said the beans would take about ten days.\n\nHe said some would do nothing at all, and that this was not a failure, because finding out that something does nothing is also a result.',NULL),(2004,86,3,NULL,'Nobody believed him. Everybody wanted theirs to be first.',NULL),(2005,86,4,NULL,'Sione\'s cup was at the sunny end.\n\nIt said SIONE in marker pen, and under that, because he had been thinking about it, it said BEAN 40.','🏷️'),(2006,86,5,'Day six','On day six, twenty-two cups had a green loop poking out of the soil, like somebody pulling a thread.\n\nBy day eight it was thirty-four. By day ten it was thirty-nine.',NULL),(2007,86,6,NULL,'Bean 40 had nothing. Not a loop, not a bump, not a crack in the soil.\n\nSione dug a very small hole with a lolly stick, which you are not supposed to do, and had a look.',NULL),(2008,86,7,NULL,'The bean had split. There was a white root coming out of it, thick and healthy and perfectly alive.\n\nIt was going sideways.','🔎'),(2009,86,8,NULL,'Not down. Not up. Sideways, around the inside of the cup, like something looking for a door.',NULL),(2010,86,9,'What Sione asked','\"Why is mine going round the edge?\"\n\nMr Enright came and looked, and then did the thing that makes him a good teacher, which is that he said: \"I do not know. What do you think?\"',NULL),(2011,86,10,NULL,'\"I think it is broken.\"\n\n\"Possibly. What else could it be?\"\n\nSione looked at the thirty-nine cups that had behaved themselves and the one that had not.\n\n\"Something is different about my cup.\"',NULL),(2012,86,11,NULL,'That is a scientific sentence and it got a marble.\n\nThree hundred and forty-three.','💡'),(2013,86,12,'The things that were different','Class 3 made a list of every way Sione\'s cup was different from the other thirty-nine.\n\nThis took most of a wet Wednesday and the list had nine things on it.',NULL),(2014,86,13,NULL,'It was at the sunny end.\nIt was nearest the radiator.\nIt had been knocked over once by Daniel, who owned up.\nIt had been watered twice on the same day by two different people.\nThe soil was from the bottom of the bag.',NULL),(2015,86,14,NULL,'Then they did the part that takes nerve, which is crossing things off.\n\nThe sunny end: crossed off, because the cup next to it was also at the sunny end and was fine.\n\nWatered twice: crossed off, same reason. Half the window sill had been watered twice.','📋'),(2016,86,15,NULL,'That left the radiator, the knock, and the soil from the bottom of the bag.',NULL),(2017,86,16,'Testing it','You cannot un-knock a bean. You cannot un-pour the soil.\n\nBut you can plant four more cups and knock one over on purpose, and that is what Class 3 did, which Mr Enright described as the best afternoon of his career.',NULL),(2018,86,17,NULL,'Cup A: normal, middle of the sill. (The control. Sione named it THE BORING ONE.)\n\nCup B: knocked over on day three, deliberately, by Daniel, who was delighted.\n\nCup C: jammed against the radiator.\n\nCup D: soil from the bottom of the bag.','🧪'),(2019,86,18,NULL,'They waited eleven days. Waiting is the part of science nobody puts in the films.',NULL),(2020,86,19,'The result','Cup A was fine. Cup B was fine — the root had turned and gone back down within a day, which Class 3 found almost disappointing and which is actually the whole point.\n\nCup D was fine, although slower.',NULL),(2021,86,20,NULL,'Cup C\'s root went sideways. Away from the radiator. Exactly like Bean 40.','♨️'),(2022,86,21,NULL,'A root grows down because of gravity. That is called gravitropism, and Class 3 can all say it, some of them correctly.\n\nBut a root will also grow away from heat and towards water, and when those pull in different directions the root picks its way between them.',NULL),(2023,86,22,NULL,'Bean 40 was not broken. Bean 40 was doing three things at once in a plastic cup two centimetres from a radiator, and the answer it came up with was: go round the side.',NULL),(2024,86,23,'What happened to Bean 40','Sione moved it to the cold end of the sill in week three.\n\nThe root turned down within two days, and a shoot came up four days after that, eighteen days later than everybody else\'s.','🌿'),(2025,86,24,NULL,'It is the biggest plant in the class now.\n\nNobody is quite sure why and Class 3 have agreed, formally, by vote, that this is allowed to be an open question.',NULL),(2026,86,25,'After','There is a display in the corridor outside Class 3. It has photographs, and a graph with eleven days on it, and the list of nine differences with six of them crossed out.',NULL),(2027,86,26,NULL,'At the top, in Sione\'s handwriting, it says: BEAN 40 WAS NOT BROKEN.\n\nAt the bottom it says: WE THOUGHT IT WAS BROKEN FOR TWELVE DAYS. THEN WE TESTED IT.','📌'),(2028,86,27,NULL,'And beside the display, on the window sill, where it gets no sun at all and is a very long way from the radiator, there is a yoghurt cup with a plant in it that is taller than the window catch.\n\nThe marker pen has nearly come off. You can still read the 40.',NULL),(2029,86,28,NULL,'The end.','🌳');
/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `content_pages` ENABLE KEYS */;
